r \nconstruction of toilets and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether this amount is considered adequate to construct a decent toilet; \n \n(c) if not, whether the Government proposes to increase the said a mount to facilitate proper \nconstruction of toilet with provision of water in rural areas of the country; and \n \n(d) if so, the steps taken in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n \n(a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No.  *173 for 28.07.2016. \n \n(a) Under Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin), there is a provision f or providing an incentive of Rs. \n12,000 for the construction of Individual household latrine (IHHL) to all Below poverty Line (BPL) \nhouseholds and to identified Above Poverty Line (APL) households (all SCs /STs, small and \nmarginal farmers, landless labourers with homestead, physical ly handicapped and women-headed \nhouseholds). \n \n(b) Yes. The incentive to eligible beneficiaries for constr uction of toilet was increased from Rs. \n10000 to Rs. 12000, to provide for water availability, including for storing w ater for hand-washing \nand cleaning with effect from 2 nd  October, 2014 with the launch of the Swachh Bharat Mission-\nGramin (SBM-G). This programme emphasizes behavior change a nd is not a supply-driven \nconstruction led programme. Therefore, Rs. 12,000 per toilet is an in centive amount and not a \nreimbursement of the cost of the toilet, which may be lower or higher than this incentive amount, \ndepending upon several factors.  \n \n(c) Does not arise. \n \n(d) Does not arise. \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|STARRED   |174", "t": "Tribunals", "head": "   \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.174 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2016 \n \n \n“ILLEGAL MINING”  \n \n \n174. SHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA AND SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \n \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the illegal mining activities across the \ncountry, if so, the details thereof indicatin g number of reported cases of illegal \nmining during each of the last three years, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b) the estimated revenue loss incurred to  the Government therefrom and the \nnumber of officials reportedly killed/died while checking the illegal mining \nduring the said period, State/UT-wise; \n \n(c) the action taken by the Gove rnment against the persons involved in the illegal \nmining and the extent of success achieved in this regard; \n \n(d) the other corrective steps being taken/proposed to be taken by the Government \nto check illegal mining and for the safe ty of the Government employees in the \ncountry; and \n \n(e) whether the Government is considering to adopt satellite technique to put a \ncheck on illegal sand-mining, if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, COAL, \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) State Governments are empowered, un der Section 23C of the Mines and \nMineral (Development and Re gulation) Act, 1957 ( MMDR Act, 1957) to make rules \nfor prevention of illegal mining, transportation and storage of minerals; and, therefore, \nmatters relating to illega l mining come under the legislative and administrative \njurisdiction of State Governments. \n \n As per quarterly returns submitted by the State Governments on illegal mining \nto India Bureau of Mines(IBM) (a subordinate office under the Ministry of Mines), the \ncases of illegal mining for major and minor minerals reported in the country for the \nyears 2013-14 to 2015-16 (Quarter ending December 2015) are at Annexure-I to \nAnnexure-III. \n \n(b) As per quarterly returns submitted by the State Governments on illegal mining \nto IBM, the value of mineral reported agains t the number of cases detected for illegal \nmining are at Annexure-I to Annexure-III. \n \n \n\n                          \n    \n(c) As per Section 23C of Mines and Mine rals (Development and Regulation) Act \n1957, State governments are empowered to fra me rules for curbing illegal mining and \nto take action in the matter. However, as stated in reply to (a) & (b) above, based on \nthe quarterly returns received from the St ate Governments., total numbers of FIR \nlodged, Court Cases filed and Fine realized for the years 2013-14 to 2015-16 (Quarter \nending December 2015) is provided below: \n \n \nYear \n \nMajor minerals Minor minerals Total \n(Major + Minor) minerals \n FIR \nlodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \ncases \nfiled \n(No) \nFine \nrealized \n(Rs. Lacs) \nFIR \nlodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \ncases \nfiled \n(Nos.) \nFine \nrealized (Rs. \nLacs) \nFIR \nlodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \ncases \nfiled \n(Nos.) \nFine \nrealized (Rs. \nLacs) \n2013-14 518 258 798.26 3331 10666 23107.93 3849 10924 23906.19 \n2014-15 404 227 1563.59 3835 9057 29032.24 4239 9284 30595.83 \n2015-16 \n(Quarter \nending \nDecember \n2015) \n711 282 572.41 4079 13945 27599.53 4790 14227 28171.94 \n \n \n \n(d)  The MMDR Act, 1957 was amended through the MMDR Amendment Act, \n2015 which came into effect from 12 th January, 2015. The Amen dment Act has, inter \nalia, provided for stringent punitive provis ions for combating illegal mining. Illegal \nmining has been made punishable with im prisonment for a term which may extend to \nfive years and with fine which may extend to five lakh rupees per hectare of the area. \nProvisions have been made for setting up of Special Courts for the purpose of \nproviding speedy trial of offences relating to illegal mining.  \n \n \n \n(e) Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM), Nagpur has entered into a MoU with National \nRemote Sensing Centre (NRSC), Hyderabad on 21.01.2016 for a pilot project “Sudoor \nDrushti” to demonstrate the feasibility of using High Resolution Satellite imagery and \nDigital Elevation Model (DEM ) in monitoring mining activities / changes over a \nperiod of time over selected group of mines.   \n \nThe Ministry of Mines has also engaged with Bhaskaracharya Institute of \nSpace Applications & Geo-Informatics (BISAG), Gujarat to develop a ‘Mine \nSurveillance System’ (MSS) for major minerals in consultation with Indian Bureau of \nMines to enhance the mechanis m of detecting illegal mining  with the use of satellite \nbased technology.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n                          \n    \nANNEXURE –I\n  RETURN ON ILLEGAL MINING FOR THE \nYEAR 2013-14 \n Major Minerals \nSR. STATE No. of  \nQuantum \nof \nmineral/ Value of  FIR Court \nFine \nrealised \n(Rs. Lakh) \nNo.  cases \nOre \nexcavate\nd /  Mineral / \nLodg\ned Cases \n    \nstacked / \nTransport\ned Ore  \n(Nos.\n) Filed \n    \n(in lakh \ntonnes) (Rs.Lacks)   (Nos.) \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 486 0.064 36.380 2 0 165.70\n2 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|STARRED   |175", "t": "Transportation through Waterways", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n                MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n \n            LOK SABHA  \n                STARRED QUESTION NO.175  \n             TO BE ANSWERED ON 28th  JULY, 2016 \nTRANSPORTATION THROUGH WATERWAYS \n*175.  SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL:  \n SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE:  \n \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state: \nYxSYå_dhW]2ým�\n(a)  the details of waterways being used in the country along with length thereof and \nthe number of ships/vessels being run thereon along with the quantum/type of \ncargo being transported, State/UT-wise;  \n(b)  the details of funds allocated/released by the Government for their development \nduring the last three years and the current year, State/UT-wise;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to increase the target of transportation \nthrough waterways in the country from existing 6 per cent to 15 per cent and if \n