uring the years 2013-14 2016-17 \n \n(RS. in Crore) \n \nक्रसं. राज्य \nAllocation under SDRF Centre’s share of SDRF released  Released from NDRF \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 2013-14 2014-15  2015-16  2016-17 \n( 05.07.16 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |432", "t": "National Livestock Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMNAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING & FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.439 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nGUIDELINES ON DEEP SEA FISHING \n \n439. SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  कृषि और कृिक कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the deep sea fishing guidelines mandate boats, that are above 15 meters overall length, \nengaged in near shore fishing just beyond the territorial wat ers of India, to obtain a letter of \npermission from the Government of India and voyage clearance from the Indian Coast Guard \nfor each fishing voyage; \n(b) if so the details thereof; \n(c) whether the new guidelines not only restrict the open access of the country’s f ishermen in the \nExclusive Economic Zone but also set a dangerous precedent of allowing foreign vessels of \nmore than 15 meters overall length to compete with our fishermen for resources which need  to \nbe conserved for utilization by the country’s fishermen;  \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the Government to protect the interest of the country’s fishermen; and \n(e) whether the Government has received a representation from the State Government of Tamil \nNadu in this regard, if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE: \n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes, Madam. As per the Guidelines for operation of Deep Sea F ishing Vessels in the \nIndian Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) issued on 12th November, 2014 a Letter of Permission \n(LOP) is required from the Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and Fisheries, Ministry \nof Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, Government of India for operating any fishing vessel(s) of \n15 meter overall length (OAL) and above or any resource specific vessel or any vessel fit for \ndeep sea fishing for their operation in Indian EE Z beyond 12 nautical miles . The LOP holder \nDSFVs are required to comply all the conditions stipulated under the said Guidelines dated 12 th \nNovember, 2014 which includes inspection of vessel(s) by nearest Coast Guard Authority \nbefore and after completion of each voyage. \n(c) No, Madam.  Any Indian citizen, Indian entrepreneur; Partnership Firm; Private Ltd. Company; \nPublic Ltd. Company; Joint Venture Company (with at least 51% Indian equity), Corporation \nand registered Cooperative Society may apply for obtaining LOP for deep -sea fishing in the \nIndian EEZ beyond 12 nautical miles. Whereas , foreign vessels are not allowed to fish within \nthe Exclusive Economic Zone of India. \n(d) Does not arise. \n(e) The State Government of Tamil Nadu vide its letter dated 03.5.2015 had raised  its concerns on \nthe Guidelines dated12th November, 2014. A Committee has been constituted on 28.7.2015 to \nsuggest draft of the National Policy on Marine Fisheries and also to review the Guidelines for \ndeep-sea fishing. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |433", "t": "Production of Arhar and Urad", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n433\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n             \nPRODUCTION OF \nARHAR AND URAD\n \n      \n \n433. \n \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nSHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe quantum of Arhar and Urad\n \npulses produced in the country during each of the \nlast three years and the current year along with the quantum of such pulses procured by \nthe Union Government and State agencies during the said period, State/ UT\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the production of such\n \nvarieties of pulses is likely to decline during the \ncurrent year and the steps that have been taken by the Government to import pulses to \nmeet their shortage in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Indian Institute of Pulses Rese\narch (IIPR) is working towards bio\n-\nintensification of pulse based cropping system, intensification of breeding programme \nand resource conservation, mechanization and minimizing post harvest yield loss and if \nso, the details thereof;\n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Gover\nnment is working upon climate risk management and efficient \nextension models for dissemination of pulse based technologies for farmers to make the \npulse cultivation more productive and remunerative in the country and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe details of steps taken/being taken by the Government to make the country \nself\n-\nsufficient in pulses?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n(\n \nSHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \nState\n-\nwise details of production of Arhar and Urad in the country during 2011\n-\n12 to \n20\n15\n-\n16 are at \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \n \n\nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \nUnder the Price Support Scheme (PSS), Government agencies undertake \nprocurement as and when prices fall below the Minimum Support Prices (MSPs).   As \nthe prices of Arhar (Tur) and Urad during 2014\n-\n15 onwards have remained above MSP, \nthere was no requiremen\nt to procure the pulses under the PSS. Details of procurement \nof Arhar and Urad during 2013\n-\n14 are as under:\n-\n \n(‘000 Tonnes)\n \nUrad\n \nArhar (Tur)\n \nState\n \nQuantity \nProcured\n \nState\n \nQuantity \nProcured\n \nMaharashtra\n \n2.71\n \nMaharashtra\n \n22.09\n \nUttar \nPradesh\n \n1.95\n \nAndhra \nPradesh\n \n21.68\n \n \nUnder the Price Stabilization Fund (PSF), Government of India has designated \nFood Corporation of India (FCI) along\n-\nwith National Agricultural Cooperative Marketing \nFederation of India Ltd. (NAFED) and Small Farmers’ Agribusiness Consortium (SFAC) \nfor procurement of pulses for creation of buffer stock of 50000 tonnes of pulses from \nkharif and 100000 tonnes of pulses fro\nm rabi crops of 2015\n-\n16 at market price. \n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of procurement of Arhar and Urad by above agencies during \n2015\n-\n16 under PSF are at \nAnnexure\n-\nII\n.\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nAs the sowing window of kharif crops including pulses is still open, it is too early \nto asse\nss their production during the current year i.e. 2016\n-\n17. However, the annual \nproduction of pulses in the country has been falling short of their requirement and \nshortage is met through imports. In order to stabilize prices of pulses, in December \n2015, the\n \nGovernment approved creation of buffer stock of 1.50 lakh tonnes of pulses \nthrough domestic procurement and imports.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nThe Indian Institute of Pulses Research (IIPR) under ICAR is working towards \nbio\n-\nintensification of pulse based cropping systems, in\ntensification of breeding \nprogramme and resource conservation, mechanization and minimizing post harvest \nyield loss. The IIPR is engaged in a number of research programmes on development \nof micro\n-\nirrigation, resource conservation techniques, pulse based re\nmunerative \ncropping systems, mechanization in pulses cultivation and reduction in post harvest \nlosses etc.   \n \n  \n \n(d)\n:\n \nIn order to address the issue of climate risk management in agriculture including \npulses (arhar, moong and urad), the ICAR is implementing\n \nan ambitious project on \n‘National Initiative on Climate Resilient Agriculture (NICRA)’ since February 2011. This \nmega project has three major objectives of strategic research, technology \ndemonstrations and capacity building. The four modules of NICRA viz.\n \nnatural resource \nmanagement, improving soil health, crop production and livestock are aimed at making \nthe farmers self\n-\nreliant.\n \n \n\n \nContd…3/\n-\n \n-\n3\n-\n \n \n \n(e)\n:\n \nIn order to increase production of pulses in the country, the Government of India \nhas been implementing through State Governments, the National Food Security Mission \n(NFSM)\n-\nPulses since 2007\n-\n08.  From 2016\n-\n17, NFSM\n-"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |435", "t": "e-Commerce Complaints", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.435 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nE-COMMERCE COMPLAINTS \n \n435. SHRIMATI RAKSHATAI KHADSE:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up a panel/committee to deal with the \ncomplaints of the consumers against companies trading on e -commerce platforms and if \nso, the details thereof indicating the composition of the said committee; and  \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to frame rules and regulations for consumer \nprotection on e-trading platforms and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) & (b) : No Madam. There is no such proposal, at present.  \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |437", "t": "Alleged Migration of Hindus", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 437 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nALLEGED MIGRATION OF HINDUS \n \n437.  SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether migration of Hindus due to  fear factor in Uttar Pradesh has \nbeen reported; \n \n(b) if so, the number of persons who have migrated and the details of the \nefforts made by the Government for their rehabilitation; \n \n(c) the action  taken by the Government  to check the same and punish \nthe persons responsible for this incident; \n \n(d) whether the Government has taken  special measures to protect the \nimmovable properties of the Hindus who have migrated; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof  alongwith the details of advisories issued by \nthe Union Government to Uttar Pradesh in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to (e):  There were recent media reports about migration of \nHindus in Uttar Pradesh. A report in this regard has been received \nvery recently from the Government of Uttar Pradesh that would \nneed  examination. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |438", "t": "Afforestation in Hilly Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.438 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nAfforestation in Hilly Areas \n \n \n438.     SHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has allocated/sanctioned funds for afforestation of hilly \nforest areas in the country;\n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during each of the last three years, State/UT -wise including \nUttar Pradesh;  \n(c)  the total area proposed to be brought under forest cover under the said project,              \nState-wise; \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal  for setting up of Wind Mills under the said \nschemes; and  \n(e) the time by which the work of afforestation and setting up of Wind Mills is likely to \nbe completed? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) & (b)   The Ministry of Environment, Forests and Climate Change (MoEF & CC) is \nimplementing a centrally  sponsored “National Afforestation Programme (NAP)” scheme for \nregeneration and restocking of degraded forests in the country.  The scheme is implemented in \nparticipatory mode under the Joint Forest Management  by State Forest Development Agency \n(SFDA) at the State level, Forest Development Agency (FDA) at the forest division/district level \nand Joint Forest Management Committee (JFMC) at the village level. The Scheme is \nimplemented in 800 FDAs including 248 FDAs falling in hilly forest areas of the country.   \nUnder NAP, the grants are released to SFDAs and implementation is monitored at the state level. \nThe details of funds released to the States including Uttar Pradesh under NAP during the last \nthree years is given in Annexure.  \n \n \n(c)  National Forest policy  aims 33% forest cover in the country. The area f or affortestation \nunder NAP is san ctioned to States on receipt of Annual Plan of Operation (APO) and by \nconsidering their proposed demand, past performance and the Budget availability.  Afforestation \nunder NAP has contributed to the forest cover  of the country  as an area of about 21 lakhs \nhectares has been sanctioned for new plantations under NAP since its inception in 2000- 02 till \n2015-16 by releasing Rs. 3639.28 crores.   \n    \n(d)  No Sir.  There is no proposal under the NAP Scheme for setting up of Wind Mills. \n \n(e) NAP is an open ending scheme where the work of  plantation and maintenance activities \nare continuously going on. As fa r as completion of setting up of Wind Mills  under NAP is \nconcerned, the question does not arise.  \n \n******** \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of parts (a &  b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.438 on “Afforestation in Hilly Areas ” asked by Shri Raghav Lakhanpal for reply on \n19.07.2016. \n \nYearwise and Statewise Details of funds released under National Afforestation \nProgramme(NAP) for the last three years \n \nRs. crores \nS. No. State 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1 Andhra Pradesh  3.75 4.56 0.87 \n2 Bihar 12.84 7.00 5.01 \n3 Chhattisgarh 21.38 20.00 10.20 \n4 Gujarat  11.68 10.50 0.00 \n5 Haryana  17.94 11.00 8.54 \n6 Himachal Pradesh  2.61 0.73 0.72 \n7 Jammu & Kashmir  8.11 3.59 0.00 \n8 Jharkhand 9.02 8.60 0.00 \n9 Karnataka  9.26 21.35 0.00 \n10 Kerala 6.99 2.82 1.05 \n11 Madhya Pradesh  22.10 21.00 1.02 \n12 Maharashtra  32.33 35.00 9.45 \n13 Orissa  5.36 17.92 14.90 \n14 Punjab  2.00 1.87 9.49 \n15 Rajasthan  2.81 3.35 0.00 \n16 Tamil Nadu  3.21 4.25 0.75 \n17 Telangana 0.00 2.03 1.42 \n18 Uttar Pradesh  20.15 12.00 0.00 \n19 Uttarakhand  6.01 2.50 2.68 \n20 West Bengal 2.96 0.78 3.05 \n  Total (Other \nStates) \n200.49 190.85 69.15 \n  North Eastern \nstates \n     \n21 Arunachal Pradesh  0.00 0.15 0.00 \n22 Assam 2.99  - 2.56 \n23 Manipur  12.12 8.00 3.67 \n24 Meghalaya 4.50 3.61 1.35 \n25 Mizoram  11.94 15.00 10.17 \n26 Nagaland  9.82 11.00 0.00 \n27 Sikkim  3.77 6.00 1.52 \n28 Tripura 11.99 9.17 5.73 \n  Total (NE States) 57.13 52.93 25.00 \n  G. Total 257.62 243.78 94.16 \n     \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |439", "t": "Guidelines on Deep Sea Fishing", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMNAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING & FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.439 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nGUIDELINES ON DEEP SEA FISHING \n \n439. SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  कृषि और कृिक कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the deep sea fishing guidelines mandate boats, that are above 15 meters overall length, \nengaged in near shore fishing just beyond the territorial wat ers of India, to obtain a letter of \npermission from the Government of India and voyage clearance from the Indian Coast Guard \nfor each fishing voyage; \n(b) if so the details thereof; \n(c) whether the new guidelines not only restrict the open access of the country’s f ishermen in the \nExclusive Economic Zone but also set a dangerous precedent of allowing foreign vessels of \nmore than 15 meters overall length to compete with our fishermen for resources which need  to \nbe conserved for utilization by the country’s fishermen;  \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the Government to protect the interest of the country’s fishermen; and \n(e) whether the Government has received a representation from the State Government of Tamil \nNadu in this regard, if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE: \n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes, Madam. As per the Guidelines for operation of Deep Sea F ishing Vessels in the \nIndian Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) issued on 12th November, 2014 a Letter of Permission \n(LOP) is required from the Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and Fisheries, Ministry \nof Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, Government of India for operating any fishing vessel(s) of \n15 meter overall length (OAL) and above or any resource specific vessel or any vessel fit for \ndeep sea fishing for their operation in Indian EE Z beyond 12 nautical miles . The LOP holder \nDSFVs are required to comply all the conditions stipulated under the said Guidelines dated 12 th \nNovember, 2014 which includes inspection of vessel(s) by nearest Coast Guard Authority \nbefore and after completion of each voyage. \n(c) No, Madam.  Any Indian citizen, Indian entrepreneur; Partnership Firm; Private Ltd. Company; \nPublic Ltd. Company; Joint Venture Company (with at least 51% Indian equity), Corporation \nand registered Cooperative Society may apply for obtaining LOP for deep -sea fishing in the \nIndian EEZ beyond 12 nautical miles. Whereas , foreign vessels are not allowed to fish within \nthe Exclusive Economic Zone of India. \n(d) Does not arise. \n(e) The State Government of Tamil Nadu vide its letter dated 03.5.2015 had raised  its concerns on \nthe Guidelines dated12th November, 2014. A Committee has been constituted on 28.7.2015 to \nsuggest draft of the National Policy on Marine Fisheries and also to review the Guidelines for \ndeep-sea fishing. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |44", "t": "Higher Educational Institutions", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 44 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT \n \nQUESTION \n \n*44. SHRI BHARATHI MOHAN R.K.: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken major skill d evelopment initiatives including an \nintegrated system of skilled manpower under one roof and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to make availa ble skilled manpower with proper \ncourse certification in those fields where there is shortage of professionals such as carpenters \nand plumbers, etc. and if so, the trades/professions identified for the purpose; and  \n \n(c)  the follow-up action taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY) \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the H ouse. \n  \n\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT TO BE LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE HOUSE IN R ESPECT OF PART (a) \nTO (c) OFLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 44 ADMITTED  FOR REPLY ON \n20.07.2016 REGARDING \"SKILL DEVELOPMENT ” \n \n \n(a) to (c) The Government of India for the first ti me created an independent Ministry of Skill \nDevelopment and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) during 2014-15 so as to harmonise and integrate \nhighly fragmented skilling efforts / programmes in the country.  The MSDE has taken a \nnumber of initiatives for integration of skilling echo system by launching the National Policy on \nSkill Development and Entrepreneurship 2015, Nation al Skill Development Mission under the \nChairmanship of the Hon’ble Prime Minister and Comm on Norms for Skill Development \nSchemes.  The Vision of the Policy is “to create an  ecosystem of empowerment by Skilling on \na large Scale at Speed with high Standards and to p romote a culture of innovation based \nentrepreneurship which can generate wealth and empl oyment so as to ensure Sustainable \nlivelihoods for all citizens in the country”. Simil arly, Common Norms define the activities \nconstituting ‘Skill Development’ in the country, sk ill development courses and their alignment \nwith the National Skills Qualification Framework, b road input standards for training \nprogrammes and the outcomes expected from these pro grammes. Further, The National Skill \nDevelopment Mission is expected to provide a strong  institutional framework at the Centre \nand States for implementation of skilling activities in the country. The Government through the \nNational Skill Development Corporation (NSDC) has a lso established Sector Skill Councils \n(SSCs) so as to ensure that skill development effor ts being made by all stakeholders in the \nsystem are in accordance with actual needs of indus try. SSCs are being set up as industry-\nled and industry-governed bodies, which will help l ink the requirements of Industry with \nappropriately trained manpower. Role and functions of the SSCs include finalisation of \nNational Occupational Standards (NOSs) and Qualific ation Packs (QPs) for job roles as per \nindustry requirement, its alignment with NSQF, asse ssment and certification etc. Currently \nthere are 40 Sector Skill Councils including Plumbing and Constructions.   \n \n The SSCs have developed QPs for more than 1600 job  roles in more than 30 sectors \nincluding for plumbing, carpenters, electrician etc . The training is being imparted under many \nof these 1600 QPs under various skill development p rogrammes, based on skill requirement \nin a region as per skill gap studies. \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |440", "t": "Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana", "head": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n440\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nRASHTRIYA KRISHI VIKAS YOJANA \n \n      \n \n440. \n \nSHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe\n \nannual growth target fixed for agriculture sector during the financial years \n2015\n-\n16 and 2016\n-\n17 and the extent to which the targets have been achieved; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the State Governments have implemented the Rashtriya Krishi Vikas \nYojana (RKVY) as per \nthe guidelines of the scheme; \n \n \n(c) \n \nif so, whether the Government has played its role in checking and implementing \nthe schemes effectively at national level; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Comptroller and Auditor General of India have drawn the attention of \nthe Gover\nnment towards certain drawbacks of the schemes in its State\n-\nwise review; \nand \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, whether the Government proposes to chalk out any effective policy for \nmeeting the annual growth target of the Agriculture sector?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE \n \nMINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \nto (c)\n:   \n \nRashtriya\n \nKrishi\n \nVikas\n \nYojana (RKVY) was launched during 2007\n-\n08 to \nprovide Additional Central \nAssistance to states to incentivize them to increase their \ninvestment in agriculture and allied sectors to achieve 4%  annual growth in this sector \nduring XI plan period. No specific annual growth target has been fixed for agriculture \nsector under the sche\nme during the particular financial years 2015\n-\n16 and 2016\n-\n17. As \nper the Provisional Estimates of Annual National Income 2015\n-\n16 and Quarterly \nEstimate of GDP released on 31.05.2016, the growth (over the previous year) in the \nGross Value Added (GVA) of agr\niculture and allied sector during 2015\n-\n16 is 1.2% at \n2011\n-\n12 prices. The scheme is being implemented in all states as per the guidelines of \nthe scheme and monitored through web\n-\nbased Management Information System. The \n\nstate\n-\nwise allocation and release of f\nunds since inception of the scheme is given at \nAnnexure.\n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(d) & (e): \n \nYes, Madam. Comptroller and Auditor General of India have reported \ncertain \nshortcomings\n \nin implementation of the scheme in the states during 2007\n-\n08 to \n2012\n-\n13. Government of India has already revised the scheme guidelines for \nimplementation from the financial year 2014\n-\n15 of the XII Plan to make implementation \nof the scheme more effective i\nn the states. As per the revised guidelines of the scheme, \nstates are required to constitute State Level Project Screening Committee (SLPSC) \nheaded by Agriculture Production Commissioner or any other officer nominated by Chief \nSecretary for screening RKVY \nproject proposals prepared by various departments of the \nstate for its suitability, its linkage to District Agriculture Plans (DAPs) and State \nAgriculture Plan (SAP) and its adherence to the RKVY guidelines before the projects \nare placed before the State L\nevel Sanctioning Committee (SLSC) headed by the Chief \nSecretary of the concerned states for approval.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement annexed in reply to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 440 due for answer on 19.07.2016 \n                            \nStatement showing state\n-\nwise allocation and release of funds under RKVY from financial year 2007\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSl. \nNo\n \nName of the \nState/UT\n \n2007\n-\n08\n \n2008\n-\n09\n \n2009\n-\n10\n \n2010\n-\n11\n \n2011\n-\n12\n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh \n \n93.13\n \n61.08\n \n316.57\n \n297.17\n \n410.00\n \n410.00\n \n393.45\n \n432.29\n \n727.74\n \n734.20\n \n601.98\n2\n \nArunachal \nPradesh\n \n2.85\n \n1.90\n \n6.88\n \n0.00\n \n16.10\n \n15.98\n \n39.08\n \n28.95\n \n8.26\n \n10.68\n \n3\n \nAssam \n \n23.77\n \n0.00\n \n142.62\n \n144.12\n \n79.86\n \n79.86\n \n256.87\n \n216.87\n \n227.77\n \n227.77\n \n399.57\n4\n \nBihar\n \n64.02\n \n57.77\n \n148.54\n \n148.54\n \n110.79\n \n110.79\n \n380.94\n \n415.10\n \n506.82\n \n506.82\n \n724.01\n5\n \nChhatisgarh  \n \n60.54\n \n52.96\n \n116.48\n \n117.45\n \n131.78\n \n136.14\n \n461.00\n \n503.42\n \n230.57\n \n212.61\n \n581.12\n6\n \nGoa\n \n2.29\n \n1.70\n \n6.91\n \n0.00\n \n11.87\n \n0.00\n \n11.31\n \n7.07\n \n49.55\n \n24.78\n \n7\n \nGujarat\n \n53.71\n \n49.81\n \n243.39\n \n243.39\n \n386.19\n \n386.19\n \n353.45\n \n388.63\n \n515.48\n \n515.48\n \n586.87\n8\n \nHaryana\n \n23.12\n \n21.52\n \n74.00\n \n39.50\n \n112.77\n \n112.77\n \n204.74\n \n226.80\n \n168.92\n \n176.87\n \n199.49\n9\n \nHimachal \nPradesh\n \n17.39\n \n16.17\n \n15.11\n \n15.11\n \n33.02\n \n33.03\n \n94.85\n \n94.85\n \n99.93\n \n99.93\n \n10\n \nJammu & \nKashmir\n \n6.85\n \n0.00\n \n16.17"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |441", "t": "National Horticulture Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n441\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nNATIONAL HORTICULTURE MISSION \n \n      \n \n441. \n \nSHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe\n \ntotal number of districts covered under the National Horticulture Mission \nacross the country, details thereof, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government is considering to expand the National Horticulture \nMission to the remaining districts as well , if so,\n \nthe details thereof, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has received a representation from the State \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu in this regard; and \n \n \n(d) \n \ni\nf so, the action taken by the Government thereon?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTR\nY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \nA total of three hundred and eighty four (384) districts under National Horticulture \nMission (NHM) are covered in\n \n23 States/U\nT\ns. State\n-\nwise number of districts covered \nunder NHM is at Annexure\n-\nA.\n \n \n(b) to (d):\n \nNo such proposal is under consideration. However, references from Tamil \nNadu\n \nstate government have been received requesting for inclusion of all districts of the \nstate under NHM.\n \n \n \nOn this issue consistent view of the department is that while finalizing/identifying \ndistricts for inclusion under NHM, a cluster approach for developi\nng potential \nhorticultural crops while ensuring backward and forwarded linkages was adopted. \nThe \nselection of the district was decided on the comparative advantage of crops based on \nagro climatic conditions, potential for developing marketing opportunities\n \nwere taken \ninto consideration. No new districts are being added as this may result in thinning out of \nalready limited resources.\n \n \n \nHowever, there is a provision under NHM scheme for setting up of infrastructure \nunits relating to post harvest management, m\narketing and other project based activities \nin non NHM districts, provided there is a clear linkage of the units with the horticulture \nproduce. Apart from this, activities for development of horticulture in non\n-\nNHM districts \ncan be carried out under other \nGovernment schemes like Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana \n\n(RKVY)\n \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA), \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY)\n \netc.\n \n \n \nAbove view of the department was conveyed to the state government.\n \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nA\n \n \n \nStatement showing State wise Districts covered under National Horticulture Mission \nunder MIDH\n.\n \n \n \nSl.No.\n \nName of States/UTs\n \nNo. of Districts\n \n1\n \nAndaman & Nicobar\n \nTwo (2)\n \n2\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \nEleven (11)\n \n3\n \nBihar\n \nTwenty Three (23)\n \n4\n \nChhattisgarh\n \nNineteen (19)\n \n5\n \nDadra & Nagar Haveli\n \nOne (1)\n \n6\n \nDelhi\n \nOne (1)\n \n7\n \nGoa\n \nTwo (2)\n \n8\n \nGujarat\n \nFifteen (15)\n \n9\n \nHaryana\n \nEighteen (18)\n \n10\n \nJharkhand\n \nSeventeen (17)\n \n11\n \nKarnataka \n \nThirty (30)\n \n12\n \nKerala\n \nFourteen (14)\n \n13\n \nLakshadweep\n \nOne (1)\n \n14\n \nMaharashtra\n \nThirty Three (33)\n \n15\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nThirty Nine (39)\n \n16\n \nOdisha \n \nTwenty Four (24)\n \n17\n \nPunjab \n \nEighteen (18)\n \n18\n \nPuducherry\n \nFour (4)\n \n19\n \nRajasthan\n \nTwenty Four (24)\n \n20\n \nTamil Nadu\n \nTwenty (20)\n \n21\n \nTelengana \n \nNine (9)\n \n22\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \nForty Five (45)\n \n23\n \nWest Bengal\n \nFourteen (14)\n \nTotal:\n \n \n384\n \n \n \nNote:\n \nHorticulture Mission for North East and Himalayan\n \nStates \n \n \n \n(HMNEH):  All the districts of North\n-\nEastern and three Himalayan \n \nStates namely Arunachal Pradesh, Assam,\n \nManipur, Mizoram, \n \nNagaland, Meghalaya, Sikkim, Tripura,\n \nHimachal Pradesh, \n \nUttarakhand and Jammu & Kashmir are\n \ncovered under HMNEH \n \nscheme.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n**********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |442", "t": "Remunerative Prices to Farmers", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n442\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nREMUNERATIVE PRICES TO FARMERS \n \n    \n \n442. \n \nSHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nSHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has taken a decision recently to increase the MSP for \nvarious agricultural crops to provide adequate remunerative prices to the farmers as \nwell as to increase the agricultural production, if so, the details thereof along with the \ntot\nal number of crops covered by MSP; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Union Government and the State Governments propose to add 10% \nprofit every year in the MSP given to the farmers, if so the details thereof; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to implement recomm\nendation of \nSwaminathan Commission in toto, if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \n \nThe Government has fixed the Minimum Support Prices (MSPs) for 2014\n-\n15, \n2015\n-\n16 and Kharif crops of 2016\n-\n17 are given at \nAnnexure.\n \n \n(b):\n \nGovernment fixes Minimum Support Prices (MSPs) of various crops based on \nthe recommendations of the Commission for Agricultural Costs & Prices (CACP), views \nof concerned State Governments and Central Ministries/Departments and other relevant \nfactors. While\n \nformulating its recommendations on price policy, the CACP considers, \ninteralia, a number of important factors including cost of production, changes in input \nprices, trends in market prices, demand and supply situation, effect on general price \nlevel, effec\nt on cost of living, etc.\n \n \n(c):\n \nThe National Commission on Farmers (NCF) under the Chairmanship of Dr. M.S. \nSwaminathan was set up in 2004. The Commission submitted its report to the \nGovernment \nin 2006. After due examination, Government accepted many of th\ne \nrecommendations of the NCF and based on these Government approved the ‘National \nPolicy For Farmers, 2007’ (NPF 2007). The NPF\n-\n2007 aims to improve economic \nviability of farming and increases net income of farmers. The Ministry has adopted \nvarious schemes\n/programmes based on the NPF, 20\n07.\n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \nMinimum Support Price for \n2014\n-\n15 and 2015\n-\n16 \n \n \n(in Rs per quintal)\n \nSl. No.\n \nCommodity\n \nVariety\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n \n \n \n \nKHARIF CROPS\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n1\n \nPADDY\n \nCommon\n \n1360\n \n1410\n \n1470\n \n \n \n \n \nGrade 'A'\n \n1400\n \n1450\n \n1510\n \n2\n \nJOWAR\n \nHybrid\n \n1530\n \n1570\n \n1625\n \n \n \n \n \nMaldandi\n \n1550\n \n1590\n \n1650\n \n3\n \nBAJRA\n \n \n \n1250\n \n1275\n \n1330\n \n4\n \nMAIZE\n \n \n \n1310\n \n1325\n \n1365\n \n5\n \nRAGI\n \n \n \n1550\n \n1650\n \n1725\n \n6\n \nARHAR(Tur)\n \n \n \n4350\n \n4625^\n \n5050^^\n \n7\n \nMOONG\n \n \n \n4600\n \n4850^\n \n5225^^\n \n8\n \nURAD\n \n \n \n4350\n \n4625^\n \n5000^^\n \n9\n \nCOTTON \n \nMedium \nStaple\n \n3750\n \n3800\n \n3860\n \n \n \n \n \nLong \nStaple\n \n4050\n \n4100\n \n4160\n \n10\n \nGROUNDNUT IN \nSHELL\n \n \n \n4000\n \n4030\n \n4220*\n \n11\n \nSUNFLOWER SEED\n \n \n \n3750\n \n3800\n \n3950*\n \n12\n \nSOYABEEN \n \nBlack \n \n2500\n \n-\n \n-\n \n \n \n \n \nYellow\n \n2560\n \n2600\n \n2775*\n \n13\n \nSESAMUM\n \n \n \n4600\n \n4700\n \n5000^\n \n14\n \nNIGERSEED\n \n \n \n3600\n \n3650\n \n3825*\n \n \n \nRABI CROPS\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n15\n \nWHEAT\n \n \n \n1450\n \n1525\n \n \n16\n \nBARLEY\n \n \n \n1150\n \n1225\n \n \n17\n \nGRAM\n \n \n \n3175\n \n3500**\n \n \n18\n \nMASUR (LENTIL)\n \n \n \n3075\n \n3400**\n \n \n19\n \nRAPESEED/MUSTARD\n \n \n \n3100\n \n3350\n \n \n20\n \nSAFFLOWER\n \n \n \n3050\n \n3300\n \n \n21\n \nTORIA\n \n \n \n3020\n \n3290\n \n \n*\nIncluding Bonus of Rs. 100 per quintal.\n \n**\nIncluding Bonus of Rs. 75 per quintal .\n \n^\nIncluding Bonus of Rs. 200 per quintal.\n \n^^\nIncluding Bonus of Rs. 425 per quintal.\n \n \n \n \n*********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |443", "t": "Micro Irrigation Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n443\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n            \nMICRO IRRIGATION \nSCHEME\n \n      \n \n443. \n \nSHRI V. ELUMALAI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe micro irrigation scheme has gained acceptance amongst the farming \ncommunity across the country, if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the said scheme is being implemented in the ratio of 60 : 40 \ni.e.\n \n60 per\n-\n \ncent by the Central Government 40 per \ncent by the State Governments, if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has received proposals from various State \nGovernments including Tamil Nadu to allocate more funds for the said scheme; and \n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the action taken by the Govern\nment thereon?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \n \nYes, Madam. Micro Irrigation scheme has been particularly accepted amongst \nthe farming community in the States namely; Maharashtra, Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, \nKarnataka, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh and \nHaryana. In other Sta\ntes, especially in North Eastern Region (NER) and Himalayan \nS\ntates implementation of Micro Irrigation  scheme is still to take off on major scale. \n \n \n(b):\n \nYes, but for NER and Himalayan States where the funding pattern is of 90:10 \nbetween Central and State \nGovernment\ns\n \nrespectively.\n \n \n(c) & (d):\n \nCentral\n \nAssistance is allocated based on overall availability of financial \nresources under PMKSY (Per Drop More Crop) to all States including State of Tamil \nNadu. As per request of Government of Tamil Nadu allocation o\nf fund has been \nenhanced from Rs.63.9 crore of 2014\n-\n15 to Rs.95.0 crore during 2016\n-\n17.\n \n \n \n**********\n \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |444", "t": "Racial Attack", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 444 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nRACIAL ATTACK \n \n444.  SHRI B. SRIRAMULU: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of racial attacks on people hailing from the North Eastern \nStates reported during each of the last three years and the current year, \nState-wise including Karnataka; \n \n(b) whether the Government has identified the reasons for increasing racial \nattacks on such people and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the measures taken by the  Government to address the issue of  racialism \nagainst such people; and \n \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal to promulgate an anti -racial law \nor strengthen the provision under IPC to safeguard the affected people from \nsuch attacks and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) The number of racial attacks reported against the people hailing from \nthe North Eastern States, as available with the Ministry during the last \nthree years and the current year is at Annexure-I  \n--2/- \n \n \n \n\n-2- \nLS.US.Q.NO.444 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \n(b) & (c):  In the wake of unfortunate death of  Nido Tania, a student from \nArunachal Pradesh, Ministry of Home Affairs set up a committee under Shri \nM.P. Bezbaruah in February, 2014  to look into security  concerns of the \npeople from North East States living in  different parts of the country. The \nCommittee had been tasked to examine reasons of attacks on people of North \nEast and suggest remedial measures.  \n \nGovernment of India have issued Advisories from time to time to all \nStates and UTs  directing them to have  a stringent mechanism to deal with \nthe incidents of racial attacks on citizens from North Eastern States.   State \nGovernments and Union Territories are taking all necessary security \nmeasures to strictly follow the directions given in the advisories. The D elhi \nPolice has set up Special Helpline No.1093 w.e.f. 14 th February, 2014 to \naddress to grievance of the citizens from North East living in Delhi.  Bengaluru \nPolice, Gurgaon Police and Mumbai Police have provided helpline \nNos.09480801020, 0124-2301559 and 08976341108 respectively. \n \n(d): The Bezbaruah Committee, recommended insertion of sections 153C and \n509A for making imputations and assertions prejudicial to human dignity and \nword gesture or act intended to insult a member of particular racial group is \nunder examination.   \n****** \n \n \n\n \nLS.US.Q.NO.444 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nDetails of state-wise racial attacks (Section 325, 326, 326A & 326B \nIPC)  on people hailing from North Eastern States as available \nduring last 3 years and current year i.e. 2013 to  2016 (upto \nApril)(Prov.). \n \nS.No. Name of \nState/City \nTotal no. of case of racial attacks reported \nagainst NE  people. \n2013 2014 2015 2016 (up to \nApril)(Prov.) \n1 Andhra Pradesh NA 0 0 0 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh \nNA 0 0 0 \n3 Assam NA 0 0 0 \n4 Bihar NA 0 0 0 \n5 Chhattisgarh NA 0 0 0 \n6 Goa NA 0 0 0 \n7 Gujarat NA 0 0 0 \n8 Haryana - 9 10 10 \n9 Himachal \nPradesh \nNA 0 0 0 \n10 Jammu & \nKashmir \nNA 0 0 0 \n11 Jharkhand NA 0 0 0 \n12 Karnataka 1 3 4 1 \n13 Kerala NA 0 0 0 \n14 Madhya \nPradesh \nNA 19 10 0 \n15 Maharashtra NA 0 0 0 \n16 Manipur NA 0 0 0 \n17 Meghalaya NA 0 0 0 \n18 Mizoram NA 0 0 0 \n19 Nagaland NA 0 0 0 \n20 Odisha NA 0 0 0 \n21 Punjab NA 0 0 0 \n22 Rajasthan NA 0 0 0 \n23 Sikkim NA 0 0 0 \n24 Tamil Nadu NA 0 0 0 \n25 Telangana NA 0 0 0 \n26 Tripura NA 0 0 0 \n27 Uttar Pradesh NA 0 0 0 \n28 Uttarakhand NA 0 0 0 \n29 West Bengal NA 0 0 0 \n30 A&N Islands NA 0 0 0 \n31 Chandigarh NA 0 0 0 \n32 D&N Haveli NA 0 0 0 \n33 Daman & Diu NA 0 0 0 \n34 Delhi NA 0 0 0 \n35 Lakshdweep NA 0 0 0 \n36 Puducherry NA 0 0 0 \nTotal  1 31 24 11 \nNA(Not Available) \n------ "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |445", "t": "Urea Plants", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 445 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 19.07.2016 \n \nUrea plants \n \n445  SHRI S.P. MUDDAHANUME GOWDA: \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government proposes to establish ten more urea production Plants in \nthe country, if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Naphtha fed urea units have urged the Government to continue with the \nexisting system of subsidy after the expiry of 100 days period; and  \n(c) whether the Government is considering to provide LNG to Naphtha fed urea units if it \ndoes not want to continue with the subsidy benefit, if so the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS, SHIPPING , \nROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS              \n  (SHRI MANSUKH L  MANDAVIYA) \n__________________________________________________________________ \n(a):     The Government  had  notified New  Investment  Policy (NIP) – 2012 on 02nd \nJanuary, 2013 and its amendment on 07 th October, 2014 to facilitate fresh investment in urea \nsector and to reduce import dependency.  Presently, Department of Fertilizers has received 6 \nproposals for setting up of Greenfield and Brownfield (Expansion) projects:-    \n \nS. No. Company Projects Ownership State \n1.  Kanpur Fertilizers & \nCement Limited \nGreenfield Project at \nJabalpur \nPrivate Madhya \nPradesh \n2.  Indo-Gulf Fertilizers \nLimited –Jagdishpur \nBrownfield Expansion urea \nproject at Jagdishpur. \nPrivate Uttar \nPradesh \n3.  Chambal Fertilizers & \nChemicals Limited-\nGadepan \nBrownfield of Ammonia-Urea \nunits at Gadepan-Kota.  \nPrivate  Rajasthan \n4.  Matix Fertilizers & \nChemicals Limited- II, \nPanagarh \nBrownfiled Ammonia-Urpea \nFertilizers Complex at \nPanagarh.   \nPrivate West Bengal \n5.  Rashtriya Chemicals & \nFertilizers Limited-Thal \nBrownfield Ammonia -Urea \nExpansion project at Thal. \nCPSU  Maharashtra \n6.  Nagarjuna Fertilizers & \nChemicals Ltd. \nBrownfield Project at \nKakinada, Andhra Pradesh. \nPrivate Andhra \nPradesh \n \n \n \n \n \n\n-: 2 :-  \n \nUnion Government has also decided to establish following six Brownfield/Revival \nurea production plants:-  \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of the fertilizer unit Model \n1.  Namrup-IV unit of the Bhramaputra Valley Fertilizer  \nCorporation Limited (BVFCL) \nPPP Model \n2.  Ramagundam unit of Fertilizer Corporation of India \nLimited (FCIL) \nRevival through \nSpecial Purpose \nVehicle (SPVs) of \nPSUs \n3.  Talcher unit of FCIL \n \n4.  Gorakhpur unit of FCIL \n \n5.  Sindri unit of FCIL \n \n6.  Barauni unit of Hindustan Fertilizer Corporation Limited \n(HFCL) \n(b) & (c): Yes, Madam.  Based on the decision of the CCEA, vide notification dated \n17th June, 2015, Department of Fertilizers has allowed continuation of production of three \nnaphtha based  urea units, i.e. Madras Fertilizers Limited (MFL) - Manali, Southern \nPetrochemicals Industries Corporation (SPIC) -  Tuticorin and Mangalore Chemicals & \nFertilizers Limited (MCFL) with Naphtha as feedstock till these plants get assured supply of \ngas either by pipeline or by any other means. \n*********** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |446", "t": "Dowry Deaths", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 446 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nDOWRY DEATHS \n \n446.  SHRI BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether cases of domestic violence  on women are on the rise in the \ncountry; \n \n(b) if so, the number of women died due to domestic violence particularly \non account of demand of dowry and the action  taken against the guilty \nduring each of the last three years and the current year, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has involved NGOs/organizations to create \nawareness among the people against the demand of dowry; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) the preventive measures taken/being taken by the Government in this \nregard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) & (b): As per information provided by the National Crime Records \nBureau (NCRB), a total of 1,06,527, 1,18,866 and 1,22,877 cases were \nreported under cruelty by husband or his relatives (section 498A IPC) \nduring 2012, 2013 and 2014 respectively. NCRB has started c ollecting \ndata on the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 446 FOR 19.07.2016 \nsince 2014. A total of 426 cases were reported under the Protection of \nWomen from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 during 2014. State/UT wise \ncases reported, cases chargesheeted, cases convicted, persons \narrested, persons chargesheeted and persons convicted under cruelty by \nhusband or his relatives (Section 498A IPC) during 2012 -2014 and the \nProtection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 d uring 2014 is \nenclosed at Annexure-I & II respectively. NCRB does not collect data on \nwomen died due to domestic violence separately. State/UT wise cases \nreported, cases chargesheeted, cases convicted, persons arrested, \npersons chargesheeted and persons co nvicted under dowry deaths \n(Section 304B IPC) during 2012-2014 is enclosed at Annexure-III. \n(c) to (e): State Governments have appointed Dowry Prohibition \nOfficers. In most of the States, the duty of Dowry Prohibition Officer has \nbeen given as additional c harge to existing State functionaries.The \nGovernment of India regularly conducts awareness generation \nprogrammes and publicity campaigns on various laws relating to women \nincluding Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961 through workshops, fairs, cultural \nprogrammes, seminars, training programmes etc. Advertisements are \nregularly brought out in the print and electronic media to create \nawareness on laws relating to rights of women.  \n…3/- \n\n-3- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 446 FOR 19.07.2016 \nThe Ministry of Home Affairs had issued a detailed advisory on 4th \nSeptember, 2009 to all the State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations wherein all the States/UTs have been advised to explore \nthe possibility of associating NGOs working in the area of combating \ncrimes against women and also that all Police stations may be directed \nto display the name and other details of Protection Officers of the area \nappointed under the Domestic Violence Act, 2005.  \n \nMinistry of Home Affairs has requested the State s/UTs dated 5th \nJanuary, 2015 on “Setting up of IUCAWs in different parts of the country, \nthe objective of these units will be to assist local police in investigation \nof heinous crimes viz. Rape, dowry death, acid attack, Human Trafficking \nagainst women et c. which is available at  \nhttp://mha.nic.in/sites/upload_files/mha/files/CrimesagainstWomen0601. \nPDF \nAdvisory dated 12th May, 2015 on Comprehensive approach towards \ncrimes against women has been issued which is available at   \nhttp://mha.nic.in/sites/upload_files/mha/files/AdvisoryCompAppCrimeAgai\nnstWomen_130515.pdf \n \n...4/- \n\n-4- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 446 FOR 19.07.2016 \nAs per Seventh Schedule of the Constitution ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’  \nare the State subjects, and as such, the primary responsibility of \nprevention detection, registration, investigation and prosecution of \ncrimes, including crimes against women pertaining to domestic violence, \nlies with the State Governments/ Union Territory Administrations \n***** \n  \n\n \n\n                  \nAnnexure - I \nState/UT wise cases registered (CR), cases charge sheeted (CS), cases convicted (CV), persons arrested (PAR), persons charge sheeted (PCS) and persons convicted (PCV) under \ncruelty by husband or his relatives  (section 498A IPC) during 2012-2014 \nSL State/UT \n2012 2013 2014 \nCR CS CV PAR PCS PCV CR CS CV PAR PCS PCV CR CS CV PAR PCS PCV \n1 Andhra Pradesh 13389 10689 661 21251 21339 1586 15084 12201 782 22265 20845 1487 6362 5646 228 9270 8864 464 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 26 20 2 26 20 2 29 20 5 32 20 3 42 30 0 44 31 0 \n3 Assam 6407 3971 205 5435 3763 287 8636 5116 142 7806 5277 168 9626 5946 159 12960 7386 168 \n4 Bihar 3686 3125 171 6963 6970 368 4533 3383 194 7923 8474 425 4672 3243 109 5997 5346 232 \n5 Chhattisgarh 980 957 242 2581 25"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |447", "t": "Arrests by NIA in Hyderabad", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 447 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nARRESTS BY NIA IN HYDERABAD \n \n447.   SHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA : \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Investigation  Agency (NIA) has recently \narrested some  persons in Hyderabad who are alleged to  be part of a \nconspiracy to carry out terror  strikes at religious places to trigger \ncommunal riots in Hyderabad and  Bengaluru and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n(b) whether the Government is carrying  out searches from time to time \nto bust IS and other terrorist modules in the country and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n \n(a): The National Investigation Agency (NIA) has registered a case to \ninvestigate the allegations that some youth and their accomplices have \nentered into a criminal conspiracy to wage war against the Government \nof India and were collecting weapons and explosive  material to target \npublic places including religious sites etc., in various parts of the \ncountry.  NIA has so far arrested seven persons from Hyderabad in this \ncase.  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 447 FOR 19.07.2016 \n(b): In order to counter terror activities, t here exists a close and \neffective coordination between intelligence and security agencies at the \nCentre and the State l evels.  This has resulted in busting of several \nterrorist modules including ISIS/ISIL cadres, by the Security Agencies. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |448", "t": "Diversion of Subsidised Urea", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 448 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 19.07.2016 \n \nDiversion of subsidized fertilizers  \n \n \n448 SHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU: \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether highly subsidized urea is being diverted for industrial use leading to  its \nshortage for agriculture, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has made it mandatory for all domestic urea manufacturers to \nproduce and importers to import neem coated urea to be used for agriculture in order to \ncurb its illegal diversion, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(b) the total amount of subsidy as well as foreign exchange likely to be saved as  a \nresult thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE   MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS, SHIPPING, \nROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n(a):    Availability of urea in the year 2015- 16 and during the current year upto June, 2016 was \n329.00 LMT and 75.41 LMT against the requirement of 313.35 LMT and 67.00 LMT \nrespectively. There is no report of shortage of urea meant for agriculture and it is expected that \nconsequent to Neem Coati ng of Urea the diversion will reduce.   Moreover, Government has \ndeclared fertilizer as an essential commodity under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955 and \nnotified Fertilizer Control Order, 1985 and Fertilizer (Movement Control) Order, 1973.  The State \nGovernments have been empowered to take appropriate action to curb diversion of fertilizers \nand initiate action against the persons who violate these orders. Department of Fertilizers has, \nfrom time to time, written to the Chief Secretaries of various States and Ministry of Home Affairs \nto activate the enforcement agencies under their jurisdiction to take appropriate action to curb \ndiversion.   \n \n \n\n-: 2 :- \n (b)& (c):  Government of India through Notification No. 12012/1/2015- FPP dated 25.05.2015 \nhas made it mandatory for all domestic producers to produce 100 % Neem Coated Urea (NCU). \nEntire quantity of indigenously produced subsidized urea is being neem coated w. e.f. 1 st  \nSeptember, 2015.  Imported Urea is also being neem coated w.e.f. Ist December, 2015.Since \nNeem Coated Urea has been introduced recently, it is too early to assess its impact on subsidy \nand import. \n******** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |449", "t": "Projects for Modernisation of Police", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 449 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nPROJECTS FOR MODERNISATION OF POLICE \n \n449.   SHRI K. PARASURAMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has  implemented specific projects to  \nmodernize police forces and Central Armed  Police Force (CAPF) in the \ncountry; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the said projects; \n \n(c) the  action taken by the Government  to establish necessary \ninfrastructure for police and CAPF personnel to enable them  to face new \nchallenges; and \n \n(d) the details of allocation of funds  made by the Government for this \npurpose during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) & (b):  The Government of India has been supplementing the efforts \nand resources of the States by implementing the Scheme for \nModernisation of Police Forces (MPF Scheme). Under this scheme, funds \nare approved and released to the State Governments for plan \ncomponents, such as police operational buildings and housing, and non -\nplan components, such as weapons, equipment for training,  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 449 FOR 19.07.2016 \ncomputerization, forensic science, Mega City Policing (MCP), mobility. \n“Police” being state  subject as per the Seventh Schedule of the \nConstitution, the projects are identified, formulated and implemented by \nthe State Governments based on their actual requirements. However, so \nfar as Central Armed Police Forces are concerned, Modernisation Plan – \nII is being implemented to increase the strike capability of Forces with \nsuperior arms & ammunitions, strategic equipment, effective transport \nvehicles, state -of-the-art communication, surveillance and border \nguarding systems which are the mainstay of any operational strategy.  \n \n(c)& (d) For establishing and upgrading of infrastructure of State \nPolice organizations, under the MPF scheme, the Government has \nallocated and released funds during the last three years and the current \nyear to the State Governments as below: \n(₹ in crore) \n \n \n \n...3/- \n \nYear Allocation Released Plan Non-Plan  Total  \n2013-14 1097.00 245.62 1342.62 1338.35 \n2014-15 860.00 537.50 1397.50 1397.22 \n2015-16 NIL 595.00 595.00 595.00 \n2016-17 NIL 595.00 595.00 NIL* \n\n-3- \n \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 449 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \n* Presently Annual Action Plans of the States are being considered and \non approval of these Plans, releasing of funds will be commenced. \n \nAn allocation of Rs.432.90 crore  has been approved for Mega City \nPolicing in six cities, namely, Hyderabad, Chennai, Mumbai, Bengaluru, \nKolkata and Ahmedabad and till date funds amounting to Rs.184.52 crore \nhas been released. Under this component, projects of CCTV surveillance, \nCommand C ontrol Centre (CCC),  Command Control Centre (CCC), Dial \n100 system, Fusion Centre/Data Centre,   Highway Patrol Cars and Aerial \nSurveillance (UAV/Helicopters) are being implemented by the respective  \nState Governments in these megacities. \n As regards CAPFs, details of allocation of funds for the last three years \nin respect of Modernisation Plan are as below: \n \n \n               Year  \nCAPF \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nAssam Rifles 33.37 23.41 31.88 \nBSF 38.31 42.83 59.64 \nCISF 5.55 8.93 10.23 \nCRPF 1.86 5.00 80.54 \nITBP 5.50 5.32 21.00 \nNSG 5.50 5.94 5.94 \nSSB 8.98 5.50 14.00 \nTotal  99.07 96.93 223.23 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |45", "t": "Setting up of Multi-Product SEZs in Telangana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 45 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2016 \n \nSETTING UP OF MULTI-PRODUCT SEZS IN TELANGANA \n45.  SHRI PRABHAKAR REDDY KOTHA: \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY  (वािणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंत्री ) be \npleased to state: \n(a) the total number of existing Special Economic Zones (SEZs) in T elangana State at \npresent;  \n(b) whether there is only one single product SEZs in the State and i f  s o ,  t h e  d e t a i l s  \nthereof and the reasons therefor;  \n(c) whether the Telangana Government  has requested the Government f or \nestablishment of Multi-Product SEZs in the State; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken by the G overnment on the said \nrequest of Telangana State? \n \nANSWER \n      okf.kT; ,oa m|ksx jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh fueZyk lhrkje.k½ ¼Lora= izHkkj½ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (b): At present, formal approval has been accorded for t he setting up of 53 \nSEZs in the State of Telangana.  Out of these, 44 SEZs have bee n notified and 26 \nSEZs are operational.  All SEZs are sector specific SEZ and have been approved by \nthe State. \n \n(c) and (d):  Special Economic Zones (SEZs) being set up under the SEZs Act, \n2005 and SEZ Rule, 2006 are primar ily private investment driven .  No proposal has \nbeen received for setting up of Multi-product SEZ in the State of Telangana.    \n \n****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |450", "t": "Security to Private Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 450 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nSECURITY TO PRIVATE SECTOR \n \n450.   DR. P. VENUGOPAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Central Industrial  Security Force (CISF) has decided to  \nprovide full time security cover by its armed  commandos to the private \nsector establishment; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether it is a fact that the CISF has  been receiving payment from \nthese private  sector establishments for the security  provided by CISF \ncommandos; and \n \n(d) if so, the total amount received from  each establishment along with \nthe strength  of personnel deployed by CISF in each  unit of the private \nsector establishments  during each of the last three years and the  \ncurrent year, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n \n(a) & (b)  The CISF Act, 1968 as amended vide Act No.22 of 2009, \nprovides for deployment of CISF in private units also.  As on date, CISF is \ndeployed in 08 Private Sector Units, as detailed below:- \n \n \n...2/- \n\n-2- \n \nLS.U.S.Q.NO. 450 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \n \n(c) & (d)  The cost of CISF deployment at respectiv e private sector \nestablishments is borne by the private establishment concerned.  The \ndetails are given below:- \nAmount in Rs \n   2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 GRAND \nTOTAL \nSN\nO \nNAME OF \nPRIVATE \n ESTT. \nSANCTIONED \nSTRENGTH \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \n KARNATAKA       \n1 Infosys Tech  \nBangalore \n176 41769759 49929409 54084208 12771957 158555333 \n2 Electronic City  \nBangalore \n84 27809608 31982638 32603426 8037192 100432864 \n3 Infosys Tech  \nMysore \n105 18567578 22532267 24865527 6049940 72015312 \n GUJARAT       \n4 Reliance  \nIndustries  \n217 90257442 99756021 105316944 25805927 321136334 \n5 CGPL/ UMPL \n Mundra \n99 0 0 24462167 12888549 37350716 \n MAHARASHTRA       \n6 Infosys Tech  \nPune \n134 61654991 69034992 74724780 18044112 223458875 \n ODISHA       \n7 Tata Steel Ltd.  \nKalingnagar \n278 0 0 23819076 23819076 47638152 \n UTTARAKHAND       \n8 Patanjali Food & \nHarbalPark \nHaridwar \n35 0 0 11688240 3891273 15579513 \n  TOTAL 1128 240059378 273235327 351564368 111308026 976167099 \n \n***** \nSNo Pvt Sector Unit Strength sanctioned for deployment \n1 Infosys Tech Bangalore, Karnataka 176 \n2 Electronic City Bangalore, Karnataka 84 \n3 Infosys Tech Mysore, Karnataka 105 \n4 Reliance Industries, Gujarat 217 \n5 CGPL/UMPL  Mundra, Gujarat 99 \n6 Infosys Tech Pune, Mahrashtra 134 \n7 Tata Steel Ltd. Kalingnagar, Odisha 278 \n8 Patanjali Food & HerbalPark Haridwar, \nUttarakhand \n35 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |451", "t": "Rented Godowns", "head": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 451 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nRENTED GODOWNS \n \n451.  SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the available storage capacity of FCI against the procurement  \ntarget of rice and wheat in Uttar Pradesh is low; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof indicating the total covered and open storage \ncapacity available in the State; \n \n(c)  whether despite the utilization of  storage capacity available in the State \nabout 30 lakh tonnes of foodgrains are kept in the rented godowns and most of \nthese godowns are owned by trading mafia who misuse these godowns to deny \nthe benefits of the Government schemes to the bonafide beneficiaries; \n \n(d)  if so, whether the Government  proposes to construct new godowns as per  \nits procurement needs; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details of the action plan finalised for the purpose? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a)& (b): No, Madam. Sufficient storage capacity is available in Uttar Pradesh (UP) \nregion with Food Corporation of India (FCI) and state agencies for meeting \nprocurement targets.  The total  capacity of  covered & open storage in the form \nof Cover and Plinth (CAP) facilities  currently available in UP with various \nagencies as on 30.06.2016 is as under :- \nStorage Agency Covered Capacity  \n(in Lakh MT) \nCAP Capacity \n(in Lakh MT) \nTOTAL  \n(in Lakh MT) \nFCI 14.95 5.19 20.14 \nCWC # 8.20 0 8.20 \nSWC $ 19.44 0.65 20.09 \nPEG * 12.53 0 12.29 \nTotal 55.12 5.84 60.96 \n# Central Warehousing Corporation.  \n$ State Warehousing Corporation. \n* Constructed and hired under Private Entrepreneurs Guarantee (PEG) Scheme.  \n(c), (d) & (e): In view of reply to (a) & (b) given above, question does not arise. \n**** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |453", "t": "GM Crops", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 453 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nGM Crops \n \n 453. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of Genetically Modified (GM) crops/seeds approved for field trials in the \ncountry in the recent past; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to grant permission for re -trial of 15-GM seeds as per \nthe new norms; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to start a nation- wide discussion again by forming an \nexpert committee to assess the advantages and disadvantages of genetically modified \ncrops in view of the rising demands of foodgrains; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the names of GM crops which are permitted for \ncultivation and the outcome thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) In India, the permission for conduct of confined field trials of GM crops (also referred as \nGenetically Engineered (GE) crops are approved by the Genetic Engineering Appraisal \nCommittee (GEAC) as per “Rules for the manufacture, use, import, export & storage of \nhazardous micro organisms, genetically engineered organisms or cells, 1989” notified \nunder the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986. GM cotton, corn, brinjal, maize, potato, \nrice & chickpea with insect resistance or herbicide tolerance or both have been approved \nfor conduct of confined field trials in the country in the last three years. \n \n(b) & (c) The permission for conduct of confined field trials of GM crops is given on a case \nby case basis after extensive review of the application by GEAC. There is neither a \nproposal nor any new norms to grant permission for re-trial of 15 GM seeds.  \n \n\n(d) GEAC is the apex committee consisting of representatives from the concerned \nministries/departments and experts for regulation of GM crops on a case by case basis as \nper “Rules for the manufacture, use, import, export & storage of hazardous micro \norganisms, genetically engineered organisms or cells, 1989” notified under the \nEnvironment (Protection) Act, 1986. The advantages/disadvantages of GM c rops are \nreviewed on a case by case basis as part of evaluation process by GEAC and is ongoing \nprocess.  \n \n(e) As on date, Bt cotton is the only crop approved for commercial cultivation in India. Bt \ncotton hybrids containing five approved events are widely cultivated in India.   \n \n \n************** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |454", "t": "Visa Policy for BRICS Countries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 454 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nVISA POLICY FOR BRICS COUNTRIES \n \n454.   SHRI G. HARI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is  considering for a visa waiver/visa on \narrival for business visitors and tourists belonging  to Brazil, Russia, \nIndia, China and South Africa (BRICS) countries; and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) & (b): A proposal in this regard received from the Department of \nCommerce is under consideration.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |455", "t": "Withdrawal of AFSPA from NER", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 455 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nWithdrawal of AFSPA from NER \n \n455.   SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is  considering to withdraw the Armed \nForces Special Powers Act (AFSPA) in the North Eastern region; \n \n(b) whether it is true that the North East States have been demanding its \nremoval for a long time and if so, the reaction of the Government \nthereto; \n \n(c) whether the said Act has been  removed in some parts of the North \nEast; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n \n(a):  No, Madam. At present there is no proposal under consideration to \nwithdraw AFSPA from the North Eastern Region. \n \n(b):  The notification for extension of notification under the Act is \nissued with the concurrence of the state governments. Govts of \nArunachal Pradesh, Assam , Meghalaya have been recommending \nissuance of notifications in these states/portions of states. Government  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 455 FOR 19.07.2016 \nof Nagaland has not supported issuance of notificatio n in that state. For \nstate of M anipur, notification of AFSPA is issued by the state \ngovernment.  \n \n(c) & (d): AFSPA, 1958 was withdrawn form the areas falling in the \njurisdiction of 30 Police Stations of Tripu ra by State Government vide its \nNotifications dated 27.5.2015. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |456", "t": "Inter-State Trade of Potato", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n456\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nINTER\n-\nSTATE TRADE OF \nPOTATO\n \n \n456. \n \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government proposes to formulate any concrete policy to promote \nthe inter\n-\nstate trade of agricultural produce including potato; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe per annum quantum of potato in tonne procured by Jharkhand from other \nStates;\n \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to implement any special scheme to promote \nthe cultivation of potato in various States of the country including, Jharkhand; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, the details thereof along with the financial assistance provided by the \nGo\nvernment to various States in this regard?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n:\n   \n \nThe Government implements Central Sector Scheme namely National \nAgricultural Market (e\n-\nNAM), whose objective \ninter alia\n \nis to develop barrier free \nNational market to promote inter\n-\nstate trade of agricultural produce including potato. \n \n \n(c) to (e):\n \nAs per i\nnformation provided by Government of Jharkhand, the annual \nreported quantum of arrival of potato in Jharkhand from other States is approximately \n2,78,543 tonne. Presently there is no special scheme to promote the cultivation of \npotato in the various States\n \nof the country including Jharkhand.\n \n\n \n********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |457", "t": "Women Friendly Agricultural Equipments", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n457\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nWOMEN FRIENDLY AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENTS \n \n      \n \n457. \n \nSHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT: \n \nSHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nmigration of agricultural workers and poor people from rural areas to \ncities in search of jobs has adversely impacted the agriculture/farming sector in the \ncountry;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government is considering a plan to d\nevelop women\n-\nfriendly farm \nequipment to sustain the agriculture sector; \n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe action being taken by the Government in this regard?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFAR\nE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nAs per Economic Survey 2013\n-\n14, the share of agriculture and allied \nsector in total employment in the country declined from 59.9% in 1999\n-\n2000 to \n58.5% in \n2004\n-\n05 and further to 48.9% in 2011\n-\n12. This reduction is on account of growth of \nsecondary and tertiary sector and is considered as a natural part\n \nof the development \nprocess. Main reasons for migration include poverty, high population pressure o\nn land, \nlack of basic infrastructural facilities like health care and education, better job \nopportunities, relatively higher wages etc. However, the adverse impact of migration on \nthe agriculture/farming sector is compensated by increased level of agricult\nural \nmechanization which is evident \nfrom the fact that the farm power availability in India has \nincreased from 0.47 kW/ha in the year 2000 to 1.84 kW/ha in the year 2013\n-\n14.\n \n   \n \n\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(c) to (e):\n \nIn order to enable women to perform various farm \noperations with lesser \ndrudgery, various implements and \nhand tools have been developed by different \nagricultural institutes under In\ndian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) viz. Central \nInstitute for Women in Agriculture, Bhubaneswar; Central Institute\n \nof Agricultural \nEngineering (C\nI\nEAE), Bhopal and All India Coordinated Research Project (AICRP) \nCentres in State Agriculture Universities. The National Gender Resource Centre in \nAgriculture \nunder the Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare\n \nhas \nbrought out a compendium of the Gender Friendly Tools/Equipments for Women in \nAgriculture.\n \n \n \nThe use of gender friendly equipments\n \nis promoted through training, \ndemonstration and financial assistance under Sub\n-\nMission on Agricultural \nMechanization. Wo\nmen beneficiaries are provided 10% additional financial assistance \nfor purchase of various agricultural machines and equipments. State Governments have \nalso been directed to ensure flow of benefits to women farmers by way of earmarking at \nleast 30% of allo\ncation of this Sub\n-\nMission.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n                                                                                                    \n \n \n \n**********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |458", "t": "Mango Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n458\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nMANGO PRODUCTION \n \n \n458. \n \nSHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA: \n \n      \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe mango production and export is likely to drop due to unseasonal \nrainfall this year, if so, the details thereof indicating the production of mango, State\n-\nwise \nalong with its export during each of the last three years and the expected foreign \nexchange e\narnings from the surplus mango production during the current year; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government is considering to provide assistance to the affected \nfarmers and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to promote mango prod\nuction in the country?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \n \nAccording to the information received from \nthe states so far, there is likely to be \nan increase of 6% in mango production in the year, 2015\n-\n16 as compared to the \nprevious year, 2014\n-\n15. However, there has been drop in the mango exports during the \nyear 2015\n-\n16.\n \n \n \nThe details of state wise production\n \nof mango during the last three years and the \ncurrent year and Exports with Foreign Exchange\n \nEarnings are at Table 1 and 2 of \nAnnexure\n-\nI respectively.\n \n \n(b):\n \nIn the states like Telangana, which has reported that there might be some mango \nfarmers affected du\ne to weather vagaries, \n \nbased on the final enumeration reports, the \nassistance will be provided to the affected farmers as per State Disaster Response \nFunds (SDRF) norms.\n \n \n\nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(c):\n \nSeveral steps taken by the Government to increase the Country’\ns production of \nfruits and vegetables including mango are as under:\n \n \n\n \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH), a Centrally \nSponsored Scheme is being implemented during XII Plan w.e.f. 2014\n-\n15, for \nholistic growth of the horticulture sector\n \ncovering all the horticulture crops \nincluding fruits and vegetables.\n \n \n\n \nThe Mission envisages production and productivity improvement of horticulture \ncrops including fruit and vegetable crops through increased coverage of area \nwith improved cultiva\nrs, rejuv\nenation of senile \n \norchards, protected cultivation, \ncreation of water resources, adoption of Integrated Pest Management (IPM), \nIntegrated Nutrient Management (INM), organic farming, including insitu \ngeneration of organic inputs. Capacity buildings of farme\nrs and technicians is \nalso provided for adopting improved technologies.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nTable\n-\n1: \nStatewise Production of Mango\n \nP: Production in '000 Tonnes\n \nSTATES/UTs \n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16 \n \n(2nd Adv. Est)\n \nANDAMAN NICOBAR\n \n2.80\n \n3.85\n \n2.93\n \n2.50\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n4406.92\n \n2737.01\n \n2822.08\n \n3049.93\n \nASSAM\n \n55.27\n \n44.59\n \n45.69\n \n57.86\n \nBIHAR\n \n1363.80\n \n1367.57\n \n1272.00\n \n1464.90\n \nCHHATISGARH\n \n291.83\n \n327