ipur 132.00 181.00 100.00 299.80 402.00\n17 Meghalaya 322.00 58.00 64.00 150.70 210.00\n18 Mizoram 98.00 95.00 56.00 50.90 95.00\n19 Nagaland 74.00 108.00 60.00 4.00 42.00\n20 Odisha 1,273.00 1,716.00 1178.00 1382.70 1650.00\n21 Punjab 213.00 292.00 302.00 221.10 135.00\n22 Rajasthan 259.00 970.00 415.00 559.90 571.00\n23 Sikkim 74.00 137.00 96.00 68.60 90.00\n24 T"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |745", "t": "New Policy on Coal Swapping", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.745 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nNEW POLICY ON COAL SWAPPING \n \n745.  SHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes  to announce a new policy on coal \nswapping; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  whether the Government is targeting  a total investment of US$12 trillion in  \npower sector in the country and if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL, NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) & (b) :  The Government has already started the process to implement the \nscheme for flexibility in utilization of domestic coal amongst various generating \nstations which would reduce cost of power generation. Under the scheme, the \nAnnual Contracted Quantity (ACQ) o f each individual coal linkage as per Fuel \nSupply Agreement, to be aggregated as consolidated ACQ for each State and \nCompany owning Central generating stations instead of individual generating \nstation. The State/Central Gencos would have flexibility to uti lize their coal in \nmost efficient and cost effective manner  in their own power plants as well as by \ntransferring coal to other State/Central Gencos Power plants for generation of \ncheaper power.  \n(c) :   The Government is making efforts to provide for the coal, power & \nrenewable sectors a possible investment opportunity of about $ 250 billion during \nfive-year period between 2015 to 2020. When extrapolated to a 15 year \nprogramme until 2030, the coal, power and renewable sector alone provides $1 \ntrillion opportunity. This investment by 2030 is in area like transmission, opening \nof new mines, to bring technology into the coal sector, push towards energy \nefficiency, to bring in new coal generating plants which will be environmentally \nsuperior plants, to make dis tribution sector profitable business for the state \ndiscoms.   \n************* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |746", "t": "Implementation of MGNREGS in Maharashtra", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.746\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nIMPLEMENTATION OF MGNREGS IN MAHARASHTRA\n746. SHRI CHANDRAKANT KHAIRE:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme\n(MGNREGS) is being implemented in Maharashtra;\n(b) if so, the number of unemployed people enrolled under the said scheme in the State\ntill date;\n(c) the number of people who have been provided employment under the scheme and\nfunds released and spent during the last three years in the State;\n(d) whether it is also a fact that unemployed people are not getting employment properly\nunder the scheme; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and steps being taken by the Government thereon?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): Yes, Madam.\n(b): This Ministry does not keep the count of unemployed persons in the country including\nMaharashtra. However, demand for work is registered as and when it is received. Details of\nhouseholds provided employment as demanded by job card holders in Maharashtra are\nindicated in the Annexure.\n(c) to (e): The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act\n(MGNREGA) provides a legal guarantee for 100 days of employment in a financial year to\nevery rural household including Maharashtra whose adult members volunteer to do unskilled\nmanual work. Not less than 100 days of employment are provided to registered workers upon\nreceipt of their demand. Details of rural households provided employment as demanded by\njob card holders, the amount released and expenditure under the MGNREGS during the last\nthree years in Maharashtra are indicated in the Annexure.\n*****\n\nAnnexure referred in reply to parts (b) &(c)to(e) of Lok Sabha Unstarred\nQuestion No. 746 dated 21.07.2016\nMaharashtra State\n(In lakh)\nFinancial Year\nNo. of HH\nprovided\nemployment\nCentral Release Total\nExpenditure\n2013-14 11.44 115292.02 127467.66\n2014-15 11.60 79951.77 160988.49\n2015-16 12.75 123834.73 184503.76\n2016-17 as on\n15/07/2016 10.27 112850.7 116903.18\nJob cards - 78.29 Lakh"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |748", "t": "Conservation of Ground Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 748 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nCONSERVATION OF GROUND WATER \n†748.        SHRI HARISH CHANDRA ALIAS HARISH DWIVEDI \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION  be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether desired results under schemes formulated for conservation of ground water have \nbeen achieved by the Government during the last three years;  \n(b) if so, whether amount allocated under the said schemes is proportionately spent by the \nCentral and State Governments;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government has set any target regarding ground- water conservation and if so, \nthe details thereof; and  \n(e) the details of the schemes formulated to check the indiscriminate exploitation of ground \nwater? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCE S, RIVER DEVELOPMENT  AND \nGANGA REJUVENATION \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a) to (e) During XI Plan, Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) had taken up \nDemonstrative Rain Water Harvesting and Artificial Recharge projects in 22 States of the \nCountry, under Central Sector Scheme  \"Ground   Water  Management  \n& Regulation\". \nUnder the scheme,  133 demonstrative recharge projects costing Rs.  99.87  crore were \napproved for construction of recharge structures  in various States/UTs with an anticipated \nannual recharge to ground water at about 55.20  MCM.   The scheme on artificial recharge \nproject has been discontinued during the XII Plan. Till date, Rs. 92.70 crore has been \nreleased. \n \nThe impact assessment studies carried out in few projects h as verified rise in ground water \nlevels, increase in sustainability   of  ground  water  abstraction  structures  and  enhancement   \nin  pumping duration. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |749", "t": "Energy from Human Waste", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 749 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \nENERGY FROM HUMAN WASTE \n749. SHRI KIRTI AZAD: \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \na. whether the Government is aware that the gas emanating from the rotting human waste can \n be utilised as a source of energy besides improving the sanitation scenario; \nb. if so, whether the Government has made any assessment in this regard; \nc. if so, the details thereof; \nd. whether this source of renewable energy can be developed; and  \ne. e.if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n      ANSWER \n MINISTER OF STATE  FOR POWER, COAL , NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY & MINES (SHRI \nPIYUSH GOYAL) \n (a): Yes, Madam. By processing the human waste scientifically through Anaerobic Digesters, biogas \ncan be  produced, which can be utilized as a source of energy for cooking, heating, lighting and \ngenerating electricity and this process helps in improving the sanitation.  \n (b) & (c): No, Madam. However, the human waste is a potential feedstock for generating biogas that \ncan be deployed for various applications. Presently, under the scheme of National Biogas and \nManure Management Programme (NBMMP) of the Ministry of New and Renew able Energy, the \nhousehold biogas plants based on cattle dung waste are linked with the sanitary toilets . This adoption  \nmainly depends upon the acceptability of the individual household in the rural and semi -urban areas \nof the country. \n (d) & (e): Yes, Madam. The large systems of human excreta processing through biogas plants can be \ndeployed by following effective methods and procedures with fulfillment of certain requirements \nsuch as –  \ni.Surface water and subsoil water-table should not be contaminated. \nii.There should be no contamination of ground water. \niii.The per capita use of water for toilet should be restricted to the limit as required for anaerobic \ndigestion of human waste. \niv.The system should not be accessible to flies or animals. \nv. There should be freedom from smell or unsightly conditions. \nvi.The effluent left out of the biogas plants should require no hand -handling and requires  safe and \ncovered  disposal.  \n \nvii.The human excreta based biogas plant leftover slurry should not be permitted to be touched by open \nhands while transforming it into organic manure which can be used for different purpose. \nviii.The method used should be simple and inexpensive in construction and operation. \nix.There should not be any danger of air pollution. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |75", "t": "Ragging in Universities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.75 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.7.2016 \n \nRagging in Universities \n  \n75.  SHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO: \nSHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN: \nDR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT: \nSHRI SATAV RAJEEV: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \nDR. J. JAYAVARDHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is planning  massive campaign including creation of \nposters, jingles, commercials and short film s against ragging which will be shown in \nuniversities across the country for this current academic year, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b)  the total number of cases of ragging in cluding cases that have resulted in death \nor maiming of students reported in different co lleges of the country during the last year \nalong with the number of States that have registered high number of ragging cases;  \n \n(c)  whether the University Grants Commissi on (UGC) has written letters asking them \nto address the issue, if so, the response thereto; and  \n \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to amend the rules/ law pertaining to \npunishment for ragging if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam. During the financial year  2015-16, MHRD allocated a sum of Rs. 5 \ncrore for Media Campaign to UGC for underta king a publicity campaign against ragging \nin Higher Educational In stitutions in the country. The UGC has spent an amount of Rs. \n4.78 crore on various activities, including preparing and broadcasting of TVCs on Anti-\nRagging by Doordarshan, maki ng of films by NFDC, co verage through FM Radio and \nprinting & mailing of posters by DAVP and broadcasting on All India Radio by Prasar \n\nBharati. The UGC had also announced a competition for students, teachers and general \npublic for wide publicity and prom otion of Anti-Ragging measur es which include Poster \ndesigning, Logo/Icon/Slogan designing and Essay competition.  \n(b):  390 cases of ragging, including death or  maiming of students, were reported in \ndifferent colleges of the count ry during the last year, incl uding the States of Uttar \nPradesh, West Bengal and Madhya Pradesh which have reported higher number of \ncases.  \n(c):  Yes, Madam. UGC has sent lette rs dated 28.7.2015 and 10.2.2016 to the Chief \nSecretaries and the Education Secretaries of the concerned States where the ragging \ncases reported are high. \n(d):  No, Madam. UGC is taking action on  punishment for ragging as per UGC’s \nRegulation on Curbing the Menace of Ragging in Higher Education Institutions, 2009. In \ncases and situations where the provisions  of the Indian Penal Code are attracted, \nappropriate action is also taken. The UGC has  also recently exp anded the definition of \n‘ragging’ by bringing out the Third Amendment of it s Anti-Ragging Regulations on \n29.6.2016.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |750", "t": "Online Services in Courts", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE\n(DEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE)\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 750\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THRUSDAY, THE 21st JULY, 2016\nOnline Services in Courts\n+750.SHRI HARIOM SINGH RATHORE\nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of courts in the country which have started online \nservices, State/ UT-wise;\n(b) the number of cases disposed of by these courts consequent \nupon commencement of online services, year and court-wise; \nand\n(c) the details of courts which have disposed of cases using \nVideo Conferencing (VC) facility along with the number of \nsuch cases disposed of during each of the last three years and \nthe current year, court-wise?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LAW AND JUSTICE AND \nELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY\n(SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY)\n(a): As per the available information, a total of 13672 courts in the \ncountry have started online services. The details are as follows:\nS.No. High Court Name No. of computerised \ncourts \n1. Allahabad 1991\n2. Andhra Pradesh 806\n3. Bombay 1896\n4. Calcutta 762\n5. Chattisgarh 242\n6. Delhi 410\n7. Gauhati 294\n\nS.No. High Court Name No. of computerised \ncourts \n8. Gujarat 710\n9. Himachal Pradesh 98\n10. Jabalpur 1119\n11. Jammu And Kashmir 102\n12. Jharkhand 430\n13. Jodhpur 778\n14. Karnataka 754\n15. Kerala 397\n16. Madras 668\n17. Orissa 423\n18. Patna 796\n19. Punjab And Haryana 743\n20. Sikkim 10\n21. Uttarakhand 152\n22. Tripura 57\n23. Manipur 27\n24. Meghalaya 7\nTotal 13672\n(b): Consequent to the computerisation of courts, online services \nare being provided to litigants such as details of case registration, \ncause list, case status, daily orders, and final judgments. \nHowever, the number of cases disposed of by courts consequent \nupon commencement of online services is not maintained \nseparately. \n(c): Video Conferencing is only a facility to expedite disposal of\ncases. It is presently being used at some of the Courts for \nproduction of accused through VC for judicial remand purposes.  \nHowever, the number of cases disposed of by courts consequent \nupon commencement of online services is not maintained \nseparately."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |751", "t": "Road Projects to Private Players", "head": "GOVERMNENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 751 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2016 \n \nROAD PROJECTS TO PRIVATE PLAYERS \n \n751.  SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n  \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n   सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any new model like hybrid model is being considered for awarding road projects to \nprivate players; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of road projects which are under consideration for award to private players under \nvarious models; \n \n(d) the latest status of road projects which were completed during the last three years as against \nthe target; and \n \n(e) whether the target has not been achieved, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (e) Yes Madam. 19 projects aggregating length of 810 kms have been awarded on \nHybrid Annuity Model so far. Default mode of delivery  for highway construction is Build -\nOperate-Transfer (BOT) (Toll).  During FY 2015 -16, 7 project s aggregating length of 874 kms  \nhave been awarded on BOT (Toll) and are in various stages of construction. The details of \nconstruction and award of National Highway projects during the last three years are as follows:- \n \nYear Construction (in km) Award (in km) \n Target Achievement Target Achievement \n2013-14 6300 4225 9638 3169 \n2014-15 6300 4340 8500 7980 \n2015-16 6300 6029 10000 10098 \n  \n Bottlenecks in the sector were identified and resolved through policy initiatives as a \nresult of which targets could be achieved as stated above.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |752", "t": "IHHL", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Drinking Water and Sanitation \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 752  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21-7-2016 \n \nIHHL \n \n752. SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \n \nWill the Minister of  DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether several lakhs of Individual House Hold Latrines (IHHLs ) have been constructed \nacross the country during the year 2015-16 under Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin); \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether it is also true that the Government is yet to rel ease its shares to the State  \nGovernments for implementation of the said scheme and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether it is also true that the State Government of Tamil  Nadu has requested for the release \nof central share for the years2015-16 and 2016-17 amounting to Rs. 602.02 crores; and \n \n(e) if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n \n (a) and (b) 127.41 lakhs of Individual House Hold Latrines (IHHLs) have b een constructed \nacross the country during the year 2015-16 under Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin) \n \n(c) During 2015-16, Rs. 6362.96 crore have been released to States for i mplementation of \nSwachh Bharat Mission (Gramin). \n \n(d) and (e) Rs. 78.94 crore and Rs.268.51 crore have been released to Tami l Nadu for the \nyears2015-16 and 2016-17 respectively under Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin).  \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |753", "t": "Maintenance of Roads under PMGSY", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.753\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nMAINTENANCE OF ROADS UNDER PMGSY\n753. SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of roads constructed and to be constructed under PMGSY in Haryana;\n(b) whether standard criteria/parameters used are strictly maintained/observed during the\nconstruction of rural roads under the said scheme;\n(c) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise including Haryana;\n(d) whether the Government is aware of the fact that the proposals regarding the\nmaintenance of dilapidated roads in rural areas are ignored under the said scheme; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and the action being taken by the Government thereon,\nState/UT-wise?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): Under Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), 520 number of road works,\nhaving length of 5561.49 km have been sanctioned by the Ministry to the State of Haryana\nand 507 number of road works, having length of 5522.52 km have been reported to be\ncompleted by the State of Haryana (upto May, 2016).\n(b)&(c): As per the Programme Guidelines, rural roads constructed under PMGSY will be in\naccordance with the provisions of Ministry of Rural Development’s  Specifications for Rural\nRoads and specification as given in the Rural Roads Manual (IRC:SP20 :2002) issued by\nIndian Road Congress (IRC).\n(d)&(e): As per PMGSY Programme Guidelines, all the roads being constructed under\nPMGSY will be covered under a 5 -years maintenance contract to be entered into along with\nthe construction contract, with the same contractor, as per the Standard Bidding Document\n(SBD).  Maintenance funds to service the contract is budgeted by the State Government and\nplaced at the disposal of State Rural Roads Development Agency (SRRDA) in a separate\nMaintenance Fund Account. On expiry of 5 -years post-construction maintenance, the roads\nshall be placed und er Zonal Maintenance Contracts consisting of 5 -years maintenance\nincluding renewal of the surface, as per cycle.  The State Governments are required to make\nthe necessary budget provision and place the funds to service the Zonal Maintenance\nContracts at the disposal of the SRRDA in the Maintenance Fund Account.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |755", "t": "Proposals under PMGSY", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.755\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nPROPOSALS UNDER PMGSY\n755. SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of proposals sent by the Government of Madhya Pradesh under the\nPradhan Mantri Grameen Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) during the last three years;\n(b) the number of schemes sanctioned out of these and the number of them pend ing for\nsanction; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government so far to award sanction to these proposals?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) & (b): The Ministry has sanctioned a total of 2151 works under PMGSY to Madhya\nPradesh in the last three years. In respect of proposals of 504 roads and 145 bridges, the\nEmpowered Committee within the Ministry has considered and advised the state to submit\ncompliances on certain specific aspects which are awaited. Once received and examined at\nthe level of the Ministry, these proposals would also be sanctioned.\n(c): The State Governments are required to submit Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) to the\nMinistry for consideration/approval of projects under PMGSY.  The project proposals are\nscrutinized by National Rural Roads Development Agency (NRRDA) to ensure that the\nproposals have been made duly keeping in view the programme guidelines and that they have\nbeen duly verified by the State Technical Agencies (STAs). The proposals of the State are\nthen put up before the Empowered Committee, c haired by Secretary (RD) in the Ministry of\nRural Development, for its consideration.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |756", "t": "Pending Road Projects", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 756 \nANSWERED ON 21ST  JULY, 2016 \n  \nPENDING ROAD PROJECTS \n \n756. SHRI RAVINDRA KUMAR PANDEY: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of ongoing/pending road  projects in the country, State/UT -wise including \nJharkhand; \n(b) the details of road projects completed in Jharkhand during the last one year; \n(c)  the details of funds allocated for  construction and repairing of road projects  in the \ncountry, State/UT-wise; \n(d) whether road projects are getting delayed/stalled due to shortage of labour; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and the  effective steps taken by  the Government to complete the \nroad projects in a time bound manner? \n \n   \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n(a) to (b) The details of ongoing and pending road projects in the country including Jharkhand \nare given at Annexure -I. Six numbers of road projects are completed during the last one year  in \nJharkhand.  \n \n(c) The details of funds allocated for  construction and repairing of road projects  in the country  \nare given at Annexure-II. \n \n(d) and (e) No Madam. However, some of the projects under different phases of N ational \nHighway Development Program are delayed mainly due to problems in land acquisition,   utility \nshifting, non availability of Soil/Aggregates, Poor performance of contractors, Environment/ \nForest/Wildlife Clearance, ROB & RUB issue with Railways, Public agitation for additional \nfacilities,  Arbitration/contractual disputes with contractors etc. \n \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY  TO PART ( a) AND (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.756  ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 ASKED BY SHRI \nRAVINDRA KUMAR PANDEY REGARDING PENDING ROAD PROJECTS. \n \nDetails of Ongoing and Pending National Highways Projects in 2016-17    \n \nS No State Ongoing Projects  Pending Projects \n1 A & N Island 7 2 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 17 1 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 \n4 Assam 14 11 \n5 Bihar 27 7 \n6 Chhattisgarh 10 0 \n7 Goa 4 3 \n8 Gujarat 16 1 \n9 Haryana 12 2 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 35 7 \n11 Jammu & Kashmir 6 0 \n12 Jharkhand 24 3 \n13 Karnataka 60 6 \n14 Kerala 4 1 \n15 Madhya Pradesh 27 12 \n16 Maharastra 33 7 \n17 Manipur 18 1 \n18 Meghalaya 13 4 \n19 Mizoram 14 5 \n20 Nagaland 7 0 \n21 Orissa 36 11 \n22 Puduchery 3 0 \n23 Punjab 18 9 \n24 Rajasthan 27 12 \n25 Sikkim 0 0 \n26 Tamil Nadu 19 2 \n27 Telangana 21 1 \n28 Uttar Pradesh 22 10 \n29 Uttarakhand 45 9 \n30 West Bengal 34 0 \n31 LWE 78 39 \n32 NHDP-IV A 106 9 \n33 NHIIP/EAP 15 0 \n34 BRO 91 48 \n35 SARDP-NE(PWD) 40 36 \n36 NHAI 185 115 \n37 NHIDCL 29 0 \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY  TO PART ( c) OF LOK SABHA UN STARRED \nQUESTION NO.756  ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 ASKED BY SHRI RAVINDRA KUMAR \nPANDEY  REGARDING PENDING ROAD PROJECTS. \n \nDetails of Funds allocated for development and maintenance of NHs in 2016-17 \n \nSl. No. State Development Maintenance \nAmount (Cr) Amount (Cr) \n1 A & N Islands 30 4.33 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 800 93.8 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 20 23.16 \n4 Assam 290 94.95 \n5 Bihar 500 63.39 \n6 Chandigarh 2 1.64 \n7 Chhattisgarh 360 50 \n8 D & N Haveli 0 0.14 \n9 Daman & Diu 0 0.09 \n10 Delhi 2 1.4 \n11 Goa 1,400.00 39.96 \n12 Gujarat 280 152.74 \n13 Haryana 150 53.32 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 250 81.92 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 56 15.99 \n16 Jharkhand 220 48.25 \n17 Karnataka 770 149.34 \n18 Kerala 250 107.13 \n19 Madhya Pradesh 425 24.35 \n20 Maharashtra 2,000.00 263.75 \n21 Manipur 60 23.38 \n22 Meghalaya 60 120.86 \n23 Mizoram 30 106.96 \n24 Nagaland 50 48.44 \n25 Odisha 730 82.79 \n26 Puducherry 20 1.56 \n27 Punjab 420 62.72 \n28 Rajasthan 700 86.26 \n29 Sikkim 0 1.99 \n30 Tamil Nadu 430 100.79 \n31 Telangana 300 77.12 \n32 Tripura 65 47.72 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 1,576.00 129.38 \n35 Uttarakhand 470 42 \n36 West Bengal 1,060.00 73.74 \n37 NHAI  19,653.00 100 \n38 BRO 0 100 \n39 SARDP-NE    5,000.00 50 \n40 LWE  1,000.00 0 \n41 EAP  1,290.00 0 \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |757", "t": "Decline in Handloom Weavers", "head": "LOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.757 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nDECLINE IN HANDLOOM WEAVERS \n \n757. SHRI LAKHAN LAL SAHU: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there has been steady decline in the number of weavers engaged in the \nhandloom industries; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof for the last two years and the current year, State-wise; \n \n(c)  whether the Government is contemplating to prepare a long term policy to solve \nthe said problem; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंरी (                       ) \nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes Madam.  So far, three handloom censuses have been conducted by the \nMinistry of Textiles which indicates that there has been decline in the number of weavers \nengaged in the handloom industries.  As per first handloom census (1987 -88), there were \n67.39 lakh handloom workers across the country which was reduced to 65.50 lakh during \nsecond handloom census (1995 -96).  Further, these declined to 43.31 lakh, during third \nhandloom census (2009 -10).   State -wise statement is annexed.   However, share of                       \nfull-time weavers to total weavers increased to 64% from 44% and share of idle looms \ndecreased to 4% from 10% in third handloom census as compared to second handlooms \ncensus.  \n \n(c) & (d): To Government has made a strategy to promote productio n and marketing \nof high value good quality handloom products for increasing earnings of weavers.  To \nimplement this strategy, the following schemes are under implementation:- \n \n(i) National Handloom Development Programme (NHDP) \n(ii) Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Scheme (CHCDS) \n(iii)Yarn Supply Scheme \n(iv) Handloom Weavers Comprehensive Welfare Scheme. \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nStatus of Total Handloom workers as per 1st, 2nd and 3rd   Handloom Census \n      S. \nNo. State Name \nNos. of Total \nworkers (1987-88) \nNos. of Total \nworkers (1995-96) \nNos. of Total \nworkers (2009-10) \n 1 Andhra Pradesh   469,086                   490,616  355,838 \n 2 Arunachal Pradesh 47,400                     53,473  33,041 \n 3 Assam 2,079,238                2,322,268  1,643,453 \n 4 Bihar 241,298                   167,707  43,392 \n 5 Chhattisgarh     8,191 \n 6 Delhi 19,727                       6,708  2,738 \n 7 Goa 109                            25  0 \n 8 Gujarat  69,320                     57,936  11,009 \n 9 Haryana 37,640                     22,810  7,967 \n 10 Himachal Pradesh 55,387                     65,099  13,458 \n 11 Jammu and Kashmir 53,503                     51,847  33,209 \n 12 Jharkhand     21,160 \n 13 Karnataka 188,749                   177,562  89,256 \n 14 Kerala  78,595                     63,153  14,679 \n 15 Madhya Pradesh 79,473                     56,106  14,761 \n 16 Maharashtra 147,478                     80,901  3,418 \n 17 Manipur 336,954                   462,087  218,753 \n 18 Meghalaya     13,612 \n 19 Mizoram     43,528 \n 20 Nagaland 147,617                   126,228  66,490 \n 21 Orissa 247,071                   246,782  114,106 \n 22 Pondicherry 9,207                       7,369  2,803 \n 23 Punjab 24,423                     13,160  2,636 \n 24 Rajasthan 84,769                     71,915  31,958 \n 25 Sikkim      568 \n 26 Tamil Nadu 758,722                   607,675  352,321 \n 27 Tripura 138,886                   291,761  137,177 \n 28 Uttar Pradesh 657,987                   420,684  257,783 \n 29 Uttarakhand     15,468 \n 30 West Bengal 766,808                   686,254  779,103 \n   Total  6,739,447                6,550,126                  4,331,876  \n \n      The table (1995-96) excludes 3 States viz. Meghalaya, Mizoram, Sikkim & 3 new States \nChhattisgarh, Uttrakhand, Jharkhand. \n \n \n  \n****** \n  \n  \n      "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |758", "t": "Inter Linking of Rivers of South India", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 758 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nINTER LINKING OF RIVERS OF SOUTH INDIA \n758.       SHRI K. PARASURAMAN \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA REJUVENATION  be \npleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government had proposed linking of rivers of southern part of the country, if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has als o constituted a committee to draw modus operandi  for linking rivers in \nSouthern part of the country, if so, the details thereof and present status of this proposal;  \n(c) whether there is any financial constraint being faced by the Government for implementa tion of this project; \nand  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA REJUVENATION \n(SUSHRI UMA BHARTI) \n \n(a) Under the National Perspective Plan (NPP) for water resources development through inter basin transfer of \nwater prepared by this Ministry, NWDA has identified 30 links (16 under Peninsular Component & 14 under \nHimalayan Component) for preparation of Fe asibility Reports. The pre -feasibility report of the all 30 links have \nbeen prepared and circulated to the concerned State Govts. by the NWDA. After survey and investigations \nFeasibility Reports of 14 links under Peninsular Component in southern part of country have been completed, as \nper details in the Annexure. \n \n(b) to (d) The Government is pursuing the Interlinking of Rivers program in a consultative manner. Its \nimplementation is dependent upon the consensus and co-operation of the concerned States and upon obtaining \nvarious mandatory clearances from Ministry/ Departments of Central and State Governments including statutory \nclearances. In order to speed up the implementation of Inter- Linking of River Programme and to arrive at a \nconsensus on the link pro posals between the States, a ‘Special Committee for Interlinking of Rivers’, has been \nconstituted by this  Ministry in September, 2014. Nine meetings of the Special Committee for Interlinking of \nRiver (ILR) have been held so far, wherein State Irrigation/ Water Resources Ministers along with the Secretaries \nof various States attended the meetings. The Committee after considering the views of all the stakeholders is \ntaking all necessary steps for expediting the objectives of interlinking rivers programme as per terms of reference \nof the Committee. \nFor providing further impetus and to arrive at a consensus on ILR proposals between the States, a “Task Force \nand various Sub-Committees for Interlinking of Rivers” have also been constituted.  The requirement of funds for \nthis initiative is substantial.  Therefore, varied sources of funds will have to be tapped for implementation of ILR \nProjects. Currently, no financial constraints are envisaged in the implementation of ILR Projects.  \n \n***** \n\nANNEXURE \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Unstarred Question No. 758 due for answer in LokSabha on \n21.07.2016 regarding Inter Linking of Rivers of South India. \n \nSl. \nNo \nName States concerned States benefited Status \nPeninsular  Component \n1 Mahanadi (Manibhadra) - \nGodavari (Dowlaiswaram) link  \nOrissa, Maharashtra,  \nAndhra Pradesh, Karnataka, \n& Chattisgarh,  \nAndhra Pradesh& \nOrissa \nFeasibility Report \nCompleted \n2 Godavari (Inchampalli) - \nKrishna (Pulichintala) link \nOrissa, Maharashtra, \nMadhya P radesh, Andhra \nPradesh, Telangana, \nKarnataka & Chattisgarh, \nTelangana& \nAndhra Pradesh \nFeasibility Report \nCompleted \n3 Godavari (Inchampalli) - \nKrishna (Nagarjunasagar) link \n-do- Telangana Feasibility Report \nCompleted \n4 Godavari (Polavaram) - Krishna \n(Vijayawada) link  \nOrissa, Maharashtra,  \nAndhra Pradesh, Karnataka, \n& Chattisgarh, \nAndhra Pradesh Feasibility Report \nCompleted \n5 Krishna (Almatti) – Pennar link -do- Andhra Pradesh& \nKarnataka \nFeasibility Report \nCompleted \n6 Krishna (Srisailam) – Pennar \nlink \n-do- -- Feasibility Report \nCompleted \n7 Krishna (Nagarjunasagar) - \nPennar (Somasila ) link \nMaharashtra,  Andhra \nPradesh& Karnataka, \n-do- Feasibility Report \nCompleted \n8 Pennar (Somasila) - Cauvery \n(Grand Anicut) link  \nAndhra Pradesh, Karnataka, \nTamil Nadu, Kerala \n&Puducherry \nAndhra Pradesh, \nTamil Nadu \n&Puducherry \nFeasibility Report \nCompleted \n \n \n9  Cauvery (Kattalai) - Vaigai -\nGundar link \nKarnataka, Tamil Nadu, \nKerala &Puducherry \nTamil Nadu Feasibility Report \nCompleted \n10 Ken-Betwa link  \na)  Ken-Betwa Link Phase-I \n \nb) Ken-Betwa link  Phase-II \n \nUttar Pradesh  & Madhya \nPradesh  \n \n          - do- \nUttar Pradesh  \n&Madhya Pradesh \n \nMadhya Pradesh \nDPR Phase-I \ncompleted in April \n2010& DPR Phase-\nII Completed in \nJanuary 2014.  \n11 Parbati -Kalisindh- - Chambal \nlink  \nMadhya Pradesh, Rajasthan \n& Uttar Pradesh (UP  \nrequested to be consulted \nduring consensus building ) \nMadhya Pradesh& \nRajasthan \nFeasibility Report \nCompleted \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |76", "t": "UGC Regulations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 76 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2016 \n \nUGC Regulations \n \n76. SHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK: \n      DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT: \n      SHRI SATAV RAJEEV: \n      SHRI V. ELUMALAI: \n      SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n      SHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO: \n      DR. J. JAYAVARDHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fa ct that the University Grants Comm ission (UGC) has revised/proposes \nto revise its rules on twinning arrangement of foreign educational institutions/universities \nwith their Indian counterparts; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to  bring in transparency and \nsimplification in this regard; and \n \n(d) whether it is also a fact that Government has announced a scheme for State universities \nfor which professors from universities abroad will be invited to spend a year to help them \nimprove curriculum pedagogy and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n(a) to (c): Yes, Madam. The University Grants Commission (UGC) has notified the UGC \n(Promotion and Maintenance of Standards of Academic Collaboration between Indian and \nForeign Educational Institutions) Regulati ons, 2016 with a view to increasing synergy \nbetween Indian and foreign academic instituti ons, to offer students additional choices, \nimprove curriculum and the delivery of knowle dge and educational content. The regulations \nare in supersession of UG C(Promotion and Maintenance of Standards of Academic \nCollaboration between Indian and Foreign Educational Institutions) Regulations, 2012.  \n As per the new regulations , Indian Universities and Colleges, having the highest grade \nof accreditation/ threshold accreditation, will now be able to apply online to the University \nGrants Commission (UGC) for starting t winning arrangement wit h quality Foreign \nEducational Institutions (F EIs) in undergraduate and postgr aduate programmes leading to \n\nthe award of a degree. The degr ee will be awarded by the Indian Educational Institutions \n(IEIs) alone; joint degrees ar e not permitted. The regulatio ns provide for time-bound \nprocessing of the on line applications by the UGC.  T he regulations are available at \nwww.egazette.nic.in / www.ugc.ac.in .  \n \n(d):  It has been announced on 3\nrd June, 2016 by the Govern ment that the Centrally \nSponsored Scheme (CSS) of Rashtriya Uchchat ar Shiksha Abhiyan (RUSA) will provide \nsupport for engagement of academics from l eading foreign Universities with State \nUniversities for curriculum and pedagogical reforms. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |761", "t": "Status of Watershed Programmes/ Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF LAND RESOURCES \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 761 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \n Status of Watershed Programmes/ Projects \n \n761. SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA:  \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether small watershed programme is not being utilised presently in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor;  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government for optimum utilisation of the programme; and  \n(d) the details of achievements or success by the Government in this regard, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)  \n  \n(a) & (b) :     Since 1995 -96, t he area development programmes on watershed basis were \nimplemented under various schemes such as the Drought Prone Areas Programme (DPAP),  the \nDesert Development Programme (DDP) and the Integrated Wastelands Development Programme \n(IWDP). In 2008-09, these programmes were merged into a single programme called the Integrated \nWatershed Management Programme (IWMP) for the development of rainfed / degraded areas. \nDuring 2015 -16, the IWMP was amalgamated as the Watershed Development Component of the \nPradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY).  Overall, the small watershed programmes have \nbenefitted the farmers.  \n \nIt may be pointed out that s ome projects taken up under the area development programmes of \nDDP, IWDP and DPAP sanctioned during the period 1995-96 to 2006-07, were foreclosed due to the \nlack of institutional mechanism for implementing the programme in the States and Districts coupled \nwith insufficient community awareness and participation.  \n \n(c) :     The Common Guidelines for Watershed Development Proj ects 2008 (Revised Edition 2011) \nprovide for  various mechanisms to ensure proper implementation , such as  regular monitoring \nincluding GIS / web based online monitoring of process and outcomes, social auditing and \nindependent and external monitoring by thir d party agencies and dedicated institutions with \nprofessional support at the State, District and project levels. In addition, the Department of Land \nResources supervises the work through periodic review meetings, a Management Information \nSystem (MIS), progress reports and third party monitoring.  \n \n(d) :   A number of impact assessment studies have been conducted through the National Institute of \nRural Development(NIRD), The Energy Resources Institute (TERI) and International Crops \nResearch Institute for the Semi-Arid Tropics (ICRISAT). The major findings of these studies include \na rise in the ground water table, increased availability of drinking water, bringing additional areas \nunder irrigation & cultivable wastelands, improved cropping intensity and reduct ion of soil erosion \netc. in the project areas. The information regarding State/ UT - wise achievements has not been \ncollated. The State/ UT - wise details of the projects undertaken under the Watershed Development \nComponent of PMKSY (erstwhile IWMP) are give n in the Annexure. These projects are at various \nstages of implementation. \n***** \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (d) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 761 \ndue for reply on 21.07.2016 \nState wise details of projects and area sanctioned under PMKSY-Watershed Development Component \n \nS.No. Name of the State Total  No of Projects \nSanctioned since \n2009 \nArea of  Projects  \nSanctioned  in \nmillion ha \n1 Andhra Pradesh 432 1.81 \n2 Bihar 123 0.612 \n3 Chhattisgarh 263 1.195 \n4 Gujarat 610 3.103 \n5 Haryana 88 0.362 \n6 Himachal Pradesh 163 0.84 \n7 Jammu & Kashmir 159 0.652 \n8 Jharkhand 171 0.911 \n9 Karnataka 571 2.569 \n10 Kerala 83 0.423 \n11 Madhya Pradesh 517 2.937 \n12 Maharashtra 1186 5.128 \n13 Odisha 310 1.70 \n14 Punjab 67 0.314 \n15 Rajasthan 1025 5.764 \n16 Tamil Nadu 270 1.368 \n17 Telangana 330 1.399 \n18 Uttar Pradesh 612 3.046 \n19 Uttarakhand 65 0.346 \n20 West Bengal 163 0.693 \n North-Eastern States   \n21 Arunachal Pradesh 156 0.467 \n22 Assam 372 1.577 \n23 Manipur 102 0.491 \n24 Meghalaya 96 0.236 \n25 Mizoram 89 0.373 \n26 Nagaland 111 0.476 \n27 Sikkim 15 0.066 \n28 Tripura 65 0.213 \n Grand Total 8214 39.07 \n \n  Note: 1) The programme is not operational in Union Territories. \n2) There is no sanctioned project in Goa. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |764", "t": "Ground Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 764 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nGROUND WATER \n 764.        SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government proposes to treat the ground water as a National property;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether there is any law in place to regulate the use of ground water;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) if not, whether there is any proposal to bring any law this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT  \nAND GANGA  REJUVENATION  \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a) & (b) Government of  India under National Water  Policy, 2012 has laid down certain  \nbasic principle to govern public policies on water resources.  As per these basic principles, \n\"water needs to be managed as a common pool community resource held by the State, under \npublic trust doctrine, to achieve food security, support livelihood, and ensure equitable and \nsustainable development for all.\" \n \n(c)  to  (e)  Water  being  a  state  subject,  it  is  primarily  the  responsibility  of  the  \nconcerned  State Governments  to make suitable legislation to regulate utilization of ground \nwater. \n \nCentral Ground Water Authority (CGWA) has been constituted under 'The Environment \n(Protection) Act, 1986' for the purpose of regulation and control of ground water \ndevelopment and management in the Country. CGWA is regulating ground water withdrawal \nby industries/infrastructure/mining projects in 'Non-Notified' areas for which guidelines/criteria \nhave been framed and circulated in public domain. \n \nFurther, Ministry of Water Resources, RD & GR has circulated a Model Bill to all the \nStates/UTs to enable them to enact  suitable  ground  water legislation  for its regulation  and \ndevelopment  which includes provision of rain water harvesting. So far, 15 States/UTs have \nadopted and implemented the ground water legislation on the lines of Model Bill. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |765", "t": "Poverty Reduction under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.765\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nPOVERTY REDUCTION UNDER MGNREGS\n765. SHRI VENKATESH BABU T.G.:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of estimated percentage of rural men and women working under\nMGNREGS, State/UT-wise;\n(b) whether the Government is aware that as per the study by the Indian Human\nDevelopment Survey (IHDS), MGNREGS has contribute d to almost a third of the\npoverty reduction in the country;\n(c) if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether any steps are being taken by the Government to streamline and strengthen the\nMGNREGS to include more poor people who are deprived of the benefits of the Act;\nand\n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): The estimated percentage of rural men and women working under Mahatma Gandhi\nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) is not maintained in the\nManagement Information System (NREGASoft).\n(b)&(c): Yes, Madam. The Government is aware of the National Council of Applied\nEconomic Research (sponsored by Indian Human Development Survey) study report that\nestimates the poverty reduction due to Mahatma Gandhi NREGA.\nThe Report on 'Poverty reduction due to MGNREGA’ states that:\n• MGNREGA’s contribution to reducing poverty is about 32%. In the absence of\nMGNREGA-induced consumption, poverty among the participants would have been 38.0%\nin 2011–12, not 31.3%.\n• MGNREGA prevented 14 million persons from falling into poverty (those non -poor\nin 2004–05 who would have become poor by 2011–12 without MGNREGA employment).\n• In spite of a high initial poverty rate (75.8% in 2004–05), poverty among adivasis was\nreduced by 27.6% and for dalits by 37.6%.\n• MGNREGA is more effective in poverty reduction in less developed areas (34%) than\nin more developed areas (27%)\n• Low-participating areas experienced much greater poverty reduction (72%) than areas\nwith a high participation rate (27%).\n\n(d)&(e): To generate awareness about the provisions of the Scheme and to provide\nadequate employment opportunities to rural households including poor people\nunder MGNREGA, all States/UTs have been requested for the following: -\n(i) Initiate appropriate IEC campaigns including wall paintings for wide\ndissemination of the provisions of the Act.\n(ii) Expand scope and coverage of demand registration system to ensure that\ndemand for work under MGNREGA does not go unregistered.\n(iii) Organize ‘Rozgar Diwas’ periodically to capture latent demand under the\nprogramme and to disseminate awareness about the provisions of the Act.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |766", "t": "Six Laning of NH 8", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 766 \nANSWERED ON 21TH JULY, 2016 \n \nSIX LANING OF NH 8 \n \n \n766. SHRI PARBHUBHAI NAGARBHAI VASAVA: \n            \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether construction work of approximately 16 km long stretch of National Highway \n(NH) 8 between Kim Chauraha to Chalthan is pending; \n \n(b) whether tender was invited for the construction of this stretch of National Highway \nalong with the reasons for the cancellation of the tender; and \n \n(c) the time by which the construction work of over bridge and underpass at four places of \nthe six lane NH is likely to be started? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a)to(c) Yes Madam. 6-laning of Kim Chauraha to kamrej is already completed. The tender for \n Six-laning of kamrej -Chalthan Section of NH -8 on EPC Mode was first invited on \n 18.06.2015 which was later on cancelled on 30.12.2015 by NHAI due to non -approval \n of project by Competent Authority at that time. Now, after completion of due process, \n the Contract Agreement for 6 -laning of Kamrej to Chalthan has been executed on \n 14.06.2016.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |767", "t": "Payment of Performance Related Payment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.767 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nPayment of Performance Related Payment \n \n \n767.  SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY: \n \n Will the Minister of COAL be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government had decided to make performance related payment \nw.e.f.January,2007 to the officers of Coal India Limited (CIL) and its subsidiaries;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the officers of the CIL and its subsidiaries are being gi ven salary as per the \nsaid standards and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the corrective measures proposed to  be taken by the \nGovernment in this regard? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL, \nPOWER, MINES AND NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) & (b): The Government has conveyed on 26.10.2015 its approval to the CIL’s proposal for \npayment of Performance Related Pay (PRP) to executives of CIL and its subsidiaries with effect \nfrom 01.01.2007. CIL has informed that PRP has been paid to the executives of CIL and its \nsubsidiaries for the years 2007-08 to 2013-14.   \n \n(c)  & (d): Officers of CIL and its subsidiaries are given salary as per their pay scales, which are \nas per Department of Public Enterprises’s orders/guidelines.   \n \n***** \n  \n\n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |768", "t": "Quality of Coal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 768 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \nQuality of Coal \n \n768. SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR \n \nWill the MINISTER OF COAL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any plan to maintain the quality of coal produced in \nthe country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether his Ministry has recently signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) \nwith other Ministries regarding the quality of coal; \n(d) if so, the details and salient features thereof; and \n(e) the time by which the work is likely to be started in this regard? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINSITER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL, POWER,  NEW \nAND RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a)& (b)  The measures taken by CIL to maintain quality of coal produced from its mines \ninclude the following:  \ni.    Deployment of more Surface Miners to facilitate selective mining. \nii. Fifteen washeries  have been planned for installation with a capacity of 112.60 \nMillion tons \niii.    Selective mining of bands of more than one meter thickness. \niv.   Appropriate positioning of OB and coal benches to avoid contamination. \nv.   Scrapping/cleaning of coal benches before blasting. \n vi.      Efforts are being made to increase underground production where high grade coal is  \navailable. \n \n \n(c)& (d) MOU on third party sampling (TPS) for coal quality monitoring at loading end at CIL \nsubsidiaries & SCCL for dispatch to Power Utili ties was signed between (i)COAL \nINDIA, NTPC and CSIR -CIMFR & (ii) COAL INDIA,ASSOCIATION OF POWER \nPRODUCER AND CSIR-CIMFR on 28.06.2016. \n \nThe salient features of MOU in brief are as under- \n \n Independent Third Party Agency to be appointed by Central Institut e for Mining & \nFuel Research (CIMFR) by a transparent process for undertaking the work of \n\nsampling & analysis of coal at the loading end on behalf of both the power plant \n(consumer) and the coal companies (supplier).  \n CSIR-CIMFR, Dhanbad will be wholly res ponsible for collection, preparation and \nanalysis of coal supplied to various Power Utilities (PUs) at loading end in context of \nFuel Supply Agreement, MOU, Forward and Special Forward e -auctions for Power \nconsumers etc.  \n Quantity to be sampled is approxim ately 150 million Metric Tonne per year which \nwill be increased in phased manner.  \n MOU valid w.e.f. 1 st January 2016. It shall remain in force for a period of 10 years \nwith provision of extension for another 5 years.    \n CIMFR shall enlist Technical Service  Provider (TSP) through transparent process for \ncollection, preparation and transportation of coal samples from loading points to \nCSIR-CIMFR. . \n Authorized representatives of power plant and Coal Company shall jointly witness \nthe process of sample collection & preparation of laboratory samples and put their \nsignature on sample tags in evidence of the process of sampling. All three - \nindependent sampler, consumer and supplier; shall also sign on the samples register \nmaintained by the coal company at the loading end.  \n The fee for independent sampling agency shall be equally borne by both the coal \ncompany (supplier) and the power utility (consumer).  \n CSIR-CIMFR shall communicate Analysis Results within 18 (eighteen) working days of \nthe sample collection to the coal company and the power plant who  may raise \ndispute, if any, within 07(seven) days of the submission such results.    \nIn case a dispute arises within the stipulated time period, the Referee sample shall be \nanalyzed at mutually agreed government laboratory and results will be binding on both Coal \nCompany and the Power Utility. \n \n(e)  CIMFR has started sampling work at some loading points of subsid iary coal companies \nw.e.f. 01.01.2016 in a phased manner. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |769", "t": "Assessment of SAGY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.769\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nASSESSMENT OF SAGY\n769. SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any assessment of implementation of Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY)\nhas been made by the Government, if so, the details of the achievements, State/UT -\nwise;\n(b) whether any expert agencies have been appointed in villages adopted under the SAGY\nfor conducting a baseline survey under Guideline No. 10(1) of the scheme;\n(c) if so, the details of total number of agencies appointed, State/UT -wise along with the\nname of the agencies involved therein;\n(d) if not, the details of functionaries conducting baseline surveys for the preparation of\nVillage Development Plan in villages adopted by MPs; and\n(e) the total number of villages to be developed under SAGY and the time -frame for the\nsame?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) : While the activities for development of the first set of Adarsh Grams are going\non, the Ministry of Rural Development have documented one hundred and one good\ninitiatives in the Adarsh Grams on the initiatives taken up by Hon’ble  Members of\nParliament(MPs) and brought out in the form of “SANKALAN”. The purpose is to share the\nbest practi ces in the SAGY gram panchayats and widely disseminate the stories that will\ninspire other MPs and Adarsh Grams to initiate similar activities. A link to “SAGY -\nSankalan” is available on the website of the Scheme www.saanjhi.gov.in under the heading\n“Good Practices”. State -wise details of some of the other success stories reported from\nSAGY Gram Panchayats are at Annexure I;\n(b) : As per the Guidelines of Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana(SAGY), the District\nCollector is the nodal officer for implementing SAGY. One of the specific responsibility\ngiven to him under the Scheme Guidelines  is the conduct of the baseline survey. The\ntimelines of conduct of baseline survey and other activities under planning are monitored by\nthe Mi nistry of Rural Development, however, the choice of agencies or persons through\nwhich it is to be conducted is that of the District Collector. Hence, no expert agencies have\nbeen appointed by the Ministry in villages adopted under SAGY for conducting a bas eline\nsurvey;\n(c) : Does not arise in view of reply in (b);\n\n(d) : In view of reply in (b), the specific information asked for is not maintained in\nthe Ministry of Rural Development. However, as per the information of the Ministry, the\ndistrict officials under the nodal officer viz,. the District Collector and members of the Civil\nSociety Organizations who are experts in the field are involved in conducting baseline\nsurveys for the preparation of Village Development Plan in villages adopted by MPs.\n(e) : The goal of SAGY is that every Member of Parliament should   develop thr ee\nAdarsh Grams by March 2019, of which one would be achieved by 2016. Thereafter, five\nsuch Adarsh Grams (one per year) will be selected and developed by 2024.\n*****\n\nAnnexure I  referred to in reply to part (a) of  Lok  Sabha Unstarred Question\nNo. 769 due for answer on 21.07.2016.\nSr.\nNo\nName of the State Success Story domain\n1 Jharkhand Skill Training to Youth, Sanitation,  and Animal\nHusbandry\n2 Kerala Organic Farming\nSpecial medical camp for Scheduled tribe  people\n3 Meghalaya Child Labour Free,Community Leadership programme\nand  Sanitation\n4 Maharashtra Soil and Water Conservation activities, Training to\nMasons, Construction of mud road through\n‘shramdan’, Construction of recharge wells, Farm\nRoad Facilities in village market, Public infrastructure\n- Construction of Village road and starting a bus\nservice,  Renovation of school building, Distribution of\nschool bags in primary schools and anganwadis and\nLED lamps for internal village road\n5 Karnataka Free Solar Lantern Distribution to the artisans\n6 Punjab SAGY Village to be Open Defecation Free\n7 Madhya Pradesh Liquor preparation stopped and reduction in liquor\nconsumption\n8 Gujarat Education, Sanitation and observing YOGA day in the\nvillage to make villagers more health conscious\n9 Uttar Pradesh Agriculture Marketing\n10 Chhattisgarh Well equipped Anganwadi renovated from local\nfinancial support"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |77", "t": "Inclusion of Yoga in Physiotherapy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 77 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2016 \n \nInclusion of Yoga in Physiotherapy \n \n77. SHRI S.R. VIJAYAKUMAR:  \nKUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH: \nDR. SUNIL BALIRAM GAIKWAD: \nSHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:  \nSHRI T. RADHAKRISHNAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) whether the University Grants Commission (U GC) has written to all universities that \nyoga teaching may be included in bac helors and masters programmes of \nPhysiotherapy;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the response of the university thereto;  \n(c) the number of Central Universities identified to implement the yoga education \nprogramme, till date;  \n(d) whether the Government has also ask ed the UGC to constitute a committee for \ncreation of good quality text books and manuals for the syllabus; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the time  by which the committee is likely to be \nconstituted? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. The University Gr ants Commission (UGC) has  informed that it \nhas written to the Vice-Chancellors of all Universities on 19\nth May, 2016 for including \nYoga syllabus in Bachelors of  Physiotherapy (BPT) and Masters of Physiotherapy \n(MPT) in the 2016-17 academic session. The template of the syllabus for BPT and MPT \nas designed by the Ministry of AYUS H is available on UGC website at \nhttp://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/1229406_UGC-Letter-Yoga.pdf. \n \n(c): Six Central Universities  will have functional  Yoga Departments from the academic \nsession 2016-17.  \n \n(d) & (e): The UGC has been requested to constitute a committee of subject matter \nexperts in consultation with Ministry of AYU SH for the creation of  good quality text \nbooks and manuals for the syllabus pr epared for B.Sc., M.Sc., Ph.D and \ndiploma/advanced diploma courses in Yoga. \n****** \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |770", "t": "Funds to NHAI", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.770  \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2016 \n \nFUNDS TO NHAI  \n770.  SHRI G. HARI: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government has approved a plan for National Highways Authority \nof India (NHAI)to raise upto Rs. 55000 crore during this fiscal;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the mechanism evolved for the purpose; \n(c)  whether the   Government has also approved NHAI to keep the proceeds of \ncompleted highway projects that will be sold out for a healthier balance sheet; \nand  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE \nIN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a)&(b)  Yes, Madam.  As per budgetary approval for the current year, Internal Extra \nBudgetary Resources (IEBR) amounting to Rs.59,279 crore has been allotted to NHAI.  \nNHAI has been already authorized to raise Rs.25,000 crore (taxable) and Rs.5,000 crore \n(54EC) for construction and maintenance of National Highways.   \n \n(c)&(d)  A proposal for monetization of completed public funded national highway \nprojects through Toll -Operate Transfer mode based on the expected collection of user \nfee receivables, through private sector efficiency and expertise is under active \nconsideration.  Proceeds from such monetization of completed national highway \nprojects shall be utilized for construction, operation and maintenance of national \nhighways.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |771", "t": "Houses for BPL", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.771\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nHOUSES FOR BPL\n771. SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has set any plan for providing financial assistance for\nconstruction of residential houses to the persons living below poverty line in rural\nareas of the country; and\n(b) if so, the details of residential units cons tructed during the last year and the current\nyear, State/UT-wise?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) : Under Indira Awaas Yojana (IAY), financial assistance was provided to the rural\nBPL household for construction of dwelling unit. IAY has been restructured into Pradhan\nMantri Awaas Yojana (Gramin) in which financial assistance is provided for construction of\nhouse to the beneficiaries identified under Socio Economic Caste Census (SECC) 2011.\n(b) : A statement indicating no. of houses constructed under PMAY -G (previously IAY)\nduring last year and current year is given in the Annexure-I.\n*****\n\nAnnexure-I\nAnnexure referred to in Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 771,part (b)  due for\nanswer on 21.7.2016 asked by Shri Bhagwanth Khubha regarding Houses for BPL.\nHouses constructed under PMAY-G (previously IAY) during the last  years and\ncurrent year\nUnits in Nos.\nS.No. State 2015-16 2016-17\n1 ANDHRA PRDESH 27491 3706\n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH. 1 0\n3 ASSAM 69785 21930\n4 BIHAR 273729 49370\n5 CHHATTISGARH 24061 2411\n6 GOA 18 7\n7 GUJARAT 40298 6173\n8 HARYANA 12959 1620\n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 3062 383\n10 J&K 1989 12\n11 JHARKHAND 25218 4393\n12 KARNATAKA 155627 842\n13 KERALA 50994 8318\n14 MADHYA PRAD. 12512 51629\n15 MAHARASHTRA 121934 6283\n16 MANIPUR 96 1\n17 MEGHALAYA 824 58\n18 MIZORAM 378 11\n19 NAGALAND 633 1\n20 ORISSA 275398 35018\n21 PUNJAB 0 0\n22 RAJASTHAN 64787 10085\n23 SIKKIM 211 54\n24 TAMIL NADU 28149 8630\n25 TELANGANA 84242 0\n26 TRIPURA 6112 848\n27 UTTAR PRAD. 155553 81161\n28 UTTARAKHAND 7723 455\n29 WEST BENGAL 353399 90355\n30 A &.N ISLANDS 0 0\n31 DADRA & NAGAR HAVELI 0 0\n32 DAMAN & DIU 0 0\n33 LAKSHADWEEP 0 0\n34 PUDUCHERRY 0 0\nTOTAL 1797183 383754\nAs reported by the States/UTs on AwaasSoft on 18.7.2016"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |772", "t": "Delay in Wage Payment under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.772\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nDELAY IN WAGE PAYMENT UNDER MGNREGS\n772. SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the fact that at some places the maoist threat is\nbeing given to panchayat leaders for delays in payment of wages to Mahatma Gandhi\nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) workers;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and action taken by the Government thereon and reasons for\ndelaying wages to workers, including details for the percentage of MGNREGS\nworkers who were paid within 15 days, State/UT-wise;\n(c) whether female workers ar e facing more delays in wage payments as compared to\nmale workers, if so, the details thereof and reasons therefor, State/UT-wise; and\n(d) the steps being taken by the Government/mechanism being evolved by Government\nfor the redressal of the issue of the wage delays to MGNREGS workers especially in\ndrought-affected areas of the country, State/UT-wise including Uttar Pradesh?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) & (b): No instance of Maoist threat being given to Panchayat leaders for delaying\nwage payment of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme\n(MGNREGS) workers has been reported to the Ministry of Rural Development.\nState/UT-wise details of the per centage of MGNREGS workers who were paid wages within\n15 days are given in Annexure.\n(c): No instance which indiciates that female workers are facing more delays in wage\npayments as compared to male workers has been reported to the Ministry of Rural\nDevelopment.\n(d): In order to ensure timely payment wages, Rules have been issued for timely payment\nof wages under which, it was suggested that clear accountability shall be fixed on the\nfunctionaries for timely performance of specific duties leading to payment of wages.\nFor ensuring timely payment of wages, the Central Government has also started National\nElectronic Fund Management System (NeFMS) in 11 States (Assam, Bihar, Haryana,\nKarnataka, Kerala Odisha Punjab, Rajasthan, Tripura, Uttar Pradesh and Uttrakhand) and\nlaunched Dashboard for tracking of pending Fund Transfer Orders, Public Fund Management\nSystem, electronic Fund Management System, Direct Benefit Transfer etc.\n*****\n\nAnnexure referred in repl y to parts (a)&(b) of Lok\nSabha Unstarred Question No. 772 dated 21.07.2016\nNo. States\n%age of wage payment\npaid within 15\ndays(2016-17 as on\n15/7/2016)\n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 95.77\n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH NR\n3 ASSAM 63.89\n4 BIHAR 28.62\n5 CHHATTISGARH 17.55\n6 GOA 43.06\n7 GUJARAT 45.20\n8 HARYANA 62.85\n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 31.32\n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 22.90\n11 JHARKHAND 78.09\n12 KARNATAKA 54.62\n13 KERALA 71.08\n14 MADHYA PRADESH 41.78\n15 MAHARASHTRA 46.56\n16 MANIPUR 78.81\n17 MEGHALAYA 45.46\n18 MIZORAM 99.98\n19 NAGALAND NR\n20 ODISHA 44.28\n21 PUNJAB 51.11\n22 RAJASTHAN 81.47\n23 SIKKIM 78.25\n24 TAMIL NADU 19.95\n25 TELANGANA 91.32\n26 TRIPURA 72.21\n27 UTTAR PRADESH 37.10\n28 UTTARAKHAND 68.61\n29 WEST BENGAL 63.91\n30 ANDAMAN & NICOBAR NR\n31 DADRA & NAGAR HAVELI NR\n32 DAMAN & DIU NR\n33 LAKSHADWEEP NR\n34 PUDUCHERRY 16.80\nNational Average 55.81\nNR=Not Reported"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |773", "t": "Banking Facilities under SAGY", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.773\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nBANKING FACILITIES UNDER SAGY\n773. KUNWAR SARVESH KUMAR:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has announced to open branches of nationalised banks with\nATM facility under Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY);\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the number of branches of banks opened under this\nscheme in the country, State/UT-wise and bank-wise including Uttar Pradesh; and\n(c) the other steps being taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) : Yes Madam.\n(b) & (c): In order to ensure implementation of Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana\n(PMJDY), the following instructions have been issued on 12.12.2014 to Banks by Ministry\nof Finance, Department of Financial Services:-\ni. Providing of banking services in each “Saansad Adarsh Gram” through bank\nbranch or through a fixed point on line inter operable BC/Kiosk;\nii. Close monitoring of implementation of PMJDY in “Saansad Adarsh Gram”.\niii. Allocation of targets to link branch of “Saansad Adarsh Gram” on various\nparameters like every adult having a bank account, issuance of RuPay card,\nsaturation of Kisan Credit Card(KCC) in the village, encouraging Self Help\nGroups/Joint Liability Groups movement & Kisan Club etc. and monitoring\nthereof.\nAs regards, the number of branches of banks opened under this scheme in the country,\nState/UT-wise and bank -wise including Uttar Pradesh, the requisite information is being\ncollected from the States/UTs and will be laid on the table of the House.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |774", "t": "Euro 6 Standard", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.774 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21st July, 2016 \n                        \nEURO 6 STANDARD \n \n774. SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a) whether the Union Government is contemplating to introduce Euro -6 standard \nthroughout the country w.e.f. 1 April 2020 to bring down the level of carbon emission; \nand  \n (b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI P. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to  (b) Government has issued draf t notification number G.S.R. 187 (E) dated 19th       \nFebruary 2016, mandating  mass emission standard for BS -VI throughout the country \nw.e.f. 1st April 2020 to bring down emission. \n                                                                             \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |775", "t": "Flaws in Drinking Water Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 775 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nFlaws in Drinking Water Projects \n \n775.  SHRI J.J.T. NATTERJEE  \nSHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA \n \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) Whether various kinds of flaws/irregularities have been found in \nimplementation of various drinking water schemes/projects in the country; \n \n(b) If so, the details thereof, State/UTwise; \n \n(c) Whether the Government is planning to establish a p arallel mechanism to \ntackle these irregularities; and \n \n(d) If so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI  RAMESH  CHANDAPPA  JIGAJINAGI \n(a) to (d)    Rural drinking water supply is a Stat e subject. Under the \ncentrally sponsored National Rural Drinking Water P rogramme (NRDWP), \nthis Ministry supplements the efforts of the States to provide drinking water \nto the rural population by providing technical & fi nancial assistance. It is \nthe State Governments which plan, approve and execu te the water supply \nschemes. Any complaint in respect of  flaws/irregul arities  reported to this \nMinistry are generally forwarded to respective Stat e Government as they \nare the implementing agencies for investigation and   for taking appropriate \naction as per the provisions.   However, this Minis try recognizes the \nimportance of monitoring of its programmes independ ently. In order to \nenhance the efficiency & efficacy of the programme,  Government of India \nhas evolved a comprehensive system of monitoring th e implementation of \nits all programmes / schemes through National Level  Monitors (NLMs) to \nreport any irregularities / flaws. The report / recommendation of the NLM is \nshared with the State Governments to take remedial measures to improve \nthe efficiency of the programme.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Sewage Treatment Plants", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Drinking Water and Sanitation \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 776  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21-7-2016 \nSewage Treatment Plants \n \n776. SHRI M. CHANDRAKASI: \n \nWill the Minister of  DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government has made any estimate of the increa se in the sewage generation \nconsequent upon the construction of toilets under Swachch Bharat Mission; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n \n(a) No sir. In rural areas because of the low density of population and  sca ttered households \nless quantity of sewage is being generated. Furthermore, under Swachh Bharat Mission \n(Gramin), twin leach pit toilets are being promoted, under which no sewage is generated.  \n \n(b) Does not arise. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |778", "t": "Power Plant in Bangladesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.778 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nPOWER PLANT IN BANGLADESH \n \n778.  DR. KIRIT SOMAIYA: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether Government is considering to build a Thermal Power Plant in \nBangladesh; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether any contract in this regard has been signed by Indian side with \nBangladesh Authority; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the funding pattern; and \n \n(e)  the benefits likely to be accrued to the country through setting up of this \nplant in Bangladesh? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL, NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) to (e) : NTPC, a Central Public Sector Company and Bangladesh Power \nDevelopment Board (BPDB) have signed a Joint Venture Agreement on 29.01.2012 \nfor promoting a joint venture company namel y Bangladesh-India Friendship Power \nCompany (Pvt.) Limited (BIFPCL),  with equal (50:50) equity participation to \ndevelop coal based power project(s) in Bangladesh. BIFPCL has awarded EPC \n(Turnkey) Package for 2x660 MW Rampal (Khulna) project to BHEL on 31. 01.2016. \nThe estimated cost of the project is approximately US$ 1680.2 million and the \nfunding pattern envisaged for the project is 70% debt and 30% equity. NTPC will \nget dividends from BIFPCL from the profit generated by it.  It will also strengthen \nthe regional power co-operation in the South-East Asia. \n************* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |779", "t": "Irregularities under MGNREGS", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.779\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nIRREGULARITIES UNDER MGNREGS\n779. SHRI ALOK SANJAR:\nSHRI DILIP PATEL:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of funds released and utilised under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural\nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) during each year in last three years\nand current year, State/UTwise;\n(b) the details of number of people availing the scheme, State/UT-wise including Gujarat;\n(c) whether some cases of irregularities in use of funds including non -payment of wages\nunder the scheme have come into notice of the Government;\n(d) if so, the details thereof during each of the last three years and current year and the\nmeasures taken by the Government to check such irregularities in future;\n(e) the effect of this scheme on migration for employment; and\n(f) whether there has bee n any rural employment generation as a result thereof and if so,\nthe details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): State/UT-wise details of amount released and expenditure under the Mahatma Gandhi\nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) during the last three years and the\ncurrent year is indicated in Annexure-I.\n(b): The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) provides a\nlegal guarantee for 100 days of e mployment in a financial year to every rural household of the\ncountry including Gujarat whose adult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work. Not less\nthan 100 days of employment are provided to registered workers upon receipt of their\ndemand. Details of households provided employment as demanded by job card holders in\nStates/UTs including Gujarat under the MGNREGS during the last three years and the current year\nare indicated in the Annexure-I.\n(c)&(d): The Ministry, under MGNREGA receives compla ints of irregularities including non -\npayment of wages etc. from States/UTs. Since the responsibility of implementation of MGNREGA\nis vested with the State Governments, all complaints received in the Ministry are forwarded to the\nconcerned State Government s for taking appropriate action including investigation, as per law.\nDetailed instructions by way of Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) for dealing with complaints\n\nhave also been issued to all States/UTs. The year -wise data of the complaints is not being\nmaintained by the Ministry. The cumulative pendency of the complaints is monitored at the\nMinistry level. The State and UT-wise details of complaints is at Annexure-II.\n(e): Research has shown that consistent and strong implementation MGNREGA has reduced\ndistress migration. Results show that number of days provided work is inversely correlated with\nmigration by members of the household.\n(f): The reduction in distress migration by the whole household has concurrently meant,\nincrease in rural employment thr ough MGNREGA. Research also says that, but for the\nimplementation of MGNREGA, the crisis of joblessness in rural markets would have been acute.\n*****\n\nAnnexure-I referred in reply to parts (a)&(b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 779 dated 21.07.2016\n(In Lakhs)\nNo. State\nNo. of HH provided employment Central Release Total Expenditure\n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\n2016-17\nas on\n15/07/2016 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\n2016-17 as\non\n15/07/2016 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\n2016-17 as\non\n15/07/2016\n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 60.40 32.97 35.95 30.44 475049.00 290314.10 307380.33 338835.08 529342.76 283946.01 449688.01 126755.96\n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 1.40 1.36 1.82 0.63 13852.67 2704.16 4395.10 9525.22 9593.54 3441.52 6558.81 19.60\n3 ASSAM 12.62 9.67 15.13 5.65 57349.95 50023.46 87830.06 45511.58 69976.59 50310.65 62124.14 72304.97\n4 BIHAR 20.59 10.36 15.49 7.83 158070.67 95968.24 102412.26 76551.00 202702.34 108170.99 161917.44 51880.39\n5 CHHATTISGARH 25.12 17.48 21.76 17.08 144602.31 150570.49 106341.30 133272.13 202397.60 175421.54 128319.57 149285.87\n6 GUJARAT 5.79 5.13 5.57 4.39 33530.02 35442.93 30598.72 43199.86 48902.62 47121.78 42862.92 32413.17\n7 HARYANA 3.25 2.18 1.69 1.34 37687.81 16715.29 12470.72 6445.17 38161.54 21647.06 14139.49 9875.37\n8 HIMACHAL PRADESH 5.39 4.53 4.23 1.56 47797.09 35542.86 39610.32 26172.66 56959.21 40623.27 38845.67 12903.42\n9 JAMMU & KASHMIR 6.58 3.32 6.53 0.59 60315.73 52171.08 55801.83 44887.03 76971.19 39159.25 76853.95 31423.03\n10 JHARKHAND 11.39 11.11 11.27 12.48 62143.28 72433.41 97879.90 95645.56 91244.10 102908.13 133251.23 77663.56\n11 KARNATAKA 14.50 10.95 12.37 8.23 159606.81 171687.07 99155.27 126815.30 206841.31 167569.57 182227.07 111934.10\n12 KERALA 15.24 13.80 15.06 6.22 127710.93 158758.02 152633.88 85250.89 130056.59 161696.54 148318.42 76466.67\n13 MADHYA PRADESH 29.09 27.89 27.02 14.68 183982.44 245163.12 236732.20 204440.40 261894.50 280536.80 250002.67 158902.37\n14 MAHARASHTRA 11.44 11.60 12.75 10.27 115292.02 79951.77 123834.73 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |78", "t": "Special Economic Zones", "head": " \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*78 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nTECHNICAL TEXTILE SECTOR \n \n*78. SHRI R.P. MARUTHARAJAA: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र��मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether technical textile sector has  been recognised  as the fastest \ngrowing segment of textile industry in the country, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  the details of schemes/projects  underway to boost technical textile \nsector; \n \n(c)  the funds allocated and utilised for  the purpose during the last and the  \ncurrent Plan period, Plan-wise; and \n \n(d)  the other steps being taken by the  Government to promote technical \ntextile sector in the country? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंरी����������������������� ���������� ������������\nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *78 FOR ANSWER ON 21.07.2016. \n \n(a): Yes, Madam.  As per the estimates of the Working Group for the Textiles Industry for the                  \n12th Five Year Plan, the market size of Indian technical textile industry is expected to grow from \nINR 76,000 crore in 2012-2013 to INR 1.58 lakh crore by 2016-17, at an anticipated growth rate of \n20% year-on-year.  \n \n(b): The details of the schemes/projects of Ministry of Textiles, Govt. of India, underway to boost \ntechnical textile sector is annexed.  \n \n(c): Plan-wise allocation and utilization of funds under various schemes of technical textiles \nsector is as follows: \n  (Rs in Crore) \n                  Table-1 \nName of the Scheme Plan period Fund Allocation/ \nAuthorization Expenditure   \nTechnology Mission on \nTechnical Textiles (TMTT) \n11th Plan \n(2010-11 to 2011-12) \n81.39 61.50 \n12th Plan \n(2012-13 to 2016-17) \n140.365 105.595 \nTotal 221.755 167.095 \n                  \n                Table-2            (Rs in Crore) \nName of the Scheme \nPlan period \nFund \nAllocation/ \nAuthorization \nExpenditure  \nScheme for promoting usage \nof Agrotextiles in North East \nRegion* \n12th Plan \n(2012-13 to 2016-17) \n34.32 19.32 \n* The Scheme for promoting usage of Agrotextiles in North East Region was approved \nduring December 2012. \n \n                 Table-3           (Rs in Crore) \nName of the Scheme \nPlan period \nFund \nAllocation/ \nAuthorization \nExpenditure  \nScheme for promoting usage \nof Geotechnical textiles in \nNorth East Region** \n12th Plan (2014-15 to  \n2016-17) \n39.00 7.63 \n**The Scheme for promoting usage of Geo-technical Textiles in North East Region was \napproved during December 2014. \n \n(d): The other steps being taken by the Ministry of Textiles, Govt. of India to promote Technical \nTextile sector in the country are as follows: \n \n(i) Under the “Amended Technology Upgradation  Fund Scheme (A -TUFS)”, it is one \nof the focus area and  15% capital investment subsidy is provided on eligible \nmachines under Technical Textiles. \n(ii) In the budget announcement for 2016 -17, custom duties on select speciality fibres \nhave been reduced to promote the sector. \n(iii) Standards of different segments of Technical Textiles are being notified from time to \ntime. \n(iv) Ministry of Textiles is organizing an annual premier show on technical textiles \nTECHNOTEX with the objective of promotion and building business linkag es \nbetween the Indian Technical Textile Industry and the participating International \nTechnical textile fraternity. \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nDetails of schemes/projects of Ministry of Textiles, Govt. of India, underway to boost \ntechnical textile sector \n \nI. Technology Mission on Technical Textiles (TMTT): \n            ` \nMinistry of Textiles has launched Technology Mission on Technical Textiles (TMTT) \nwith two mini- missions for a period of five years (from 2010 -11 to 2014-15) with a fund \noutlay of Rs. 200 crore during De cember 2010.The scheme completed its tenure in 2014 -\n15 and it has been extended for the remaining two years i.e. 2015 -16 & 2016 -17 of 12th \nfive year plan.  The following new components have been added to the scheme during its \nextended period:- \n \na)  Scheme for Promoting usage of Agrotextiles in Rest of India: \n \n          With the overwhelming response of the Schemes for promoting usage of \nAgrotextiles in NER, a new Component \"Scheme for Promoting Usage of \nAgrotextiles in Rest of India\" has been introduced & funded under Mini-Mission-II \nof Technology Mission on Technical Textiles (TMTT) for a period of two years \n(2015-16 & 2016 -17) with a fund outlay of Rs. 5.00 crores, for Promoting Usage \nof Agrotextiles. \n \n(b) Focus Incubation Centers (FIC) \n \n       In order to help the potential investors to enter into technical textiles, \nMinistry of Textiles is setting up Focus Incubation Centers (FIC) in the COEs \nestablished under TMTT on plug and play model. Accordingly an amount of \nRs.17.45 crores has been sancti oned to six CoEs namely ATIRA, DKTE, NITRA, \nPSG College of Tech, SASMIRA & SITRA for se"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |780", "t": "Multimodal Ports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.780 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2016 \nMULTIMODAL PORTS \n \n780.  SHRI KANWAR SINGH TANWAR:  \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:  \nपोतप�रवहनमंत् \n(a) whether the Government proposes to develop multimodal ports in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof along with the names of locations \nidentified for the purpose; \n(c) whether Sahebganj in Jharkhand has been considered for the purpose and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n(d)  the details of funds allocated and released for the purpose so far? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a)&(b):The development of multimodal Inland Water Transport terminals under the Jal Marg \nVikas Project have been planned at Varanasi in Uttar Pradesh, Sahibganj in Jharkhand \nand Haldia in West Bengal along the National Waterway -1. The contract for Phase -I of \nthe terminal at Varanasi has been awarded and t he contractor has commenced \nmobilization. Tenders for Phase -I of the Sahebganj and Haldia terminals have been \nissued. \n \n(c):   The location of terminal at Sahebganj is proposed at Samda Nala and Rampur villages. \nThe terminal is planned to be linked to National Highway-80 and Sakhri Railway Station.  \nThe requirement of land for the main terminal, rail and road connectivity, resettlement \nand rehabilitation of 235 households has been assessed at 195 acres.  The estimated cost \ntowards acquisition of land, including cost towards Rehabilitation and Resettlement of \nproject affected families, is Rs .187 crore and that for civil construction of Phase -I of the \nterminal is Rs.292.10 crore. \n \n(d):    During 2015-16, Rs.76.99 crore was released to the Government of Jharkhand as the cost \nof land acquisition. In 2016-17, Rs.27.32 crore has been released so far for the purpose. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |781", "t": "Road Asset Management System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.781  \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2016 \n \nROAD ASSET MANAGEMENT SYSTEM \n781.  SHRI BHARATHI MOHAN R.K.:  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state:  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government has any plan to commissioning roads across the country and \nupdate  the data in Road Asset Management System (RAMS), if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether the Government has any application to find out the issues concerning with \njunctions, quality of the road, width of the road, width of the right of way and \nencroachment on both sides and have any idea about to solve these area;  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and action taken by the Government in this regard; and  \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE \nIN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (d)  Yes, Madam.  3,000 km of National Highways has already been awarded for being \ntaken up under Road Asset Management System (RAMS).  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |782", "t": "Irrigation Projects in Drought Prone Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 782 \nANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nIRRIGATION PROJECTS IN DROUGHT PRONE AREAS \n782.         SHRI K.R.P. PRABAKARAN \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has any plan to announce new irrigation projects in the drought \nprone areas across the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government has set up a team to identify the potential/affected areas in the \ncountry, State/UT-wise; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon, so far, along with the funds allocated \nfor the purpose? \nANSWER \nTHE MINI STER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & \nGANGA REJUVENATION \n (DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a) & (b)   Water being a State subject, planning, execution, operation and maintenance of \nwater resources projects including dams and canals are undertaken by the concerned State \nGovernments as per their own resources and priorities. In order to supplement the efforts of State \nGovernments, Central Government provides technical and financial assistance to encourage \nsustainable development and efficient management of water resources through various schemes \nand programmes viz. Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana (PMKSY) etc. \n(c)  &  (d)     Ministry of Water Resources, River Development & Ganga Rejuvenation  \nconstituted technical teams comprising the officers from regional offices of Central Water \nCommission & Central Ground Water Board and State Governments to analyse the problems and \ncauses leading to such situation,  identify water resources management challenges, gaps in water \ninformation and  plans to recharge with the aim to long term solutions. The teams have submitted \nthe reports. \nA sum of Rs. 1147.20 crores ha ve been released to the States during the current financial \nyear by Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers’ Welfare under PMKSY (Per Drop More Crop) \ncomponent for micro-irrigation and other interventions. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |783", "t": "Abolition of Legislative Councils", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE\nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 783\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THURSDAY, 21st JULY, 2016\nABOLITION OF LEGISLATIVE COUNCILS\n783. SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY:\nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of States having the Legislative Councils in the country; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to abolish the said system of Legislative Councils; \n(c) if so, the details and the objectives thereof; and \n(d) whether any committee has recommended for the same and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE AND ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION \nTECHNOLOGY\n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD)\n(a): At present, there are seven (7) States having Legislative Councils, viz.;\n(1) Andhra Pradesh, (2) Bihar, (3) Jammu and Kashmir, (4) Karnataka, (5) \nMaharahstra, (6) Telangana, and (7) Uttar Pradesh.\n(b) to (d): There is no proposal to abolish the system of Legislative Councils. However, \nthe procedure in this regard is that the State Legislative Assembly of the State has to \npass a resolution to that effect by a majority of the total membership of the Assembly \nand by a majority of not less than two-thirds of the members of the Assembly present\nand voting. Thereafter, appropriate action may be taken by the Central Government \nunder Article 169 of the Constitution."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |784", "t": "Solar Energy Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 784 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \nSOLAR ENERGY PROGRAMME \n784. SHRI P. KUMAR: \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is planning to accelerate the solar energy programme in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the cost of maintenance of solar energy equipments is very high; and \n(d) if so, the reasons therefor and the  steps taken by the  Government to lower the maintenance cost \nof solar energy equipments? \n      ANSWER \n \n MINISTER OF STATE  POWER, COAL , AND MINES NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY (SHRI \nPIYUSH GOYAL) \n(a) &(b): Yes, Madam. The Government has revised t he National Solar Power Mission tar get of \nGrid Connected Solar Power projects from 20,000 MW by 2022 to 1,00,000 MW  by 2022 so as to \naccelerate the solar energy programme in the country. \n(c) &(d): The cost of maintenance of solar power projects  is low compared to thermal projects  as \nthere are no moving parts in photovoltaic panels.  \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |785", "t": "CRF for J&K", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.785 \nANSWERED ON 21ST JULY, 2016 \n \nCRF FOR J&K \n \n785. SHRI JUGAL KISHORE: \n \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसडक परिवहन एवम िाज्मार्ग मंत्री  \nbe  pleased to state : \n \n(a) the details of roads in J&K selected for construction/repair/maintenance from the Central \nRoad Fund; and  \n(b) the funds allocated to the State during the last three years? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a): The details of roads sanctioned for construction under Central Road Fund (CRF) \nScheme for the State of Jammu & Kashmir are enclosed as annexed.  \n \n(b): As per CRF Rules, accrual of funds and release to State Government are based on \nthe population & geographical area of the State and submission of utilization certificates \nby the State Government respectively.  The details regarding accrual and release of funds \nto the state of J&K during last three years are as follows: \n \nYear  Accrual (Rs. in Crore) Release (Rs. in Crore) \n2013-14 131.87 79.19 \n2014-15 131.87 43.96 \n2015-16 142.86 42.97 \n \n  \n\n         ANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 785 FOR ANSWER ON 21.07.2016 ASKED BY SHRI \nJUGAL KISHO