\nPunjab 2 0 2\nRajasthan 0 0 0\nTamil Nadu 1 0 1\nUttar Pradesh 1 0 1\nWest Bengal 0 0 0"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |974", "t": "Social Sector Schemes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA                                                                 \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING                                                                            \nLOK SABHA                                                                                     \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 974                                                                \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2016 \n \nSOCIAL SECTOR SCHEMES \n \n974.  SHRI GODSE HEMANT TUKARAM \n         \nWill the Minister of Planning be pleased to state: \n (a) the details of the schemes in social sector for which financial assistance has been given to \nStates/UTs by the Government;  \n (b) the funds allocated and spent for each scheme, State/UT-wise;  \n (c) the criteria adopted to sanction financial assistance to each State/UT in this regard; \n(d) the proposal of State Government of Maharashtra pending for the last three years; and \n(e) the time by which clearances are likely to be given to the proposed schemes?        \n \n ANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT & \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING &URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n(a) & (b): The Social Sector Schemes are implemented by various Ministries viz. Food & Public \nDistribution, Health & Family Welfare, Human Resource Development, Labour & Employment, \nMinority Affairs, Rural Development, Social Justice &  Empowerment, Housing and Urban \nPoverty Alleviation, Tribal Affairs, Urban Development , Drinking Water & Sanitation  and \nWomen & Child Development. Among these schemes, there are 12 major schemes having larger \noutlays. The scheme-wise details of the major S ocial Sector Schemes, along with funds released \nto the States/UTs during 2013-14 to 2015-16 are given in the Annexure. \n(c): The Social Sector Schemes are implemented by the States/Administrative \nMinistries/Departments concerned as per the guidelines of the  individual schemes, prepared in \nconsultation with the States/UTs, which also includes  the criteria adopted to sanction financial \nassistance to States/UTs.  \n(d) & (e): No proposal/project  during last three years from the State of Maharashtra is pending \nwith NITI Aayog.  \n****** \n\n                                                                                                       ANNEXURE \nAnnexure to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 974.  to be answered on 22.07.2017 on Social Sector \nSchemes raised by \n SHRI GODSE HEMANT TUKARAM \nState- wise Releases during 2013-14, 2014-15 and 2015-16 under Social Sector Schemes (Rs. Crore) \nStates/UTs  Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) National Health Mission (NHM) MGNREGA \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nArunachal \nPradesh \n192.62 336.08 181.79 96.44 139.82 222.98 158.53 27.04 44.26 \nAssam 1318.21 983.28 1006.93 1101.07 917.42 998.60 573.50 500.23 878.30 \nHimachal Pradesh 114.53 125.47 121.39 213.57 188.28 249.06 477.97 355.43 396.21 \nJammu & \nKashmir  \n891.44 512.77 1299.81 409.26 347.00 386.89 603.16 521.71 558.02 \nMaipur 131.94 214.66 183.55 90.06 135.28 120.07 231.00 219.97 255.45 \nMeghalaya 283.41 204.05 166.27 127.17 107.15 107.66 271.06 277.86 222.03 \nMizoram 106.58 147.40 94.38 83.00 103.82 95.24 244.74 111.41 285.30 \nNagaland 158.03 205.69 87.40 135.02 116.09 108.48 292.15 113.05 266.66 \nSikkim 41.95 45.26 40.54 46.31 51.87 40.45 106.84 73.86 86.23 \nTripura 159.91 198.00 169.57 141.43 125.21 140.59 943.66 636.62 1359.81 \nUttarkhand 220.43 233.03 227.02 254.87 284.06 286.68 330.00 286.36 450.77 \nAndhra Pradesh 0.00 1,876.74 670.86 313.50 548.64 665.48 0.00 4,599.74 3073.80 \nBihar 2610.13 2171.34 2518.67 1273.02 1,229.22 1276.31 1580.71 959.68 1,024.12 \nChhattisgarh 767 927.65 622.91 372.91 508.75 424.70 1446.02 1,505.70 1065.56 \nGoa 13.34 13.10 8.14 21.03 27.28 17.34 2.06 1.38 2.47 \nGujarat 805.6 790.25 619.60 911.18 870.58 718.58 335.3 354.43 307.24 \nHaryana 350.88 421.11 345.01 356.31 281.88 318.75 376.88 167.15 124.87 \nJharkhand 450.11 764.21 561.21 417.04 389.54 430.03 621.43 724.33 987.93 \nKarnataka 653.71 668.59 421.38 675.31 758.39 782.95 1596.07 1,716.87 994.18 \nKerala 208.01 223.04 130.40 378.73 541.40 317.80 1277.11 1,587.58 1,526.34 \nMadhya Pradesh 2004.09 1,490.95 1,601.98 925.17 1,200.61 1,159.09 1839.82 2,451.63 2,367.32 \nMaharastra 657.08 582.89 412.25 1327.94 1495.66 1,176.34 1152.92 799.52 1,239.71 \nOdisha 739.56 666.95 820.82 625.93 677.91 671.87 757.53 1,035.30 1,479.41 \nPunjab 383.24 362.16 300.04 355.6 393.88 306.63 226.15 189.48 245.57 \nRajasthan 2424.89 2,480.42 1,934.62 977.23 1,156.63 1,341.45 2059.43 2,976.10 2,713.25 \nTamilNadu 968.27 1,358.20 821.12 979.52 992.27 1,119.56 4690.21 3,781.80 5,485.92 \nTelagana 1747.15 492.45 219.90 619.54 391.54 449.42 4750.49 223.36 1,833.10 \nUttar Pradesh 4682.07 4,507.72 5,064.56 3239.14 2,642.92 2,971.48 2896.39 2,513.41 2,702.23 \nWest Bengal 1531.96 233.03 846.79 1000.2 284.06 1,062.42 2894.38 286.36 4,730.67 \nA&N Islands 6.12 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |975", "t": "Procurement of Defence Equipment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE \nLOK  SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.975 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016 \n \n \nPROCUREMENT OF DEFENCE EQUIPMENT \n \n975. SHRIMATI NEELAM SONKER:   \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has signed agreement with foreign countries for \nprocurement of defence equipment; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the total amount invested by the Government for procurement of defen ce \nequipment from foreign countries during each of the last three years and the current year \nindicating the amount in terms of percentage of total defence budget;  \n \n(d)  whether the Indian Air Force and Navy have very less number of modern fighter \naircraft and warships in comparison to China; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and the corrective measures taken by the Government in \nthis regard? \n \n                  \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                      (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h               ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n \n(a) to (e): Government constantly reviews the security scenario and \naccordingly decides to induct appropriate defence equipment including \naircrafts and warships to keep the Armed Forces in  a state of readiness to \nmeet any eventualities and to defend national interests against any \npotential threats or adversaries. \n \nDuring the last three financial years (2013 -14  to  2015 -16), 56 \ncontracts have been signed with foreign vendors with cumulative  value of \nRs.53684.32 crore for Capital procurement of defence equipment for the \nArmed Forces such as Aircrafts, UAVs, Helicopters and Ammunition.  \n……2/- \n\n:  2  : \n \n \n \nThe expenditure on Capital acquisition from foreign vendors and \npercentage of such expend iture of the total expenditure on Capital \nacquisition during each of the last three years is as under:- \n \nYear Expenditure (Rs. in crore) \n\\ \n2013-14 35082.10 (52.47%) \n2014-15 24992.36 (38.11%) \n2015-16 22422.12 (36.30%) \n \n \nThe expenditure on capital procur ement from foreign vendors during \n2016-17 (upto 30.6.2016) is Rs.2795.78 crore. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |976", "t": "Low Power Transmitter", "head": "        \n       GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 976 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2016) \n \nLOW POWER TRANSMITTER  \n976. SHRI RAJU SHETTY: \n \nWill the MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the number of Very Low Power Transmitter in Doordarshan network in the \ncountry along with the transmission capacity, location and State/UT-wise;  \n(b) the details of viewership of TV  programmes relayed by Very Low Power \nDoordarshan Kendras (DDKs) along with the expenditure incurred to run these \nDDKs during the last three years;  \n(c) whether the transmission capacity of the said DDKs of the country has not \nbeen enhanced so far, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;  \n(d) whether the Government proposes to close the low power DDKs located in \nvarious parts of the country, if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) the time by which the decision is likely to be taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND \nBROADCASTING  \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n \n(a) Prasar Bharati has informed that there are 368 Very Low Power TV \nTransmitters (VLPTs) of 10W/ 50W power and 14 Transposers of 10W power in \nthe country. State/UT-wise, locations of the above mentioned VLPTs are given at \nAnnexure-I. \n \n(b) (i) No in-house survey has been conducted to assess Low Power Transmitter \nViewership by DD. Broadcast Audience Research Council (BARC) also does not \nprovide LPTV viewership data. \n \n\n \n(ii) LPTVs/ VLPTs functioning in Doordarshan Network are looked after by the \nrespective Doordarshan Maintenance Centres  (DMCs) spread over the country. \nDoordarshan allocates funds to the DMCs to meet the operational cost of the \nLPTVs/VLPTs. The total expenditure incurred by DMCs during the last three \nyears is as under:- \n2013-14       Rs. 318.16 Crore \n2014-15       Rs. 349.66 Crore \n2015-16       Rs. 365.65 Crore \n \n(c)  Upgradation/ Modernization of Doordarshan Kendras (induction of new \ntechnologies, replacement of old ageing equipment and augmentation/ \nupgradation of facilities) is a continuous process. Details of the VLPTs upgraded \nto LPT/ HPT, so far, are given at Annexure-II. \n(d) & (e) Prasar Bharati has already decided to close the  following four Low \nPower Transmitters (LPTs) as they lie in the coverage zone of nearby High \nPower Transmitters (HPTs): \n1. Madhepura (Bihar)   \n2. Simri Bakhtiarpur (Bihar) \n3. Khagaria (Bihar) \n4. Kalna (west Bengal) \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 976 FOR ANSWER ON 22.07.2016 \n \nVery Low Power Transmitters in Doordarshan network \nSTATE/UT TRANSMITTERs \n    \nANDHRA \nPRADESH \n VLPTs   (8)   \n KANIGIRI PADERU DUTTALUR \n MADIPARDU PARWATIPURAM ICCHAPURAM \n MARIPADU SEETAMPETA  \n    \n TRANSPOSER (1)   \n VIJAYAWADA   \n    \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH \nVLPTs (39)   \n ALONG HAWAI RAGA \n BARIRIJO HAYULIANG ROING \n BASAR HUNLI RUPA \n BOLENG INKIYONG SAGALEE \n BOMDILLA KALAKTANG SANGRAM \n CHANGLANG KHIMYONG SEIJOSA \n CHAYANGTAJO KHONSA SEPPA \n DAPORIZO MARIYANG TALIHA \n DARAK MECHUKA TAWANG \n DEOMALI MUKTO TIRBIN \n DIRANG NAMPONG TUTING \n GEKU NAMSAI YOMCHA \n GENSI PALIN ZIRO \n    \nASSAM   VLPT (1)   \n DIGBOI   \n    \n TRANSPOSER (1)   \n GUWAHATI   \n    \nBIHAR VLPTs (2)   \n MASRAKH MARHAURA  \n    \nCHHATTISGARH VLPTs (8)   \n BIJAPUR KONDAGAON PATHALGAON \n DEVBHOG KOYLIBEDA SARANGARH \n\n JASHPURNAGAR PAKHANJORE  \n    \nGUJARAT VLPTs (3)   \n KAKRAPAR NETRANG SAGWARA \n    \nHIMACHAL \nPRADESH \nVLPTs (39)   \n AJHU FORT DALHAUSI NEHRI \n ASHAPURI DIAR NICHAR \n AWAH DEVI HAMIRPUR PALAMPUR \n BAIJNATH HOLI PARWANOO \n BANDLA JAHALMA PIRBHAYANU \n \n BANJAR JATINGIRI \n(PHOOLADHAR) \nROHRU \n BHARMOUR JOGINDERNAGAR SARKAGHAT \n BHARTHI KAJA SHIVBADAR \n BIJLI MAHADEV KALPA THANEDAR \n CHAMBA KARSOG TISSA \n CHAUPAL KEYLONG UDAIPUR \n CHAURI KHAS KHARA PATHAR UNA \n CHIRGAON KOTKHAI VEER \n    \n TRANSPOSERS (2)   \n RAJGARH SOLAN  \n    \nJHARKHAND VLPTs (3)   \n SIMDEGA RAMGARH HILL GARHWA (DD News) \n    \nJAMMU & \nKASHMIR \nVLPTs (84)   \n ABRAN HANLE PADAM \n ARDH KUMARI HIRA NAGAR PAHALGAM (RLS- K) \n ARNAS ICHAR PANAMIK \n ASHMUQAM (RLS- \nK) \nJAJJAR KOTLI PANICKER \n BANI KALAKOT PONI \n BANIHAL KANGAN (RLS- K) PULWAMA (RLS- K) \n BARAMULLA (RLS- \nK) \nKARGIL (RLS- K) RAMBAN \n BASECAMP \n(SIACHIN) \nKHALTSI RAMKOT \n BASGO KHATLAI RAMNAGAR \n BASOLI KHREW (RLS- K) RINGDOM GOMPA \n BATALIK KISHTWAR SAKTI \n BATOT KOTRANKA SANASAR \n\n BHADARWA KUD SANKOO \n BILAWAR LATI SHOPIAN (RLS- K) \n BODH KHURBOO LOLLAB VALLEY \n(RLS- K) \nSONMARG (RLS- K) \n BONIYAR (RLS- K) LORAN SUDH- MAHADEV \n BUDHAL MACHIL (RLS- K) TANGMARG \n CHAKROI MAHORE TANGSTE \n CHANANI MANDI TATAPANI \n CHUMATHANG MANIGAM (RLS- \nK) \nTHANAMANDI \n \n \n CHUSHUL MANJAKOT THATHRI \n DAH MANSUR TIMSOGAM \n DASKIT MENDHAR TRAL (RLS- K) \n DHAR MULBEKH TURTOK \n DODA NAGROTA URI \n DOMCHUK NIMU URI (RLS- K) \n DRAS NOWGAM (RLS- \nK) \nYUSMARG (RLS- K) \n GUJJARON \nNAGROTA \nNYEMA ZANGLA \n    \n TRANSPOSE"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |977", "t": "Performance of MFIs", "head": "Draft Reply\nGovt. of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial services\nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 977\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22th July, 2016 / Ashadha 31, 1938 (SAKA)\nPerformance of MFIs \nQUESTION           \n                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         \n977. SHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI:\n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be \npleased to state:\n(a) whether the Micro Finance \nInstitutions (MFIs) including \nLocal Area Banks (LABs) are \nfunctioning satisfactorily and \nhave a significant positive effect \non the economy of the country;\n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if \nnot, the reasons for their failure; \nand\n(c) the steps taken by the \nGovernment to improve the \ncondition of these MFIs and \nLabs?\nANSWER\nTo be answered by \n( MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE)\n(SHRI  SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR)\n(a) & (b): Non-Banking Financial Company - Micro Finance \nInstitutions (NBFC-MFIs) having legal company structure are \nregulated by Reserve Bank of India (RBI). As per RBI, these \nMFIs are functioning satisfactorily and have a positive impact \non the economy of the country. Number of such MFIs \nregistered with RBI has increased from 46 in March, 2014 to \n70 in March, 2016 and loans & advances of these MFIs has \nincreased from Rs.20,969 crore to Rs.44,557 crore during the \nsame period.\n                 Presently three Local Area Bank (LABs) are in \nexistence viz. Coastal LAB Ltd., Krishna Bhima Samruddhi \nLAB Ltd. and Subhadra LAB Ltd. These are functioning \nreasonably well with high Capital to Risk Weighted Assets \nRatio (CRAR), negligible NPAs and reasonable profits.\n(c):  The steps taken by RBI to improve the condition of MFIs \nand LABs, include inter alia a separate category of NBFC-\nMFIs, a Self-Regulatory Organisation (SRO) structure, \nguidelines on Fair Practice Code, interest rate caps to protect \ncustomers, LABs owned and controlled by residents made \neligible for conversion into Small Finance Bank etc. \n\nGovt. of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial services\nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 977\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22th July, 2016 / Ashadha 31, 1938 (SAKA)\nPerformance of MFIs \nQUESTION                \n                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            \n977. SHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Micro Finance Institutions (MFIs) including Local Area Banks (LABs) are \nfunctioning satisfactorily and have a significant positive effect on the economy of the \ncountry;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons for their failure; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government to improve the condition of these MFIs and Labs?\nANSWER\n(MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE)\n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR)\n(a) & (b):   Non-Banking Financial Company - Micro Finance Institutions (NBFC-MFIs) \nhaving legal company structure are regulated by Reserve Bank of India (RBI). As per \nRBI, these MFIs are functioning satisfactorily and have a positive impact on the \neconomy of the country. Number of such MFIs registered with RBI has increased from \n46 in March, 2014 to 70 in March, 2016 and loans and advances of these MFIs has \nincreased from Rs.20,969 crore to Rs.44,557 crore during the same period.\n                 Presently three Local Area Bank (LABs) are in existence viz. Coastal LAB \nLtd., Krishna Bhima Samruddhi LAB Ltd. and Subhadra LAB Ltd. These are functioning \nreasonably well with high Capital to Risk Weighted Assets Ratio (CRAR), negligible \nNPAs and reasonable profits.\n(c): The steps taken by RBI to improve the condition of MFIs and LABs, include inter \nalia a separate category of NBFC-MFIs, a Self-Regulatory Organisation (SRO) \nstructure, guidelines on Fair Practice Code, interest rate caps to protect customers, \nLABs owned and controlled by residents made eligible for conversion into Small \nFinance Bank etc.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |978", "t": "Premature Deaths", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 978\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nPREMATURE DEATHS\n978. SHRI ADHIR RANJAN CHOWDHURY:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether approx. 1.6 million premature deaths per year in India are caused due to health issues\nfrom air pollution, if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the details of speciality treatments and related infrastructu re available in the country to fight\nthe above menace; and\n(c) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to ensure availability of treatment facilities for\nair pollution in far flung villages and to bring down mortality rate due to air pollution?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL)\n(a) to (c): As per Global Burden of Disease Study in 2010, approximately 1.6 million premature\ndeaths (under 70 years of age) are attributable to household and ambient air pollution in India.\nDiseases attributable to air pollution include chronic respiratory diseases, pneumonia in Children\nand asthma, which are treated at various public health facilities in the country. A Steering\nCommittee of experts was con stituted in January, 2014 to assess the extent of the problem and\nsuggest action plan to mitigate adverse health impacts. The Committee submitted report in\nAugust, 2015 and the same was accepted by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare in\nDecember, 2015. The Report has been circulated to all concerned Ministries for appropriate\naction.  Following the recommendations of the Committee, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary\nDisease (COPD), which is mainly attributed to air pollution, has been included under National\nProgramme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke.\n…………….."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |979", "t": "Diseases among Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 979\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nDISEASES AMONG CHILDREN\n979. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN BHATT:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to formulate a plan/formulated a plan to protect and\nprevent occurrence of diseases among children; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) & (b): Yes. Under National Health Mission (NHM) various child health related\ninterventions are being implemented to protect and prevent occurrence of diseases among\nchildren. These interventions are as follows:\ni. Promotion of Institutional deliveries through cash incentive under Janani Suraksha Yojana\n(JSY) and Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK) which entitles all pregnant women\ndelivering in public health institutions to absolutely free and no expense ante -natal check-\nups, delivery including Caesarean section, post -natal care and treatment of sick infants till\none year of age.\nii. Strengthening of delivery points for providing comprehensive and quality Reproductive,\nmaternal, newborn, Child and Adolescent Health (RMNCH+A) Services,  ensuring\nessential newborn care at all delivery points, establishment of Special Newb orn Care Units\n(SNCU), Newborn Stabilization Units (NBSU) and Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) units for\ncare of sick and small babies.\niii. Early initiation and exclusive breastfeeding for first six months and appropriate Infant and\nYoung Child Feeding (IYCF) prac tices are promoted in convergence with Ministry of\nWomen and Child Development. Village Health and Nutrition Days (VHNDs) are observed\nfor provision of maternal and child health services and creating awareness on maternal and\nchild care including health and nutrition education.\n\niv. Treatment of sick children with severe acute malnutrition at special units called the\nNutrition Rehabilitation Centres (NRCs), set up at public health facilities. Presently 960\nsuch centres are functional across the country.\nv. Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) is being supported to provide vaccination to\nchildren against many life threatening diseases such as Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus,\nPoliomyelitis, Tuberculosis, Measles, Hepatitis B, Meningitis and Pneumonia due to\nHaemophilus Influenzae type B. In addition, vaccination against Japanese Encephalitis is\ncarried out in endemic districts and vaccination against Rotavirus diarrhoea is provided in\nfour states (Odisha, Himachal Pradesh, Haryana and Andhra Pradesh).\nvi. Name based tr acking of mothers and children till two years of age is done to ensure\ncomplete antenatal, intranatal, postnatal care and complete immunization as per schedule.\nvii. Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK) for health screening, early detection of birth\ndefects, diseases, deficiencies, development delays including disability and early\nintervention services has been operationalized to provide comprehensive care to all the\nchildren in the age group of 0-18 years.\nviii. Some other important interventions are Iron and f olic acid (IFA) supplementation for the\nprevention of anaemia among the vulnerable age groups, annual deworming on National\nDeworming Day (NDD), home visitation by ASHAs to promote exclusive breast feeding\nand early detection and referral of sick newborns and promoting the use of ORS and Zinc\nfor management of diarrhoea in children.\nix. To sharpen the focus on the low performing districts, 184 High Priority Districts (HPDs)\nhave been prioritized for Reproductive Maternal Newborn Child Health+ Adolescent\n(RMNCH+A) interventions for achieving improved maternal and child health outcomes."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |98", "t": "Quarterly Report on Employment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 98\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016\nKIDNEY TRADE\n*98. ADV. M. UDHAYAKUMAR:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has taken note of illegal trade in kidney reported recently\nin Delhi and other hospitals in the country;\n(b) whether the prevalence of illegal organ trade is a result of increasing gap between\nthe demand and supply of organs for transplants across the country and if so, the\ndetails thereof; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government to bridge this gap including re -visiting and\nrevising guidelines for cadaver -based donations for the benefit of pati ents in urgent\nneed of transplants?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 98* FOR 22ND JULY, 2016\n(a)  Yes.\n(b) The huge gap between the availability of and requirement for organs contributes to the illegal\ntrade in human organs.\n(c)  Health is a State subject. However, the Government of India has, in close cooperation with the\nStates, taken a number of steps to increase donation of cadaver organs to bridge the gap between the\ndemand for and supply of organs and to save the lives of a large number of persons suffering from\nend stage organ failure. The Government of India has, as part of t he National Organ Transplant\nProgrammes, set up an apex national level organization namely National Organs and Tissues\nTransplant Organisation (NOTTO) at New Delhi to promote retrieval of organs and tissues. Updated\ninformation on organ donation related ar eas is made available to the general public on NOTTO\nwebsite. NOTTO has a 24x7 call centre with toll free helpline number (1800114770) and a National\nOrgan and Tissue Donation and Transplant Registry has also been launched. Financial assistance has\nalso be en sanctioned for establishing four Regional Organ and Tissue Transplant Organization\n(ROTTO) in Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Assam and Chandigarh. Organ donation awareness\nprogrammes and training of transplant coordinators, etc have been taken up in a big way.\n*********"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |980", "t": "Online Medical Report Facility", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 980\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nONLINE MEDICAL REPORT FACILITY\n980. SHRI NAGAR RODMAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has implemented or proposes to implement online medical test\nreport facility for patients in Government hospitals; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the funds allocated and utilised by States/ UTs as on date?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL)\n(a): Yes. Online Registration System (ORS) is being implemented in various hospitals in\nStates/UTs to provide online registration, online appointment services to citizens along with\nonline viewing of medical test report on ORS portal by citizens. So far it has been implemented\nin 43 hospitals. However, the module for online viewing of reports has been started only in four\nhospitals (viz. AIIMS, New Delhi; RML, New Delhi; NIMHANS, Bengaluru; and PGIMER,\nChandigarh) through this Portal (http://ors.gov.in/copp/lab_report_gate.jsp)\n(b): Financial assistance is being provided for introducing this application in District/Sub -\ndistrict hospitals & CHSs under National Health Mission (NHM) as per the proposal received\nfrom states/ UTs in their program implementation Plan (PIP)"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |981", "t": "Kisan TV", "head": "         GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 981 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2016) \n \nKISAN TV \n981.  SHRI K.  PARASURAMAN: \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n(a)  the number of telecasting centres established by the Government for Kisan TV \nin Tamil Nadu;  \n \n(b)  whether the number of research related programmes in agriculture, telecasted \non Kisan TV, is less and even the said Channel is not available to people who avail \nprivate DTH companies;  \n \n(c)  if so, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(d)  the steps taken for optimum telecasting of Kisan TV channel to all parts of the \ncountry particularly in rural areas? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND \nBROADCASTING  \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n \n  \n(a) to (d) Prasar Bharati has informed that the State of Tamil Nadu does not \nhave any telecasting centre (terrestrial transmitter) for Kisan TV. DD  Kisan is \navailable on Doordarshan’s Free to Air DTH service. \n \nDD Kisan Channel carries programmes related to agricultural research such \nas “Vichar Vimarsh”, “Khet Khalihaan”, “Vaad - Samvaad”, “Hello Kisan” etc.  It is \nmandatory for all private DTH operators  to carry this channel on their DTH \nplatforms. \n \n“DD Kisan”, which is a farmer-centric channel, was launched on 26-05-2015 \nfrom Delhi. It is available in the bouquet of Doordarshan’s DTH Service, the signal \nof which can be received anywhere in the country (except A&N Islands), including \nrural areas, with the help of a small sized dish receive unit. \n \nIn addition to above, digital High Power Transmitters of Doordarshan, one \neach at Delhi & Raipur, are also transmitting Kisan TV Channel “DD Kisan”  in \nterrestrial mode. \n \nDD-Kisan is also available through Live Streaming and accessible on \nmobile. \n****** \n\n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |982", "t": "Army Aviation Fleet", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE \nLOK  SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.982 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016 \n \n \nARMY AVIATION FLEET \n \n982. SHRI  SISIR KUMAR ADHIKARI: \n  \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether most of the Army Aviation fleet are more than thirty years old ; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to replace and acquire modern aviation fleet for \nthe Armed Forces; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n                  \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                      (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h               ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n(a) & (b): About 50% of helicopters held in the inventory of Army Aviation \nfleet are more than thirty years old. \n \n(c) & (d): An Inter Government Agreement has been signed in 2015 with \nRussian Government for procurement of 200 Kamo v helicopters for \nreplacing older Cheetah / Chetak fleet. \n \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |983", "t": "Research under CCRYN", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(AYUSH)\nLOK  SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.983\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nRESEARCH UNDER CCRYN\n983. KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government/Central Council for Research in Yoga and Naturopathy (CCRYN) is\nalso considering a number of research proposals received from public and private research\ninstitutes including Swami Vivekananda Yoga Research Foundation under its Extra Mural\nResearch (EMR) scheme for specific diseases including Cancer;\n(b) if so, the details of the proposals received from such institutions; and\n(c) whether the Cancer Centre of All India Institute of Medical Sciences has also successfully\ntested a coded Ayurvedic drug for improving quality of life of breast cancer patients recently, if\nso, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a): Yes.\n(b): The details of proposals received from various institutions is at Annexure-A.\n(c): The Central Council for Research in Ayurvedic Sciences (CCRAS), an autonomous\norganisation under Ministry of AYUSH, in collaboration with All India Institute of Medical\nSciences, New Delhi, conducted a study to evaluate the efficacy of AYUSH QOL -2C in\nimproving quality of life in non metastatic Breas t cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy/\nradiotherapy. The study has been completed and data analysis started. The objective of the\nstudy did not include cure of cancer.\n\nCentral Council for Research in Yoga & Naturopathy, New Delhi\nAnnexure -‘A’.\nList of proposals received under EMR scheme\nS.No. Name & Address of the PI Title of the Project\n1. Dr. Anju Dhawan,\nProf, NDDTC, and Deptt of\nPsychiatry, AIIMS,\nNew Delhi\nTo Assess the effect of Yoga of Psychological\nin School Children\n2. Dr. Sonali Jhanjee,\nAdditional Prof, National Drug\nDependence Treatment Centre\n(NDDTC), AIIMS\nA Pilot study of Yoga for smokeless tobacco\ncessation\n3. Dr. Raghavendra Rao M\nHealth Care Global Enterprise ltd.\n(HCG), #8, P-Kalignagrao Road,\nSampangiram Nagar, Bangalore\nNeural Correlates of Yoga in Breast Cancer\nPatients with Chemo Brain: A randomized\ncontrolled trial\n4. Dr. Ratna Sharma,\nProfessor, Deptt. of Physiology,\nAll India Institute of Medical\nSciences, Ansari Nagar, New\nDelhi-110029\nEEG Microstate Signatures and Resting\nNeural Sources of Meditation and Stress\n5. Dr. Manjari Tripathi,\nProfessor, Deptt. of Neurology,\nAnsari Nagar, New Delhi-110029\nA Randomized Controlled Clinical Trial to\nDetermine the Efficacy of a Standardized\nYoga Intervention compared with standard\ncare for improving OSA\n6. Dr. Rima Dada,\nProfessor, Laboratory for\nMolecular Reproduction and\nGenetics, Deptt. of Anatomy, All\nIndia Institute of Medical Sciences,\nAnsari Nagar, New Delhi-110029\nA Translational randomized trial of Yoga\nbased Lifestyle Intervention for major\nDepressive Disorder (MDD) Management in\nIndia and analyzing biomarkers of Cellular\naging associated with it\n7. Dr. Rima Dada,\nProfessor, Laboratory for\nMolecular Reproduction and\nGenetics, Deptt. of Anatomy, All\nIndia Institute of Medical Sciences,\nAnsari Nagar, New Delhi-110029\nEffect of Yoga Based Intervention on\nOxidative damage to Sperm DNA: Impact on\nChildhood Cancer\n8. Dr. K. Satya Lakshmi,\nDirector, National Institute of\nNaturopathy (NIN), Ministry of\nAYUSH, Bapu Bhavan, Matoshree\nRamabai Ambedkar Road, Pune-\n411001, Maharashtra\nNaturopathy and Yoga as a Primary\nPrevention Model In Addressing Modifiable\nCancer Risk Factors. A Pilot Parallel\nRandomised Control Trial\n9. Dr. Kamakhya Kumar,\nDev Sanskriti Vishvavidyalaya,\nShantikunj, Haridwar-249411\nA Psycho-physiological approach of Yoga\ntowards PTSD Patients\n10. Dr. Santosh Vishvakarma,\nDev Sanskriti Vishvavidyalaya,\nEffect of Pranic Healing and Marma Therapy\non Insomnia, Subjective well - being and\n\nShantikunj, Haridwar-249411 cognitive ability of college students - a\ncomparative study\n11. Dr. Vijay Kumar Singh,\nAssistant Professor, Dept. of Yoga\n& Health, Dev Sanskriti\nVishvavidyalaya, Shantikunj,\nHaridwar-249411\nEffect of a Nine Day Long Lifestyle\nModification Program ( Sanjivani Sadhana\nProgram) on subjective well -being, mood\nstates and cognitive ability in adults\n12. Prof. Suresh Lal Barnwal,\nProfessor and Head, Dept. of Yoga\n& Health, Dev Sanskriti\nVishvavidyalaya, Shantikunj,\nHaridwar-249411\nEffect of a Yoga Practices P ackage and Yagya\nTherapy on Insomnia, Subjective Well -being\nand Cognitive Ability of Corporate Employees\n- A Comparative Study\n13. Dr. Bandi Hari Krishna,\nAssistant Professor Physiology,\nSri Venkateswara Medical College\n& SVRR GGH, Tirupati, Andhra\nPradesh- 517507\nStandardization and Clinical Evaluation of\nMeditation to Mild Cognitive Impairment\n14. Dr. Rajesh S.K.,\nAssistant Professor, #19, Eknath\nBhavan, Gavipuram Circle,\nKempegowdanagar"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |984", "t": "Women Journalists Workshop", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 984 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2016  \n \nWOMEN JOURNALISTS WORKSHOP  \n \n984. SHRI MALYADRI SRIRAM: \nWill the Minister of WOMEN & CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state :  \na)   whether the Government has recently organized All India Women \nJournalists Workshop in the country;  \n \nb)    if so, the details thereof indicating the participants therein from across the \ncountry, State/UT-wise therein;  \n \nc)     the funds allocated and spent in this regard; and  \n \nd)   the details of discussions held therein along with the benefits accrued \nthereby?  \n \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRIMATI MANEKA SANJAY GANDHI)  \n \na) & b)  Yes, All India Women Journalists Workshop was organized on \n07.06.2016 at Vigyan Bhavan, New Delhi. List of Participants, State/UT -wise is \nenclosed at Annexure – I.  \n \nc)  A sum of Rs. 1 crore was allocated by the Ministry of Finance for the said \nworkshop. Details of expenditure incurred in this regard are enclosed at Annexure-II.  \n \nd)  During the Workshop, a comprehensive presentation was made on \nschemes/programme/activities/interventions undertaken by the Ministry in the last \ntwo years. It was explained how for the first time the Ministry has been examining the \nissue of safety of women in a holistic manner and providing care and protection to the \nchildren in a comprehensive manner. It was highlighted during the workshop about \nthe numerous ‘firsts’ of the Min istry including Beti Bachao Beti Padhao scheme, \nsetting up of One Stop Centres for women in distress, Mahila e -Haat, panic button on \nmobiles, guidelines for matrimonial websites, 33% reservation for women in police \nforce, gender champion scheme, unique ini tiative with railways for missing children, \ncomprehensive adoption reforms, junk food guidelines, construction of shelter homes \nfor widows and many more such initiatives.  \n \n\nAn exhibition was also organized outside the venue showcasing the live monitoring \nsystem of Childline -Child Helpline 1098, and district level initiatives of Beti Bachao Beti \nPadhao.  \n \n The Ministry of Commerce & Industry also made a presentation covering the \nachievements and new initiatives of the government during the last two years.  \n \nThe women journalists highlighted various issues affecting the common man during \ntheir interaction with the Minister of Women and Child Development and the Minister of State \nfor Commerce & Industry.  \n \n Women journalists were invited for an in -depth discuss ion about the Ministry’s \ninterventions for women and children. It is expected that after the workshop the journalists \nwould definitely be well equipped to spread messages amongst target people about the \npolicy/programme/interventions of the Ministry in emp owering women and nurturing children. \nWith the help of these journalists, the Ministry would able to reach out in the remote area as \nwell.  \n \n******* \n \n  \n\n \nAnnexure - 1 \nList of Participants of Journalist in All India Women Journalist Workshop \nheld on 07.06.2016 \nS. No. Name State/UTs \n1 Shuchismita S Jammu & Kashmir \n2 Neelima Srivastava Jammu & Kashmir \n3 Farzana Syed Jammu & Kashmir \n4 Zehru Nissa Jammu & Kashmir \n5 Zennat Zeeshan Jammu & Kashmir \n6 Sumaiya`Yusuf Jammu & Kashmir \n7 Shalini Rai Himachal Pradesh \n8 Neena Gautam Himachal Pradesh \n9 Kamlesh Verma Himachal Pradesh \n10 Renuka Gautam Himachal Pradesh \n11 Archana Phull Himachal Pradesh \n12 Bhawani Negi Himachal Pradesh \n13 Kusum Arora Punjab \n14 Kusum Agnihotri Punjab \n15 Rashmi Talwar Punjab \n16 Meenu Halhiki Punjab \n17 Gagan Kaur Teja Punjab \n18 Pushpinder Kaur Punjab \n19 Usha Kaur Jashan Punjab \n20 Usha Pawar Punjab \n21 Madhu Walia Haryana \n22 Komal Rohilla Haryana \n23 Nidhi Haryana \n24 Arti Dalal Haryana \n25 Rita Sharma Haryana \n26 Shashi Sharma Uttarakhand \n27 Jyotsana Uttarakhand \n\n28 Uma Sharma Chandigarh \n29 Ruby Singh Chandigarh \n30 Nisha Sharma Chandigarh \n31 Sonika Bhatia Chandigarh \n32 Kamlesh Rathore Uttar Pradesh \n33 Shikha Singh Uttar Pradesh \n34 Suman Gupta Uttar Pradesh \n35 Ira Awasthy Uttar Pradesh \n36 Anita Verma Uttar Pradesh \n37 Manisha Upadhyay Uttar Pradesh \n38 Sangita Srivastava Uttar Pradesh \n39 Shrinkhla Pandey Uttar Pradesh \n40 Ditti Vajpayee Uttar Pradesh \n41 Swati Shukla Uttar Pradesh \n42 Meera Jataw Uttar Pradesh \n43 Kavita Uttar Pradesh \n44 Amrita Murya Rajasthan \n45 Usha Joshi Rajasthan \n46 Hansa Vaishnav  Madhya Pradesh \n47 Ranjana Dubey Madhya Pradesh \n48 Shruti Jain Madhya Pradesh \n49 Savita Tiwari Madhya Pradesh \n50 Rumni Ghosh Madhya Pradesh \n51 Ruby Sarkar Madhya Pradesh \n52 Naaz Patel Madhya Pradesh \n53 Reena Sharma Madhya Pradesh \n54 Aarti Sharma  Madhya Pradesh \n55 Kavita Mandhare Madhya Pradesh \n56 Indira Rajesh Mangal Madhya Pradesh \n57 Rajani Khaitan  Madhya Pradesh \n58 Sadia Javed Madhya Pradesh \n59 Priyanka Gupta Chhattisgarh \n60 Neha Srivastava Chhattisgarh \n\n61 Jyoti Singh Chhattisgarh \n62 Shagufta Sheereen "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |985", "t": "Sick Banks", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†985\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22nd July, 2016/ Ashadha 31, 1938 (SAKA)\nSick Banks\nQUESTION\n†985.SHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether a large number of banks have become sick across the country at\npresent;\n(b) if so, the details of such banks and the estimated loss of the funds invested in\nsuch banks, bank-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has taken any measure to turn the sick banks\nprofitable; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nThe Minister of State in the Ministry of Finance\n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR)\n(a) to (d): Out of 27 Public Sector Banks (PSBs), 14 have posted losses. The main\nreason for such losses was Asset Quality Review (AQR) exercise initiative by\nReserve Bank of India (RBI) during FY16, due to which banks had to suddenly make\nprovisions for accounts categorized as non-performing asset (NPA) under AQR\nexercise.\nThe Government has taken various measures to strengthen the financial\nposition of banks which inter-alia include a four-year recapitalization program for\nPSBs under which Rs. 25,000 crore was given last year and another Rs. 25,000\ncrore has been allocated this year. It is expected that going further, provisioning\nrequirements will go down and profitability will improve.\n*******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |986", "t": "Shortage of Pilots", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE \nLOK  SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.986 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016 \n \n \nSHORTAGE OF PILOTS \n \n986. SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH: \n  \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there is shortage of pilots in the Indian Air Force (IAF); \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has taken steps to develop sufficient infrastru cture for \ntraining of pilots to fill the shortage gap; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e)  if not, the steps taken by the Government to meet the shortage ? \n \n                  \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                      (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h              ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n(a) & (b): The available strength of pilots in Air Force is sufficient to meet \nthe current operational requirements of the Indian Air Force (IAF).  The \nstrength of pilo ts as on 1 st July, 2016 is 3874 against the sanctioned \nstrength of 4062. \n \n(c) to (e): A comprehensive roadmap to increase training capacity for ab -\ninitio training in IAF is already in place.  Development of infrastructure for \ntraining purpose based on the requirement of Indian Air Force is                  \na continuous process. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |987", "t": "Changes in Certification to Films", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 987 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2016 \n \nCHANGES IN CERTIFICATION TO FILMS \n \n987: SHRI G. HARI \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Central Board of Film Certification (CBFC) proposes to \nprovide only certification to films and refrain from suggestion on cuts or \nedits that usually invite controversy, if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the producers would be allowed to seek a change in certification \nby resorting to cuts of their own accord; and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND \nBROADCASTING [COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)]: \n \n(a) to (c) : Central Board of Film Certification (CBFC) certifies films in accordance \nwith the Cinematograph Act, 1952, Cinematograph (Certification) Rules, 1983 and \nthe guidelines issued thereunder. In accordance with the existing provisions of the \nCinematograph (Certification) Rules, 1983 for the purpose of certification for public \nexhibition, every revised version or shorter version of a film shall be deemed to be \na fresh film and certified accordingly. Any decision of the CBFC not acceptable t o \nthe applicant can be challenged before the Film Certification Appellate Tribunal \n(FCAT) which is currently headed by a retired judge of the High Court. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |988", "t": "Action on Doctors", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 988\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nACTION ON DOCTORS\n988. SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether a number of senior doctors in AIIMS, New Delhi have been put under suspension\naccount of serious mistakes committed by them\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with reasons therefor;\n(c) whether the Government is likely to withhold the seniority of doctors responsible for such\nserious misconduct; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) to (d): No. None of the faculty member(s) in AIIMS, New Delhi are presently under\nsuspension."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |989", "t": "Committee on Bad Loans", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUSTION NO. 989\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016 / ASHADHA 31, 1938 (SAKA)\nCOMMITTEE ON BAD LOANS\nQUESTION\n989. SHRIMATI BUTTA RENUKA:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has constituted a committee to look into the issues of bad loans\nin the wake of recent instances of bankruptcy by the debtors, if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the said committee has submitted its report on bad loans;\n(c) if so, the details of main recommendations of the said committee and the action taken by\nthe Government thereon;\n(d) if not, the time by which the said committee is likely to submit its report; and\n(e) the details of the measures being taken by the Government to curtail bad debts and save\nthe exchequer from bad loans?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE\n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR)\n(a) No, Sir.\n(b) to (d) Does not arise.\n(e) The government has taken specific measures to address issues in sectors such as\nInfrastructure (Power, Roads etc.), Steel and Textiles, where incidence of NPAs is high. The\ngovernment has also approved establishment of six (6) new Debt Recovery Tribunals\n(DRTs), to speed up the recovery of bad loans of the banking sector, in addition to existing\nthirty three. Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has also undertaken steps which include (i)\nFormation of Joint Lenders’ Forum (JLF) for revitalizing stressed assets in the system, (ii)\nFlexible Structuring for long term project loans to Infrastructure and Core industries, and (iii)\nStrategic Debt Restructuring (SDR) scheme (iv) Scheme for Sustainable Structuring of\nStressed Assets (S4A).The Government has recently issued advisory to banks to take action\nagainst guarantors in event of default by borrower under relevant sections of SARFAESI Act,\nIndian Contract Act & RDDB&FI Act, since in the event of default; the liability of the guarantor\nis co-extensive with the borrower.\n******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |99", "t": "Promotion of Steel Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 99 \nANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 22ND JULY, 2016 \n[ASHADHA 31, 1938 (SAKA)] \n \nCORPORATE FRAUDS \n \nQUESTION \n \n*99. SHRI LAXMAN GILUWA: \n         SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS                  dkjiksjsV dk;Z \nea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Market Research and Analysis Unit (MRAU) has been set \nup in the Serious Fraud Investigation Office (SFIO) with the \nobjective of revamping corporate sector regulatory system; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the cases of serious frauds \nunearthed by this Unit; \n \n(c) the action taken by the Government so far in these cases; \n \n(d) the number of persons against whom allegations have been proved \nand the details of the action taken against the accused; and \n \n(e) the other measures taken/ being taken by the Government to \ncheck recurrence of serious corporate frauds? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS      (SHRI ARUN JAITLEY) \n \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 99 FOR ANSWER IN LOK SABHA ON 22-07-2016 \n(a) to (d) Market Research and Analysis Unit (MRAU) was set up with \nthe following objectives: \n(i) Repository of information; \n(ii) Improving investigation skills; \n(iii) Adoption of best practices; \n(iv) Co-ordination with other investigative agencies. \nMRAU prepares research reports based on information received \nfrom various resources. MRAU has submitted 5 reports covering 196 \ncompanies during 2015 -16, to the Ministry of Corporate Affairs. \nInvestigation has been ordered involving 16 companies. \n(e) Government has taken a number of measures to check recurrence \nof corporate frauds: \n(i) 'Fraud' as a substantive offence has been introduced in the \nCompanies Act, 2013. \n \n(ii) Statutory status to the Serious Fraud Investigation Office \nhas been granted under the said Act. \n \n(iii) Stricter norms of Corporate Governance a nd their \nimplementation under the Companies Act, 2013. \n \n(iv) Increasing application of technology for early detection of \nfrauds through data analysis, surveillance and usage of \nforensic tools, etc. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |990", "t": "NDB Loan", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Economic Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 990 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY July 22, 2016/Ashadha 31, 1938 (Saka) \n \nNDB Loan \n \n \nQuestion \n \n990. ADV. NARENDRA KESHAV SAWAIKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the New Development Bank (NDB) formed by BRICS group of emerging nations \nhave approved its first loan to its member nations, if so, the details thereof together with \nIndia’s share in the loan; \n \n(b) whether the Government has identified the projects where this loan is to be utilised; and \n \n(c) if so, the details of the projects and their locations? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE (SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n \n(a): The New Development Bank (NDB) on April 13, 2016  approved its first set of \nloans as under:   \n \ni) Sovereign loan of RMB 525 million (equivalent of USD 81 million) to the \nPeople’s Republic of China for Lingang Distributed Solar Power Project \nimplemented by Shanghai Lingang Hongbo New energy Development Co. Ltd ; \n \nii) Loan of USD 180 million with sovereign guarantee from the Republic of South \nAfrica to Eskom Holdings State -Owned-Company Limited for integrating renewable \n\nenergy independent power producers (RE -IPP) projects and augmenting \ntransmission capacity for further development of Soweto Area; \n \niii) Loan of USD 250 million with sovereign guarantee from the Government of \nIndia to Canara Bank for Renewable Energy Financing Scheme; and \n \niv) Two-step loan of USD 300 million without sovereign guarantee to Banco \nNacional de Desenvolvimento Econômico e Social (BNDES) for financing of \nrenewable energy and associated transmission projects in Brazil. \n \n(b) & (c): The loan of USD 250 million is for on-lending to renewable energy projects \nunder Renewable Energy Financing S cheme (wind, solar, small hydropower, \nbiomass, geo-thermal, waste to energy and municipal waste to electricity, etc.). The \nloan is developed as Multi-tranche Financing Facility with three tranches of USD 75 \nmillion (available from 2016), followed by second  tranche of USD 75 million \n(available till 31 December 2019 ) and third tranche loan of USD 100 million  \n(available till 31 December 2022) . Canara Bank has identified few maiden projects \nfor financing with this loan. The details are given below: \nS. \nNo. \nName of the \nCompany \nLocation Capacity \n1 M/s Mysore \nMercantile Co \nLtd \nGojnaur, \nGadag, \nKarnataka \n6 MW Wind \npower \ngeneration \n2 M/s Welspun \nEnergy \nRajasthan \nPrivate Ltd \nPratapgarh, \nRajasthan \n200.80 MW \nWind power \ngeneration \n3 M/s Nupower \nRenewables \nLtd. \nSangli \nMaharashtra \n49 MW Wind \npower \ngeneration \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |991", "t": "Loan to Women Drivers through Rashtriya Mahila Kosh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 991 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2016  \n  \n \nLOAN TO WOMEN DRIVERS THROUGH RASHTRIYA MAHILA KOSH \n \n \n991. DR.A.SAMPATH \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \na) whether the Government has any proposal to give loan through Rashtriya Mahila Kosh to women \ndrivers to run e-rickshaws to ensure safety in the public transport facility; and \nb) if so, the details thereof and the progress made in this direction so far? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI MANEKA SANJAY GANDHI) \n \n(a) & (b): Rashtriya Mahila Kosh (RMK) provides micro finance to women beneficiaries mainly for income \ngenerating activit ies in the informal sector through Intermediary Organisations (IMO), which includes \nSection 8 Companies, Women Development Organisations (WDO), Non -Governmental Organisations \n(NGO) etc. An individual beneficiary, who is a member of any one of the group viz . Self Help Groups \n(SHG), Joint Liability Groups (JLG), Grameen Model -JLG, Mahila Mandals and other types of group \npromoted by Central / State Government or any National / State Missions & Schemes, can also avail loan \ndirectly from RMK. \nSince e-rickshaw would fall in the category of income generating activities, the applicant shall be \neligible for loan from RMK as per scheme guidelines {i.e subject to maximum ceiling per NGO (i.e Rs 2 cr \nfor 1 state or Rs 6 cr for 3 states) and Rs 35,000/- & Rs 50,000/- per beneficiary for new & repeat borrower, \nrespectively)}.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |992", "t": "Non-Invasive method for Cancer Treatment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 992\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nNON-INVASIVE METHOD FOR CANCER TREATMENT\n992. SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether non-invasive method has been developed to kill cancer cells in two hours, if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(b) whether it is a cost effective method as compared to the methods w hich are currently in\npractice for the cancer treatment and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether this cure is available in the country, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons\ntherefor and by when it will be available in India?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) to (c): As reported by Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR), it is aware of the\nfindings of research undertaken and reported in Journal of Clinical Onc ology in which\nphotodynamic cancer therapy (non -invasive) is used to kill cancer cells in triple negative breast\ncancer patients. However, this modality is still in the experimental stages and further studies are\nrequired before it can be applied clinically."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |993", "t": "Essential Medicines", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 993\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nESSENTIAL MEDICINES\n993. SHRI ALOK SANJAR:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of medicines listed as essential medicines and the efforts made by the\nGovernment to ensure their adequate   availability to the common man of the country;\n(b) whether the World Health Organisation (WHO) has given any guidelines in this regard, if so,\nthe details thereof and the steps taken by the Government for compliance of the same;\n(c) whether the WHO had advised that the doctors in the country should prescribe only certain\nmedicines from the list of essential medicines; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a): 376 medicines have been listed in the National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM),\n2015. With a view to ensuring availability of essential medicines at reasonable prices, the\nGovernment has formulated the National Pharmaceuticals Pricing Policy (NPPP) and put in\nplace a regulatory framework for pricing of drugs. Under t his policy, the availability of essential\nmedicines at reasonable prices is ensured while at the same time also providing sufficient\nopportunity for innovation and competition.\n(b) to (d): WHO brings out a Model List of Essential Medicines from time to time which is\nnot country specific. The Government of India also brings out its National List of Essential\nMedicines (NLEM) from time to time keeping in view the disease pattern of the country , latest\nscientific evidence on the efficacy, safety and cost effectiveness of medicines and the standard\ntreatment guidelines. NLEM 2015 was notified on 23.12.2015. The standard treatment guidelines\nunder the Clinical Establishments Act, 2010 and differen t National Health Programmes provide\nguidance for prescribing medicines."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |994", "t": "Ambulance Services in Rural Area", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 994\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nAMBULANCE SERVICES IN RURAL AREA\n994. SHRI HARI MANJHI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken note that non -availability of ambulance services resulting\nin large number of deaths in rural areas;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the number of deaths repor ted during the last three years,\nState/UT-wise;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to provide funds for hightech ambulances for emergency\nlife saving assistance in case of emergency; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) &(b): No such data is maintained centrally.\n(c) & (d) : Public health being a State subject, the primary responsibility to strengthen\nhealthcare system lies with the State Governments. However, under the National Health Mission\n(NHM), technical and financial support is provided to States/UTs to strengthen their healthcare\nsystems including support for emergency transport services for patients, including Advance Life\nSupport (ALS) ambulances, based on the proposals made by the States/UTs in their Programme\nImplementation Plans.\nState/UT-wise details of operationa l Dial 108, Dial 102/Dial 104 ambulances are\nprovided as Annexure.\n………….\n\nAnnexure\nAmbulances Operational under NHM (as on 31-03-2016)\nSl.no. State/UTs Dial 108 Dial 102/104\n1 Bihar 49 742\n2 Chhattisgarh 239 300\n3 Himachal Pradesh 195 125\n4 Jammu & Kashmir 0 331\n5 Jharkhand 0 0\n6 Madhya Pradesh 606 0\n7 Orissa 420 455\n8 Rajasthan 741 600\n9 Uttar Pradesh 988 1964\n10 Uttarakhand 139 109\n11 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0\n12 Assam 380 316\n13 Manipur 0 43\n14 Meghalaya 42 0\n15 Mizoram 0 60\n16 Nagaland 0 75\n17 Sikkim 0 0\n18 Tripura 0 0\n19 Andhra Pradesh 439 0\n20 Goa 38 0\n21 Gujarat 406 0\n22 Haryana 0 375\n23 Karnataka 711 0\n24 Kerala 50 0\n25 Maharashtra 937 2674\n26 Punjab 242 0\n27 Tamil Nadu 704 0\n28 Telangana 316 0\n29 West Bengal 0 0\n30 A&N Island 0 1\n31 Chandigarh 6 9\n32 D&N Haveli 0 0\n33 Daman & Diu 3 4\n34 Delhi 0 120\n35 Lakshadweep 0 0\n36 Puducherry 10 1\nAll India 7661 8304"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |996", "t": "Norms of Food Safety", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 996\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nNORMS OF FOOD SAFETY\n996. DR. SWAMI SAKSHIJI MAHARAJ:\nSHRI ANOOP MISHRA:\nSHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether certain food processing companies in the country are not complying with standards\nAct by Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) keeping with international\nstandards;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UTwise including Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh;\n(c) whether FSSAI has prepared a report in this regard and submitted its report to the\nGovernment for stringent action against   such companies, if so, the details thereof and the action\ntaken thereon; and\n(d) whether the Government is aware that certain imported items like chocolates, wafers and\nchips are being sold across the country beyond their expiry dates, if so, the details thereof along\nwith the steps taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) &(b): Standards for articles of food prescribed under the Food Safety (FSS) Act, 2006\nand regulations thereunder, are  primarily enforced by the Food Safety Departments of the\nStates/Union Territories. Random sampling and testing of food products, etc. is undertaken by\nofficials of Food Safety Departments of the respective States/UTs to check compliance of\nstandards laid down under the FSS  Act, 2006 and Regulations thereun der. In cases, where the\nfood samples are found to be non -conforming to the prescribed standards, recourse is taken to\npenal provisions under Chapter IX of the FSS Act, 2006. As per the information made available\nby State/UT Governments to the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI), the\ndetails of samples collected, tested, found not conforming and action taken during the year 2014 -\n15 are at Annexure.\n(c): No such report has been submitted by the FSSAI to the Ministry of Health and Family\nWelfare. However, action including penal action as per provisions of FSS Act and Regulations\nthereunder is taken by the State authorities concer